window.__ARXIV_DATA__ = {"papers":[{"id":"2610.40653v1","title":"Micro-Edits: Computational Support for Fine-Scale Edits in Film","abstract":"In film editing, every frame matters. While macro, content-level edits shape the high-level narrative of a film, small local edits often control timing and direct visual attention. For example, editors can adjust the precise timing of clips to control the length of a meaningful silence, or cut to shots that highlight a specific character's reactions to important moments. These small, detailed edits are slow and tedious to make in standard editing tools because users have to map high-level narrative goals to low-level frame-by-frame adjustments. At the same time, these fine-scale adjustments, which we call micro-edits, can be nuanced and expressive, with different choices serving different creative goals. In this work, we start by conducting a formative study with professional editors to understand the structure of micro-edits and how they are used. We then derive a mathematical formalization for this structure to develop tools that leverage AI and optimization to make exploring micro-edits simpler and more efficient for users. Finally, we validate our tools in multiple user studies and a wide range of video examples.","authors":["Nhan Tran","Hijung Valentina Shin","Abe Davis","Mackenzie Leake"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:59:49Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:59:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40653v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40653v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40653v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":103501,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：In film editing, every frame matters. While macro, content-level edits shape the high-level narrative of a film, small local edits often control timing and direct visual attention. For example, editors can adjust the precise timing of clips to control the len...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:53+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40643v1","title":"Luggage re-identification: A study of strategies towards a real-world application","abstract":"Luggage re-identification (ReID) is a critical task in video surveillance for forensics, security or border management (suspicious or abandoned luggage recognition), as well as luggage management (lost luggage recovery). It enables the association of observations of the same luggage across disjoint camera views in complex environments such as airports, train stations and border crossing. While existing approaches primarily focus on appearance-based cropped image matching under relatively clean conditions, real-world scenarios are characterized by frequent occlusions, cluttered background and crowded scenes which significantly degrade performance. Yet, strategies leveraging the complementarity of multiple shots and views from different video streams is overlooked. In this paper, we study strategies for an end-to-end pipeline that operates from multiple video streams to detect and re-identify luggage. We evaluate different variants on a benchmark dataset acquired in real-world environments to address the questions of selecting and merging different views and shots of the same luggage as well as training on different environment datasets for an overall ReID performance improvement in practical applications.","authors":["Quentin Dhôte","Romaric Audigier"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:59Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40643v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40643v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40643v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":43141,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Luggage re-identification (ReID) is a critical task in video surveillance for forensics, security or border management (suspicious or abandoned luggage recognition), as well as luggage management (lost luggage recovery). It enables the association of observat...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:54+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40640v1","title":"SonoWorld4D: From One Video to a 4D Audio-Visual Scene via Diffusion Synchronization","abstract":"Recent progress in visual scene generation has extended beyond static 3D scenes toward the more challenging problem of generating dynamic 4D scenes from minimal input. However, sound, despite being intrinsically coupled with dynamic objects and events, remains largely unmodeled in prior work. We introduce Video-to-4DAVScene, a new task that turns a video into an explorable, time-varying 4D audio-visual scene, and present the SonoWorld4D framework for this task. Given a monocular video, our method generates a 360 static background, reconstructs explicit motion trajectories of dynamic, sound-emitting foreground objects, and synthesizes a physically-grounded sound field consistent across multiple sources through dual-space audio diffusion synchronization. The resulting 4D scene supports real-time novel-view visual and spatial audio rendering. Evaluation on real-world dynamic scenes and a user study demonstrates SonoWorld4D generates 4D audio-visual scenes with perceptually realistic, physically grounded spatial audio consistent with scene geometry and visual content.","authors":["Derong Jin","Xiyi Chen","Ming C. Lin","Ruohan Gao"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:45Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40640v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40640v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40640v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93729,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Recent progress in visual scene generation has extended beyond static 3D scenes toward the more challenging problem of generating dynamic 4D scenes from minimal input. However, sound, despite being intrinsically coupled with dynamic objects and events, remain...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:54+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40636v1","title":"Probing Spatial State Representations in Visual Foundation Models","abstract":"An observer sees only part of the surrounding world, yet must keep track of what lies beyond the current view. Spatial perception therefore involves more than recognizing individual images: it calls for a persistent spatial state that is updated under self-motion and used to guide action. Do visual foundation models provide representations that support such a state? We study this question through four levels of spatial capability---spatial structure, object persistence, predictive spatial update, and actionability---using frozen-feature probes on ten representations under streaming egocentric observations of static indoor environments. The resulting capability profiles vary across tasks, network layers, and prefix lengths: strong readout of the visible scene does not ensure accurate object persistence or future-feature retrieval. Matched controls separate readout accuracy from reliance on visual history and motion. Native future prediction retains recoverable spatial structure, but a spatial reader trained on real features does not transfer to predicted features unchanged. Finally, goal-dependent candidate selection does not establish reliable continuous action utility: predicted actions respond to goal changes, yet supplying the correct goal does not reliably improve progress. Across the four levels, object persistence and actionability build on camera-pose readout, whereas feature prediction builds on visible-scene readout. Together, these findings motivate evaluating spatial representations not only by what can be decoded from them, but by whether that information is maintained beyond observation, updated predictively, and used for goal-directed action.","authors":["Baiqiao Yin","Zhongang Cai","Ruisi Wang","Qiwei Liang","Lei Yang","Ziwei Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:16Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40636v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40636v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40636v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":97438,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：An observer sees only part of the surrounding world, yet must keep track of what lies beyond the current view. Spatial perception therefore involves more than recognizing individual images: it calls for a persistent spatial state that is updated under self-mo...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:54+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40632v1","title":"SymAnt: Grounding Long-Horizon Action Anticipation with Adaptable Symbolic Priors","abstract":"As robots and intelligent systems increasingly interact with humans, they must anticipate long sequences of future actions from only brief observations. While purely neural models typically require extensive observations to predict future actions and remain difficult to interpret and adjust, symbolic priors offer a structured alternative. To this end, we introduce SymAnt, a novel neuro-symbolic approach that unifies the expressiveness of neural networks with the structured reasoning of scene and knowledge graphs. Given a short video, SymAnt performs a joint-search over both graphs to construct a symbolic context, enabling unified reasoning over spatial relationships, object attributes, affordances, and associated actions. This symbolic context serves as a prior for an iterative refinement process that progressively generates and improves the sequence of future actions. By grounding predictions in symbolic context, SymAnt anticipates long-horizon actions from short observation windows in a parameter-efficient manner, while enabling users to directly edit the underlying knowledge to adjust predictions without further training. Through our experiments, we demonstrate the effectiveness of SymAnt, particularly when only short observation contexts are available, across five diverse datasets: Breakfast, 50 Salads, EPIC-KITCHENS, EGTEA Gaze+, and Assembly101.","authors":["Fnu Aryan","Yaqi Xie","Joseph Campbell","Woojun Kim","Kwonjoon Lee","Hossein Nourkhiz Mahjoub","Ehsan Moradi-Pari","Katia Sycara","Simon Stepputtis"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:00Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40632v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40632v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40632v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93062,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：As robots and intelligent systems increasingly interact with humans, they must anticipate long sequences of future actions from only brief observations. While purely neural models typically require extensive observations to predict future actions and remain d...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:55+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40616v1","title":"Difficulty Calibration Across Time for Membership Inference in Diffusion Models","abstract":"Modern diffusion models are trained on massive datasets that often include copyrighted or private images. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) address this by testing whether a given image was in a model's training set. The standard approach scores an image by its reconstruction loss and flags low-loss images as members. This often fails because some images are simply easier to reconstruct than others, so a simple unseen image can score a lower loss than a complex training image. Difficulty calibration separates memorization from how difficult an image is to begin with. We show how to calibrate along two axes of time: training time and diffusion time. First, across training time, we introduce Reference-Model Calibration (RMC), which compares a fine-tuned model's loss against an earlier checkpoint. This cleanly isolates newly memorized data and gives near-perfect auditing, even without text captions. Since earlier checkpoints are not always available, we next present Temporal Self-Calibration (TSC), which calibrates across diffusion time using only a single model. Our key observation is that an image's difficulty persists across noise levels, whereas memorization is localized in time. By comparing the loss at a target timestep against neighboring timesteps from the same model, TSC attenuates image difficulty using the model's own trajectory. Empirically, our methods beat the state of the art by a wide margin, and unlike most existing attacks, generalize across different model parameterizations such as $x$-, $v$-, and $ε$-prediction models. Code is available at https://github.com/MichaelJoseph777/TSC.","authors":["Michael Joseph","Yedid Hoshen"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:54:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:54:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40616v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40616v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40616v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":88164,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Modern diffusion models are trained on massive datasets that often include copyrighted or private images. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) address this by testing whether a given image was in a model's training set. The standard approach scores an image by...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40614v1","title":"L3G-World: Real-Time Infinite World Generation with Latent 3D Gaussian Memory","abstract":"Interactive world models generate visual content conditioned on user actions, yet maintaining spatial consistency over long-horizon exploration in real time remains challenging. Existing approaches rely on either temporal context or explicit 3D memory, both of which scale poorly because memory grows with the length of the generated stream. Moreover, explicit 3D memories condition latent video generators by re-encoding RGB renderings, which precludes real-time generation. In this paper, we introduce a Latent 3D Gaussian Memory (L3G) that combines the efficiency of feed-forward reconstruction with a compact, rapidly renderable scene representation. Building on this memory, we propose L3G-World, in which a memory updater and a video diffusion transformer (DiT) alternate autoregressively to generate spatially consistent worlds. The memory updater spawns Gaussians in newly observed regions and refines existing ones, so that the memory grows with scene extent rather than stream length. Each Gaussian further stores a VAE latent feature, allowing the memory to be rendered directly into the DiT's latent space without VAE re-encoding. Experiments show that L3G-World achieves state-of-the-art generation quality and long-horizon spatial consistency, with up to $38.6\\times$ fewer 3D primitives, $3.2\\times$ faster memory updates, and $359\\times$ faster memory rendering relative to the most efficient prior 3D-memory baseline for each metric, enabling real-time world generation at 9 FPS.","authors":["Jinhyeok Choi","JoungBin Lee","Jongmin Lee","Minkyeong Jeon","Jiyoung Kim","Kaeun Han","Yoonjin Yoon","Seungryong Kim"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:54:42Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:54:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40614v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40614v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40614v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":97455,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Interactive world models generate visual content conditioned on user actions, yet maintaining spatial consistency over long-horizon exploration in real time remains challenging. Existing approaches rely on either temporal context or explicit 3D memory, both o...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40607v1","title":"Fight World Model: Customizable Combat through Visual Interaction Proxies","abstract":"A playable, programmable game world must maintain persistent state, enforce executable rules, and support extensible gameplay mechanics. Existing video world models can generate plausible combat, yet struggle to keep hit reactions and health updates consistent with game rules. We introduce Fight World Model (FWM), which separates rule-governed combat execution from video rendering. An executable engine maintains state and resolves interactions. A deterministic compiler encodes these outcomes and attempted actions in a spatially and temporally aligned interaction proxy. The proxy aligns action and skill windows, brief hit reactions, and persistent health by fighter identity and timing. These cues can be controlled separately to render interactions under modified rules. Identity-bound references preserve character appearance across side switches. We train FWM on FightWorld Corpus, spanning 52 skill effects, 187 character-appearance variants, and 15 arenas. Without retraining the renderer, FWM supports damage scaling, limited-hit immunity, lifesteal, and multi-stage skill mechanisms beyond the native game. It also allows character and arena customization. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of FWM, which outperforms existing game world models in action fidelity, hit reactions, health-state consistency, and character appearance preservation. Our project is available at https://fight-world-model.github.io.","authors":["Jiahua Li","Zixu John","Tom Zhong","Fuping Wu","Tianhao Xu","Ruicheng Zhang","Jun Zhou","Zunnan Xu"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:53:02Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:53:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40607v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40607v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40607v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":60417,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：A playable, programmable game world must maintain persistent state, enforce executable rules, and support extensible gameplay mechanics. Existing video world models can generate plausible combat, yet struggle to keep hit reactions and health updates consisten...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40599v1","title":"HairDeck: Animation-Ready Stylized Hair Cards from a Single Image","abstract":"The hair of stylized characters in games and avatars is built from hair cards: artist-placed ribbons whose geometry is the visible hair, animated by spring bones. Existing image-based methods produce realistic hair or a single fused mesh, while CHARM, the only generator of stylized hair cards, decodes a hairstyle autoregressively as a long sequence of control points. Observing that a card hairstyle is an unordered set of artist-defined primitives, we propose HairDeck, which generates it as one: each card is a single token, and a set diffusion transformer denoises all cards together. From a single image, HairDeck generates the cards and the spring-bone chains that drive them, yielding an animatable asset that exports to ready-to-use downstream formats. On CHARM's own test set, it lowers Chamfer distance by 44% and card-orientation error by more than half while running 33 times faster, and from single images it outperforms CHARM, image-to-3D pipelines and strand-based methods. Project page: https://hyzcluster.github.io/HairDeck","authors":["Yuze He","Yixiang Wei","Wang Zhao","Chuhao Chen","Yanning Zhou","Yong-Jin Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:50:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:50:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40599v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40599v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40599v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":77653,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：The hair of stylized characters in games and avatars is built from hair cards: artist-placed ribbons whose geometry is the visible hair, animated by spring bones. Existing image-based methods produce realistic hair or a single fused mesh, while CHARM, the onl...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40578v1","title":"VeriCUA: Verification as Agentic Exploration for Computer-Using Agents","abstract":"Building and scaling computer-using agents (CUAs) across desktop, mobile, and web environments requires reliable task-completion verification for data curation, evaluation, and reinforcement learning. Typical automated approaches prompt vision-language models (VLMs) to judge trajectories or generate programs to inspect application state. However, both rely on partial views of complex digital environments. VLM judges are restricted to supplied observations, while generated programs depend on accessible state. These views can omit decisive evidence or leave completion judgments unsupported. Tailoring separate verifiers to different platforms and access settings adds further engineering cost. We present VeriCUA, a platform-agnostic framework for verification through agentic exploration. It treats verification as dynamic evidence gathering and assessment within a shared procedure for online and offline settings across platforms and observation modalities. After inspecting the available context to construct a task rubric, the verifier selects GUI or code checks for unresolved requirements and combines their findings under explicit evidence rules. Experiments show improved agreement with reference labels over the compared baselines across these settings, with the largest offline gains on hard cases and stronger rejection of false completion claims online. Further analyses connect these gains to complementary evidence and test-time exploration.","authors":["Qiushi Sun","Da Yu","Alexander Knop","Amer Sinha","Lingpeng Kong","Ben Kao","Ravi Kumar","Pritish Kamath"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:45:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:45:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40578v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40578v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40578v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":70955,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Building and scaling computer-using agents (CUAs) across desktop, mobile, and web environments requires reliable task-completion verification for data curation, evaluation, and reinforcement learning. Typical automated approaches prompt vision-language models...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40530v1","title":"PhysCount: Do MLLMs Count Visual Appearances or Physical Entities?","abstract":"Images reveal appearances, but counting often asks about physical objects. A single entity may be visible directly, in several reflections, or through glass, so detecting every visible occurrence does not determine how many entities are present. Existing visual counting benchmarks rarely separate these two operations. We introduce PhysCount, a benchmark for evaluating whether multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can distinguish what appears in an image from what physically exists in the scene. PhysCount contains 546 real-world images and 4,595 human-written questions organized into 651 target-specific groups. For each group, annotators locate the relevant appearances, label how each is observed, and identify appearances of the same entity. Four complementary question families test the enumeration of appearances, the enumeration of physical entities, image-formation-specific counting, and appearance--entity correspondence. We measure both accuracy on each family and the proportion of groups answered entirely correctly. Across 40 MLLMs, counting visible appearances is substantially easier than recovering the identities that connect them. Even the strongest model answers every question correctly in only 13.36\\% of groups. Performance declines when one entity produces many appearances or when its appearances cross observation modes. Higher image resolution improves access to visual evidence but brings limited gains in complete-group accuracy, whereas answering related questions jointly produces a marked improvement. These results expose a gap between recognizing what is visible and correctly recovering the physical entities behind those observations.","authors":["Lidong Lu","Jiaxin Lv","Liangrui Dong","Xinyi Mao","Lidong Zou","Zhenquan Li","Tong Lu","Guo Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:33:34Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:33:34Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40530v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40530v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40530v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":128776,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Images reveal appearances, but counting often asks about physical objects. A single entity may be visible directly, in several reflections, or through glass, so detecting every visible occurrence does not determine how many entities are present. Existing visu...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40525v1","title":"CrossWeave: Cross-Modal Scene and Instance Retrieval from Sparse 2D-3D Alignment","abstract":"Cross-modal scene and instance retrieval aims to retrieve a corresponding 3D environment or object given query 2D images, serving as a foundational mechanism for spatial reasoning in robotics and AR/VR. Existing methods achieve this by learning alignment only at the level of whole scenes or objects, which provides no supervision for the fine-grained local structure within them. In this work, we show that supervision on a set of sparse 2D-3D correspondences, without scene- or instance-level contrastive objectives, is sufficient to train a single model for both scene- and instance-level retrieval. Specifically, we propose $\\textbf{CrossWeave}$, a novel framework for cross-modal retrieval that learns sparse patch-point alignment, which alone outperforms existing methods. An optimal transport-based aggregator over the aligned features gives further gains by clustering structurally redundant regions, freeing descriptor capacity for semantically distinct local features. CrossWeave achieves state-of-the-art performance on both scene- and instance-level retrieval on ScanNet and 3RScan with a $\\textit{single model}$, whereas existing methods require separate models for each task. Our results suggest that whole-scene encoding is unnecessary for cross-modal retrieval and that sparse local correspondence supervision is a more effective and flexible alternative.","authors":["Aadith Warrier","Gnana Prakash Punnavajhala","Siddharth Tourani","Muhammad Haris Khan","Avinash Sharma","Madhava Krishna"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:32:46Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:32:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40525v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40525v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40525v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","3d","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":64753,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, 3d, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Cross-modal scene and instance retrieval aims to retrieve a corresponding 3D environment or object given query 2D images, serving as a foundational mechanism for spatial reasoning in robotics and AR/VR. Existing methods achieve this by learning alignment only...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40495v1","title":"LucidLyra: Feed-Forward Generative 3D Scene Reconstruction and Joint Understanding via Multi-Task Distillation","abstract":"We present LucidLyra, a framework that makes generatively reconstructed 3D scenes directly queryable for semantics and instances in a feed-forward manner from video latents. While generative 3D reconstruction can synthesize virtual environments from a single image, downstream applications in robotics and physical AI require not only visual realism but also access to scene semantics and object identities. Existing 3D understanding approaches, however, require captured multi-view scenes, have limited generalization capabilities, or rely on expensive per-scene processing and optimization. To bridge this gap, our method distills complementary knowledge from a camera-controlled video diffusion model and frozen 2D vision foundation models into a unified model to jointly infer 3D Gaussians encoding geometry, appearance, open-vocabulary semantics, and class-agnostic instance cues. The resulting scenes support novel-view rendering, open-vocabulary segmentation, and object grouping without per-scene optimization, while training requires neither manual 3D annotations nor captured multi-view data. Our method outperforms baselines combining state-of-the-art 3DGS segmentation approaches with generative 3D scene reconstruction on curated synthetic and real-image-conditioned benchmarks, while preserving reconstruction quality. Furthermore, our latent formulation naturally extends to monocular video, reconstructing structured dynamic 3D scenes across time. By turning renderable but unstructured outputs into queryable 3D worlds, LucidLyra enables downstream applications including 3D scene editing, 3D asset mining, and robotic simulation.","authors":["Oliver Hahn","Rodrigo Marcuzzi","Sangeetha Grama Srinivasan","Alessandro Burzio","Sherwin Bahmani","Jiahui Huang","Stefan Roth","Shengyu Huang","Qunjie Zhou","Laura Leal-Taixé"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:18:51Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:18:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40495v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40495v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40495v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","instance","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":113478,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, instance, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present LucidLyra, a framework that makes generatively reconstructed 3D scenes directly queryable for semantics and instances in a feed-forward manner from video latents. While generative 3D reconstruction can synthesize virtual environments from a single...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40480v1","title":"AVPS-360: A Spatial Audio-Visual Benchmark for Instance Segmentation and Tracking of Sound-Producing Persons","abstract":"Audio-visual instance segmentation (AVIS) segments and tracks individual sound-producing objects in videos. Existing benchmarks predominantly use monaural audio, which lacks inter-channel cues for spatially distinguishing concurrent sound sources. We introduce AVPS-360 (Audio-Visual Person Segmentation 360), a spatial audio-visual benchmark bridging AVIS and sound event localisation and detection (SELD). Built upon STARSS23, it pairs first-order Ambisonics audio with synchronised 360° video across 230 recordings totalling 10.5 hours. Recordings average 164.0 seconds. The dataset captures intermittent sound production in real-world interactions dominated by speech, alongside laughter, clapping, and footsteps. It provides 31,678 manually reviewed instance masks linked to source identities and sound-event labels. We also present a baseline that integrates spatial audio with visual features for frame-level segmentation and identity association across frames. Experiments on AVPS-360 show that existing AVIS methods struggle with detection and identity association in long recordings with intermittent sound production, while our baseline substantially improves performance in both aspects.","authors":["Yifu Sun","Parthasaarathy Sudarsanam","Tuomas Virtanen","Archontis Politis"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:12:04Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:12:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40480v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40480v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40480v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":28953,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Audio-visual instance segmentation (AVIS) segments and tracks individual sound-producing objects in videos. Existing benchmarks predominantly use monaural audio, which lacks inter-channel cues for spatially distinguishing concurrent sound sources. We introduc...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40478v1","title":"Accurate MRI-free PET Quantification and Reliability Assessment via CT-to-MRI Synthesis and Conformal Prediction","abstract":"Standardized uptake value ratio (SUVR) quantification of amyloid PET commonly relies on a subject-specific T1-weighted MRI, yet a usable MRI is not always available. We therefore combined CT-to-MRI synthesis with conformal prediction for MRI-free PET quantification with reliability assessment. A slice-consistent Brownian Bridge Diffusion Model was strengthened with multi-dataset training and data-source conditioning to enrich the anatomical prior learned from MRI, and with expanded multi-slice CT context to exploit the through-plane continuity of subject-specific anatomy in CT. Synthetic MRI was used for FreeSurfer-based anatomical parcellation and PET quantification, while deep-ensemble uncertainty was calibrated with conformal prediction to estimate a plausible range for the corresponding real MRI-based SUVR. On $^{11}$C-PiB PET from OASIS-3, the proposed method achieved the highest synthesis and parcellation fidelity among the compared methods. Mean cortical SUVR from the proposed synthetic MRI agreed closely with the real MRI-based reference (MAE $0.016 \\pm 0.015$, Pearson $r = 0.9987$) and yielded the same amyloid-positivity classification as the real MRI in all 87 test subjects at the prespecified cut-off. Conformal prediction achieved empirical coverage close to the prespecified levels, and at 90% target coverage, 85 of 87 subjects had prediction intervals that supported an unambiguous amyloid-positivity decision. Zero-shot evaluation on an independent external cohort showed that the proposed CT-to-MRI model could support PET quantification without retraining, although conformal calibration required target-cohort recalibration to recover the intended coverage. These findings support CT-to-MRI-based MRI-free PET quantification as a promising approach for providing accurate SUVR measurements and an explicit assessment of their reliability.","authors":["Jungwoo Seo","Seung Yun Choi","Kun Ho Lee","Seun Jeon","Byoung Seok Ye","Jiook Cha"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:11:19Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:11:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40478v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40478v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40478v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":142992,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Standardized uptake value ratio (SUVR) quantification of amyloid PET commonly relies on a subject-specific T1-weighted MRI, yet a usable MRI is not always available. We therefore combined CT-to-MRI synthesis with conformal prediction for MRI-free PET quantifi...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40467v1","title":"InfantNet: A Large Scale Dataset for Infant Body Pose and Shape Estimation","abstract":"Infant pose and shape estimation is essential for applications in childcare, developmental monitoring, and medical diagnosis. However, existing methods and datasets are largely designed for adults, and direct transfer to infants fails due to substantial differences in body proportions, articulation limits, and frequent self-occlusion. To address this gap, we introduce InfantNet, the largest real-image infant dataset to date, comprising 108,902 RGB images of infants aged 6-18 months. Each image is annotated with 2D keypoints, and a curated subset of 11,642 images additionally includes 3D pose and shape annotations with full SMIL parameters, verified through multi-view rendering and reprojection-error filtering. We use an iterative annotation pipeline to ensure high fidelity across both 2D and 3D labels. InfantNet establishes a large-scale, comprehensive benchmark for infant 2D keypoint detection and 3D pose-and-shape recovery. Baseline experiments demonstrate that state-of-the-art adult pose estimators do not generalize well to infants, whereas fine-tuning on InfantNet yields a consistent improvement. The gains are even more pronounced for 3D pose and shape estimation, where SMIL-based estimation demonstrates robust performance with accuracy comparable to results reported for SMPL-based models. By releasing the InfantNet dataset and benchmark, we provide a vital resource for advancing infant pose analysis and related healthcare applications.","authors":["Zhipeng Hui","Jiahao Wang","Xun Dong","Guofeng Zhang","Xingrui Wang","Jiawei Peng","Qihao Liu","Xiaoding Yuan","Yi Zhang","Junjie Oscar Yin","Wufei Ma","Qinfan Lyu","Sara N. Lupolt","Keeve Edward Nachman","Adam Kortylewski","Alan Yuille"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:06:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:06:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40467v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40467v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40467v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":73536,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Infant pose and shape estimation is essential for applications in childcare, developmental monitoring, and medical diagnosis. However, existing methods and datasets are largely designed for adults, and direct transfer to infants fails due to substantial diffe...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40455v1","title":"A Unified Benchmark for Aerial-Enhanced Autonomous Driving: The KITScenes Aerial Dataset","abstract":"Aerial imagery provides complementary spatial context for autonomous driving by exposing road structure beyond the immediate perception range of onboard sensors. Yet map construction from aerial imagery, aerial-assisted online HD mapping, and cross-view localization are typically studied on separate data and benchmarks. We introduce KITScenes Aerial, an extension of the KITScenes Multimodal dataset that augments synchronized onboard sensor data, vehicle poses, and rich Lanelet2 map annotations with spatially associated aerial imagery and higher-resolution neural representations, providing a common basis for a variety of research tasks. Our mapping evaluation focuses on all 53 KITScenes map classes across different model designs, spatial ranges, and aerial resolutions, while retaining compatibility with the widely used three-class setting. Representative baselines show that aerial context is particularly beneficial for fine-grained and extended-range mapping, more than doubling 53-class mAP at the larger mapping extent. Higher-resolution aerial imagery yields a further 4.3 mAP (+24 %) improvement, with gains extending to challenging map elements such as traffic lights. We additionally evaluate cross-view localization under geographically disjoint conditions, where an adapted fine-grained feature-matching baseline achieves a mean translation error of 5.13 m. KITScenes Aerial thus enables unified study of aerial and ground-level information across complementary mapping and localization tasks. Project page: https://kitscenes.com/aerial","authors":["Daniel Lengerer","Richard Schwarzkopf","Jonas Merkert","Fabian Immel","Rupert Polley","Frank Bieder","Jan-Hendrik Pauls","J. Marius Zöllner","Klaus Bogenberger","Christoph Stiller","Carsten Markgraf"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:01:30Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:01:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40455v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40455v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40455v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":56309,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Aerial imagery provides complementary spatial context for autonomous driving by exposing road structure beyond the immediate perception range of onboard sensors. Yet map construction from aerial imagery, aerial-assisted online HD mapping, and cross-view local...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40432v1","title":"Soil Moisture Estimation in Precision Agriculture: A Knowledge Distillation Approach","abstract":"Accurate soil moisture estimation is critical for precision agriculture, influencing irrigation, fertilization, and harvesting strategies. Traditional methods primarily rely on meteorological and soil property data, while recent approaches incorporate visual modalities such as aerial and geospatial imagery. However, these high-resolution images are expensive and computationally demanding to analyze. In this work, we propose Soil Moisture Distillation (SMD), a Knowledge Distillation (KD)-based multimodal learning framework that leverages unimodal teachers to enhance multimodal soil moisture estimation. SMD surpasses not only traditional multimodal fusion techniques, including MIS-ME, but also outperforms unimodal models, demonstrating the benefits of guided unimodal knowledge transfer. Additionally, we conduct a comparative analysis of soil patches and crop images, showing that crop images provide superior visual cues, leading to improved prediction accuracy. SMD achieves a 2% improvement in MAPE for soil patches and 1.2% for crops over state-of-the-art baselines, highlighting the effectiveness of leveraging both meteorological and visual data through knowledge distillation for enhanced soil moisture forecasting.","authors":["Mohammed Rakib","Vrinda Nagula","Arunkumar Bagavathi"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:53:56Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:53:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40432v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40432v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40432v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":1317,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Accurate soil moisture estimation is critical for precision agriculture, influencing irrigation, fertilization, and harvesting strategies. Traditional methods primarily rely on meteorological and soil property data, while recent approaches incorporate visual...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"abstract","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40401v1","title":"Continuous-Action World Models: Learning Cross-Embodiment Spatiotemporal Dynamics Priors","abstract":"We introduce Continuous-Action World Models (CoAWM), a framework for learning cross-embodiment spatiotemporal dynamics priors that support robot action generation across embodiments and execution frequencies. At its core is a shared latent function space that captures the spatial and temporal structure of robot actions. We learn this space through two stages. First, function pretraining brings trajectories from different embodiments and sampling frequencies into a common representation, learning shared dynamics priors through action reconstruction and consistency across temporal sampling views. By accommodating heterogeneous robot trajectories, this stage broadens the range of data sources available for pretraining and facilitates scaling to larger and more diverse datasets. Second, we connect the pretrained function space to a world-model backbone, enabling observations, language instructions, and the current robot state to condition the prediction of a latent action function. A single decoder takes any query time and returns the corresponding end-effector action. By separating shared dynamics priors from task-conditioned action selection and temporal sampling, our approach provides a unified foundation for transferring robot experience across embodiments and control frequencies.","authors":["Liangyu Li","Qingwen Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:34:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:34:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40401v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40401v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40401v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":48924,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We introduce Continuous-Action World Models (CoAWM), a framework for learning cross-embodiment spatiotemporal dynamics priors that support robot action generation across embodiments and execution frequencies. At its core is a shared latent function space that...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:58+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40373v1","title":"HiFOR: Hierarchical Foresight with Action-Guided World Grounding for Vision-Language-Action Model","abstract":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have demonstrated promising capabilities in robotic manipulation by integrating visual perception, language understanding, and action generation. Recent advances have further incorporated future prediction to enhance the understanding of environmental dynamics and task progression. However, existing methods often model short-term dynamics and long-term task objectives separately, while directly incorporating predicted futures into action policies without sufficiently considering their relevance to current action requirements. To address these limitations, we propose HiFOR, a hierarchical foresight framework with action-guided world retrieval. Specifically, Hierarchical Foresight Prediction jointly predicts short-term state evolution, semantic milestones, and final goal states through a Hierarchical Dependency Mask (HDM), establishing dependencies across temporal horizons. Building upon these predictions, Structure-Aware Latent World Modeling organizes future latent representations along the horizon, view, and spatial dimensions to facilitate fine-grained retrieval. Furthermore, Action-Guided Factorized World Retrieval adaptively retrieves relevant future information from the structured latent world based on evolving action representations during denoising, providing targeted guidance for action generation. Extensive experiments on two simulation benchmarks and six real-world robotic manipulation tasks demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach.","authors":["Rongxing Ding","Hongyu Qu","Kehong Wu","Xudong Wang","Fan Zhang","Xinguang Xiang","Xiangbo Shu"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:21:06Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:21:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40373v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40373v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40373v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":55936,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have demonstrated promising capabilities in robotic manipulation by integrating visual perception, language understanding, and action generation. Recent advances have further incorporated future prediction to enhance the un...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:58+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40339v1","title":"Wizard: Manipulate Any World in Real Time via Agentic Control","abstract":"Recent advances in video world models have enabled the generation of increasingly realistic and interactive virtual environments, opening new possibilities for player-customizable gameplay. Agentic frameworks further advance this direction by decoupling explicit game logic and controllable world representations from visual generation. However, existing approaches support only simple game settings and predefined motion patterns, generalize poorly to unseen environments and characters, and offer limited real-time interactivity. In this paper, we introduce Wizard, an agentic game world model that enables real-time, precise control over character motions while generalizing zero-shot across novel scenes, characters, and actions. To achieve this, we construct a diverse dataset spanning 750 scenes and 178 characters, incorporating skeleton and geometry information as structured representations. We leverage these representations as intermediate proxies to condition a video generation model, together with an adaptive masking mechanism that improves alignment between the conditioning signals and generated video rollouts. Furthermore, we introduce an agentic framework that explicitly maintains and updates game states, allowing players to freely configure their virtual worlds and exercise fine-grained control over character motions while preserving visual quality and gameplay consistency. By combining agentic control with structured proxy-based generation, our framework unlocks new possibilities for player-driven game creation with real-time, generalizable, and mechanically consistent world simulation.","authors":["Yuzhe Wu","Zijun Lin","Yeying Jin"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:03:19Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:03:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40339v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40339v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40339v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40333v1","title":"RFW-A: Agentic Procedural Generation of 400K Articulated Assets over 500 Categories","abstract":"High-quality articulated assets are essential for embodied perception, simulation, and manipulation, yet constructing them at scale remains difficult. Manual annotation and procedural modeling require substantial human effort. Recent agentic systems automate asset construction through program synthesis, but repeat costly reasoning and refinement for every new asset. Our key observation is that assets within a category can share construction logic despite variations in structure and appearance. Agentic synthesis and procedural generation are therefore complementary: agents can construct reusable programs, while procedural execution reuses the resulting logic across many samples. We therefore present RFW-A, which combines the two in a new articulated-asset generation paradigm. Starting from existing articulated programs, RFW-A organizes reusable components and their dependencies into category-level generators. Agents test and refine these generators across sampled configurations and motion states. Once constructed and validated, the generators produce diverse assets through procedural execution with no further model calls or token charges. The estimated API cost of generator authoring averages $10.62, amortizing to $0.05 per asset over 200 assets, versus $1.24 for the sampled per-asset agentic synthesis baseline. Using RFW-A, we built RFW-A-400K, containing over 400K sim-ready articulated assets across more than 500 categories. Experiments show high structural and kinematic validity and stable execution across physics simulators. Beyond asset validity, training with RFW-A-400K improves part-level articulation estimation on seen and unseen categories, while policies trained in simulation with retrieved and program-edited assets transfer zero-shot to real articulated objects.","authors":["Yuanbo Lyu","Changjian Pan","Saining Zhang","Yi Yang","Peishuo Li","Zhiheng Li","Jialu Liu","Junhao Chen","Jiawei Weng","Yupeng Gao","Weiqing Xiao","Wenyi Li","Jiarui Guo","Rui Zhang","Jingrun Du","Weiyi Lin","Zenan Meng","Hu Wei","Lin Qu","Zongzheng Zhang","Hao Zhao","Chaojian Li"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:58:15Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:58:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40333v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40333v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40333v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40327v1","title":"Radiology Guideline-derived Decision Graph for Efficient Clinical Decision Support","abstract":"Radiological risk stratification and management planning are essential downstream tasks in medical imaging. Automating these tasks is challenging because clinical guideline criteria are distributed across prose, tables, and cross-referenced definitions. We present Guideline-derived Decision Graph (GDGraph), a framework that decouples guideline compilation from case evaluation. During compilation, a large language model partitions guidelines into section-based chunks, extracts local decision subgraphs with linked definitions, and aggregates related branches into an executable decision graph. During inference, a fast System One model answers localized clinical questions while deterministic traversal resolves Boolean logic, turning opaque single-pass predictions into a traceable decision path without task-specific training. Across 347 cases spanning renal cyst, adnexal mass, and thyroid nodule guidelines, GDGraph improves System One accuracy from 57.3% to 90.8%, outperforming retrieval-augmented GPT-4o (80.1%) and graph-guided GPT-4o with chain-of-thought (82.7%) at substantially lower latency and cost. The source code will be available.","authors":["Xuewen Zhou","Zhen Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:56:27Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:56:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40327v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40327v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40327v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40309v1","title":"Textual Response-Aware Guidance for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) exhibit strong zero-shot generalization but remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks. Existing adversarial fine-tuning methods often use pre-trained model guidance to preserve image--text alignment while improving robustness. Our analysis of visual representation drift reveals complementary limitations of two representative forms of guidance. Matching visual features provides broad coverage by constraining all directions equally, but lacks semantic emphasis. Matching prediction distributions prioritizes semantically relevant directions, but remains limited by the candidate texts and prediction probabilities. These limitations can hinder preservation of pre-trained image--text relationships and degrade zero-shot performance. To address these issues, we propose Textual Response-Aware Guidance (TRAG), which constrains visual representation drift using the pre-trained text encoder's responses to local variations in input embeddings. Specifically, TRAG constructs anchor-level constraints from the response directions and magnitudes around semantic anchors, then aggregates them using pre-trained image--anchor relevance, yielding an image-adaptive constraint. Extensive experiments across architectures and tasks demonstrate state-of-the-art clean and robust performance. Further analysis shows that TRAG helps preserve pre-trained image--text relationships.","authors":["Yubo Cui","Xianchao Guan","Zheng Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:48:43Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:48:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40309v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40309v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40309v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40284v1","title":"LoRe-MIM: Lost-or-Replaced Masked Image Modeling for Self-Supervised Representation Learning in Whole-Body PET/CT","abstract":"Deep learning models for whole-body positron emission tomography/computed tomography (PET/CT) hold significant clinical potential for disease detection, treatment planning, and response assessment. However, the time and cost associated with expert annotations remain major bottlenecks in developing robust and generalizable models. Although self-supervised learning (SSL) has emerged as a promising approach to mitigating annotation scarcity, its application to whole-body PET/CT remains relatively underexplored due to the limited availability of large-scale datasets and the challenges associated with learning complementary cross-modal representations. To address these limitations, we curate a heterogeneous, multi-tracer, multi-institutional cohort comprising 6,531 whole-body PET/CT scans and propose LoRe-MIM (Lost-Or-Replaced Masked Image Modelling), a novel self-supervised framework designed to learn multimodal representations through masked image reconstruction. Specifically, LoRe-MIM introduces a masking strategy in which image regions are either replaced with zeros (Lost) or substituted with corresponding complementary imaging modality (Replaced), encouraging the model to capture both modality-specific characteristics and cross-modal relationships. We evaluate the learned representations across multiple downstream tasks, including multimodal PET/CT image reconstruction, patch-based classification into cancer vs. non-cancer, whole-body lesion segmentation, label-efficient training, and robustness assessment. Experimental results demonstrate that LoRe-MIM consistently outperforms the corresponding state-of-the-art baselines across these tasks, highlighting its potential as a scalable and generalizable self-supervised learning framework for whole-body PET/CT analysis.","authors":["Amartya Bhattacharya","Bashirul Azam Biswas","Biratal Raj Wagle","Michael S. Leapman","Matthew E. Maeder","James B. Yu","Indrani Bhattacharya"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:36:15Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:36:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40284v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40284v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40284v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40276v1","title":"TaskFrame: Connecting Task-Level Planning with Frame-Level Control for Game Agents","abstract":"Building general game agents requires both high-level task reasoning and low-latency action control in rapidly changing visual environments. These capabilities favor models with substantially different computational profiles, making them difficult to realize within a single model. In this work, we present TaskFrame, a hierarchical planner-controller framework that connects task-level planning with frame-level keyboard-and-mouse control. A vision-language task planner decomposes a high-level goal into an ordered sequence of atomic instructions and is optimized through supervised learning and dependency-aware GRPO. The hidden states of special goal tokens that terminate each atomic instruction provide continuous goal embeddings that are cached and reused across control steps. A causal Transformer controller combines the embedding of the active goal with recent observations to predict discrete keyboard and mouse-button actions and continuous camera motion at every game step. We train the system in three stages: planner training, controller pretraining, and joint alignment. Experiments in Minecraft show that TaskFrame is competitive with general-purpose vision-language models on instruction decomposition, and that in closed-loop evaluation the agent achieves a higher combat success rate than the evaluated baselines.","authors":["Guangzhi Wang","Junfu Pu","Yiran Wang","Xingyilang Yin","Honghao Chen","Cheng Cheng","Lingen Li","Yinan Zhou","Jiarui Wu","Yinuo Jiang","Hongbo Jin","Xiaodong Cun","Ying Shan"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:31:37Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:31:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40276v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40276v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40276v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40212v1","title":"Benchmarking color judgment in multimodal LLMs with CIE-computable ground truth","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are now used as zero-shot judges of image quality, but their color judgments are rarely tested with colorimetric ground truth. We present a five-task benchmark based on the CIE colorimetric framework: AWB pair judgment, skin-tone bias direction, CCT estimation, CIE Delta E 2000 pair ordering, and color naming under changing illuminants. We test eight MLLM endpoints, including four Qwen3-VL variants, GLM-4.5V, GPT-5.4, Claude-Opus-4.7, and Gemini-3.1-Pro. The models often solve coarse binary AWB judgments, but they remain weak on numeric CCT estimation and on some fine color-difference judgments. On ColorChecker-24 stimuli, the three closed-weight frontier models reach CCT MAE of 715-1056 K, compared with 1358-2133 K for the five open-weight models. A non-ColorChecker control shows that this advantage is partly template-dependent. The results suggest that current MLLMs have useful color words and coarse visual priors, but their color judgments are not reliably calibrated to CIE-computable color differences.","authors":["Shuwei Yue","Kaida Xiao","Zhihua Wang","Qiao Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:58:08Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:58:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40212v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40212v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40212v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40199v1","title":"Decision-Level Error Attribution and Depth-Aware Safety Evaluation of Monocular RGB Human Tracking for Autonomous Robot Navigation","abstract":"Monocular RGB tracking is useful for robots operating near people, but perception errors affect binary stop/proceed decisions in ways that geometric accuracy metrics do not capture. We examine how much decision error arises from the safety rule versus perception. We prove that lateral-only distance is a lower bound on planar distance: with accurate positions, it cannot miss dangers identified by the planar rule, but it produces unnecessary stops. We introduce a two-factor Shapley attribution separating rule and perception contributions, and further distinguish missed detections from localization errors. Experiments use ETH and UCY pedestrian videos with simulated stationary robots, disjoint calibration and evaluation pedestrians, and time-block bootstrap intervals. Across 898 pedestrians in three scenes, the lateral rule missed no dangers on annotated positions but achieved only 5.6-31.9% stop precision. In two scenes with RGB perception, calibrated ground-plane localization achieved 0.59 m and 0.51 m planar RMSE, compared with 1.72 m and 1.39 m for a geometric baseline and 0.81 m and 0.68 m for calibrated Depth Anything V2. Detection recall of 0.43 and 0.26 made missed detections the leading source of missed danger. A two-standard-deviation safety margin with ground-plane localization matched or improved on the lateral rule's missed-danger rate in all eight scene-threshold settings and reduced unnecessary stops in seven. All robot interactions are simulated, and outcomes are offline decision classifications, not demonstrations of collision avoidance.","authors":["Abdulahi Taiwo","Chijioke Cyriacus Ekechi"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:52:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:52:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40199v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40199v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40199v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40191v1","title":"Video Generation Meets Agent Harness: Agentic Video Generation, Agentic World Modeling and Recursive Self-Improvement","abstract":"Video generation is shifting from end-to-end synthesis toward agentic generation, where planning, tool use, and feedback enable coherent, controllable multi-shot videos. In parallel, video world models provide action-conditioned observations and predicted futures, serving as interactive environments for agents. We study their intersection through the agent harness: an executable runtime that constructs context, exposes tools, maintains state, routes feedback, and manages budgets around video models. This report examines three directions. First, agentic video generation, where harnesses coordinate scripting, shot planning, asset management, production, and editing, alongside the evaluation challenges of these systems. Second, agentic world modeling, where harnesses mediate observation, control, execution, and state. We discuss hybrid designs in which game engines or agent-written programs maintain world state while video models render observations, and how agents and world models can mutually evaluate and improve one another. Third, recursive self-improvement (RSI) in video model development, distinguishing output revisions, updates to harness state, generator parameters, and evaluators, and changes to the improvement procedure itself. We identify how these improvements feed back into subsequent development. Across these directions, we analyze essential agent capabilities, including audiovisual perception, long-horizon planning, memory, code-based interaction, feedback-guided control, skill reuse, and multi-agent coordination. We conclude with challenges in autonomous video production, lifelong memory, programmable hybrid control, autonomous model development, provenance, cost accounting, and evaluation linking video quality and task performance to sustained improvements in models and harnesses.","authors":["Liaoyuan Fan","Yuhao Zheng","Dongxing Mao","Hangyu Ran","Siyuan Hu","Weijia Mao","Rui Zhao","Weijia Wu","Guian Fang","Yanzhe Chen","Zeyu Zhu","Yuxin Jiang","Yiren Song","Yuchao Gu","Junchao Yi","Qi Hu","Dan Kong","Alex Jinpeng Wang","Kevin Qinghong Lin","Mike Zheng Shou","Wei Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:47:39Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:47:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40191v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40191v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40191v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40190v1","title":"AdMuse: Skill-Guided Creative Advertising Image Generation","abstract":"An outstanding creative advertising image should both attract attention and communicate a product's positioning. How to choose a persuasive conceptual bridge before rendering begins is challenging for advertising generation. We present AdMuse, a skill-guided multi-agent framework that distills high-quality advertisements into reusable creative skills for cross-domain advertising strategy. Rather than retrieving isolated exemplars, AdMuse abstracts recurring bridge patterns into a skill library that can guide new product briefs. At generation time, AdMuse coordinates skill routing, multi-candidate ideation and selection, and VLM-guided visual refinement: diverse skills propose alternative concepts, relative judgment selects the most promising direction, and vision-language feedback helps realize the selected intent in the final image. This design treats advertising creativity as a structured decision process rather than a single prompt-writing step. Experiments across diverse product and brand positionings show that AdMuse produces visually compelling advertisements that faithfully communicate product positioning.","authors":["Minyan Luo","Yuxin Zhang","Meng Zhao","Xincan Wang","Fuzhang Wu","Tong-Yee Lee","Oliver Deussen","Fan Tang","Weiming Dong"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:47:22Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:47:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40190v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40190v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40190v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40166v1","title":"EventSeeColor: Learning to Reconstruct Color from Monochrome Events","abstract":"Event cameras capture visual brightness changes under challenging lighting conditions. However, traditional event-based image reconstruction targets grayscale intensity and produces images without scene color. Color distinguishes surfaces with similar intensity. Reconstructing color from events can reveal appearance differences that reconstructed grayscale images cannot express. Although monochrome event cameras do not directly measure color, their event-triggering patterns depend on surface reflectance, illumination, and the sensor's spectral response. These patterns may contain cues that support color reconstruction. In this paper, we present EventSeeColor, which uses event-reconstructed grayscale and aligned events to jointly guide a diffusion colorizer. Grayscale-derived semantic context supports color inference, while region-balanced chroma supervision and local RGB refinement improve color distribution, brightness, and texture. In the combined evaluation on two real event datasets, EventSeeColor achieves the best structural similarity, learned perceptual scores, and chromatic-region color difference among the external methods.","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Langyi Chen","Haoxian Zhou","Zelin Zhang","Haodong Chen","Qiang Qu","Xiaoming Chen","Weidong Cai"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:38:48Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:38:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40166v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40166v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40166v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40153v1","title":"Hierarchical Step Decomposition for Training-free Online Video Step Grounding","abstract":"Given a set of procedural steps, Video Step Grounding (VSG) aims to identify which steps occur in a video and localize when they are performed. Existing methods are either offline, requiring access to the full video before making predictions, or online, but relying on fixed-length video segments producing noisy, over-segmented outputs. In this work, we propose Hierarchical Step Decomposition (HSD), a training-free framework for online VSG that leverages the semantic understanding capabilities of Large Multimodal Models. HSD addresses two central challenges in online grounding: constructing semantically coherent temporal segments from a streaming video, and bridging the semantic gap between coarse procedural step descriptions and visual observations. To construct elementary temporal units, HSD incrementally groups incoming segments into semantically consistent events, using an anchor caption that is continuously validated against the visual stream. Based on these events, we perform zero-shot procedural step classification and selectively refine uncertain predictions by decomposing candidate steps into finegrained substeps that capture more nuanced visual evidence. By operating on semantically coherent events rather than every fixed-length segment, HSD reduces redundant computation and produces more stable predictions. Experiments on HT-Step, Ego4D Goal-Step, and CrossTask show that HSD substantially outperforms state-of-the-art methods, overall achieving better step recognition, more accurate temporal localization, and faster inference.","authors":["Long Zhou","Luca Zanella","Paolo Rota","Massimiliano Mancini","Elisa Ricci"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:35:33Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:35:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40153v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40153v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40153v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40137v1","title":"NeurEO: dissecting Earth observation embeddings with computational neuroscience","abstract":"Earth embeddings are compressed representations of rich Earth observation data that enable downstream models to tackle a wide range of complex geospatial tasks. Yet, this increase in performance comes at the cost of decreased interpretability, as the semantic meaning of Earth embeddings is unknown. To address this challenge, we borrow methods from the field of computational neuroscience, which aim to interpret activity in (biological) neurons. Here, we adapt and apply these methods to assign meaning to AlphaEarth embeddings. We analyse embedding features both as stand-alone data, equivalent to spontaneous neural activity, and in relation to land cover and elevation correlates, equivalent to stimulus-response neural activity. We demonstrate correlational and multi-scale structure in the embedding features. Embedding features can display highly specific and directional tuning to land cover and elevation, such as a `North vs South woodland edge' response. When we remove the main trends of combined land cover responses (like water versus land tuning), we discover fine-grained representational details with increased effective dimensionality. Together, our analyses start to unravel what these Earth embeddings represent, which is critical for developing trustworthy downstream applications.","authors":["Thijs L van der Plas","Jacob JW Bakermans","Ioannis N Athanasiadis"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:28:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:28:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40137v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40137v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40137v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40131v1","title":"VideoMemBench: How Well Do MLLMs Remember in Video Conversations?","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can now process hour-long videos, making extended multi-turn dialogue over a single video a natural mode of use. Sustaining such a dialogue requires more than answering each question in isolation: the model must retrieve and reuse information established earlier in the conversation, both what it has seen and what it has said, an ability we term in-context video memory. Existing video QA benchmarks leave this ability unmeasured: their questions are mutually independent, never reference one another, and each is answerable from the video alone, so nothing ever requires carrying information across turns. We introduce VideoMemBench, in which each sample pairs a single video with a long multi-turn dialogue, whose turns are connected by typed cross-turn dependencies: an Episodic Dependency refers back to a visual episode described in an earlier turn, probing memory of what the model has seen, while an Answer Dependency builds on the model's own earlier answer, probing memory of what the model has said. Every dependency is verified considering two properties: 1) necessity, meaning the question cannot be answered without its earlier turns, and 2) non-leakage, meaning the question does not restate the information from its earlier turns. To enforce these properties, we design a construction pipeline with three LLM roles: a planner, a generator, and a judger, with each dialogue reviewed by human experts. VideoMemBench comprises 326 videos with 2,476 turns, of which 1,387 depend on earlier turns, including multi-parent dependencies and long-range call-back. Among over 20 evaluated open- and closed-source MLLMs, Claude Opus 5 performs the best with an overall score of only 45.7\\%, highlighting the challenge of in-context video memory. Our dataset and evaluation are released.","authors":["Zhengdong Hu","Chao Wang","Kaituo Feng","Huanjin Yao","Mingfei Lu","Hehe Fan","Yi Yang"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:22:51Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:22:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40131v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40131v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40131v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40124v1","title":"It Takes Two to Probe, Thousands to Retrieve: Putting Vision--Language Probes to the Retrieval Test","abstract":"Vision--language models (VLMs) are tested for fine-grained understanding with image--text matching probes, a model must prefer the correct image or caption over handcrafted negative. In retrieval, the correct item must beat thousands of alternatives. We measure how these two evaluation methods relate by using two probing benchmarks, \\svo{} and SugarCrepe, for retrieval tasks. For 100 image and caption queries we judge the top-10 retrievals from full dataset of six VLMs, with human annotators and an LLM assessor. We than compare how well models rank pool of items on the same queries, while changing only where the negatives comes from: the benchmark, a random draw, or the judged-incorrect retrievals of other models. On \\svo{} the accuracy of retrieving a positive over handcrafted negatives is significantly higher than for retrieved ones (the correct item is preferred in 85\\% against 77\\% of the time). Using pool of ten retrieved negatives leads to same accuracy as when one uses pool of 200 to 430 random negatives. On SugarCrepe the order is reversed: its handcrafted negatives are harder than the retrieved ones. Incomplete relevance labels explain most of the low retrieval scores on \\svo{}, where the top-ranked item is correct for 54--90\\% of the queries under human labels against 6--21\\% under the benchmark's labels. However, we detect no association between passing the probe for a query and retrieving a correct item first. The LLM assessor o3 agrees with individual annotators ($κ$ 0.57--0.61) slightly less than they agree with each other (0.64--0.68).","authors":["Ivana Beňová","Michal Gregor","Marián Šimko"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:20:31Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:20:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40124v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40124v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40124v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40121v1","title":"Learning a Motion Manifold to Constrain Ambiguity in Unsupervised 4D Medical Image Interpolation","abstract":"Unsupervised 4D medical image interpolation reconstructs intermediate anatomical states from temporally sparse observations without intermediate supervision. Distinct motion fields may attain comparable endpoint agreement while producing different intermediate anatomy, giving rise to motion ambiguity. This ambiguity is difficult to constrain when many independently adjustable motion variables must be estimated from the observed endpoints. We develop a framework that learns a motion manifold to restrict candidate motion fields while retaining sample-specific variation. Cross-Sample Spectral Basis Learning extracts recurring motion structure from endpoint-derived dense estimates and encodes it in a shared spectral basis. Sample-Conditioned Cayley Basis Reorientation uses predicted motion coordinates to rotate the effective motion directions for each endpoint pair without increasing the number of independent coordinates. Their composition defines a nonlinear family of complete velocity fields across endpoint pairs, accommodating anatomical and motion-state differences within a restricted representation. Integrating each predicted field supports bidirectional reconstruction at arbitrary intermediate times. Theoretical analysis establishes sufficient conditions under which endpoint observations distinguish nearby motions within the learned family. Experiments on ACDC and 4D-Lung demonstrate state-of-the-art performance among the evaluated methods trained without intermediate volumes and competitive results against methods using intermediate supervision.","authors":["Haojin Li","Huazhu Fu","Heng Li","Jiang Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:17:16Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:17:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40121v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40121v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40121v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40119v1","title":"SynthFaces: Balanced Large Scale Human Dataset","abstract":"The pursuit of fair and equitable AI in human-centric tasks is critically hindered by the inherent biases and privacy risks of real-world datasets, which offer imbalanced representations of identity and often contain sensitive personal information. Consequently, state-of-the-art generative models trained on this skewed data exhibit unfair performance, particularly in identity-preserving generation. In this work we propose a pipeline for controllable generation of dataset with diverse faces for generative models training and finetuning. We achieve this by expanding foundational Arc2Face diffusion model with a flow matching generative model in the manifold of identity embeddings. We demonstrate that such approach achieves both goals of generative fidelity and label diversity not present in real human-centric datasets. As a result we collect and further contextualize a highly balanced identity-centric dataset of humans named SynthFaces. By design, our released images and identities are synthetic, reducing direct reliance on real personal images; we empirically check identity separation with a nearest-neighbor non-leakage metric. We purpose it for training and tuning of fair models for various downstream tasks, and showcase its usability in finetuning an existing adapter-based method for personalized generation.","authors":["Sergey Karpukhin","Denis Bobkov","Kirill Morozov","Aibek Alanov"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:17:06Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:17:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40119v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40119v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40119v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40115v1","title":"SelfThinker: Self-Evolving Reasoning via Reliability-Verified Intrinsic Visual Thoughts","abstract":"Unified multimodal models can both generate and understand images within a single set of weights, offering an opportunity to use image synthesis as training supervision for visual comprehension. However, existing visual chain-of-thought methods rely on human-annotated helper images during training and require slow image generation at test time. We present SelfThinker, the first self-evolving framework that turns a unified model's self-generated visual thoughts into stronger understanding without human image annotations. Given a visual problem, SelfThinker samples candidate helper images from its generation pathway and uses its frozen understanding pathway to score each candidate via conditional pointwise mutual information. High-utility visual thoughts then refine the generator via flow matching and serve as privileged teacher context for on-policy self-distillation. At inference, SelfThinker reasons directly from the original image in text without generating intermediate images. Although trained only on small synthetic visual-search and navigation datasets, SelfThinker transfers broadly to natural-image and spatial benchmarks, raising the seven-benchmark average of ThinkMorph-7B from 52.7 to 64.0 (+11.3 points). Controlled image-perturbation probes further show that SelfThinker surpasses ThinkMorph-7B by up to 40.4 points and its pretrained BAGEL-7B backbone by up to 23.2 points across fine-grained and spatial perception tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/zhangquanchen/SelfThinker.","authors":["Zhangquan Chen","Ruqi Huang"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:14:16Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:14:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40115v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40115v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40115v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40094v1","title":"RepConTUR: A Report-Conditioned Volumetric Multimodal Model for Abdominal Tumor Segmentation","abstract":"Tumor delineation is required for radiotherapy planning, yet automatic segmentation is limited by errors, predominantly false positives, that distort dose and increase editing time. The radiology report accompanies the scan and localizes disease at the suborgan level, but existing text-promptable models condition on short referring phrases, and most operate in 2D. We present RepConTUR, a single native-3D early-fusion multimodal model that conditions volumetric abdominal tumor segmentation on the narrative report. We extend AbdomenAtlas 3.0 to a suborgan-level promptable segmentation dataset and generate report-based VQA pairs, yielding four report-derived tasks on the same annotations: referring segmentation (RES), report-grounded segmentation (GSEG), report generation (RRG), and visual question answering (VQA). We train RepConTUR, comprising a 3D CT encoder, an early-fusion language model, and a native-3D segmentation module, jointly on the four tasks. Across liver, kidney and pancreas, RepConTUR attains the highest Dice similarity coefficient (DSC), instance-wise precision, and detection F1 among the text-promptable baselines, and adding the report raises mean precision while leaving recall comparable. Against two vision-only specialists, nnU-Net and the report-supervised R-Super, RepConTUR attains the highest value on every in-domain metric for liver and pancreas and the highest mean DSC, recall, precision, and detection F1 over the four out-of-distribution public cohorts. Ablations indicate that conditioning on the narrative report raises detection F1 from 0.499 to 0.528 within the same model, that joint GSEG training raises RES F1 from 0.450 to 0.482, and that the native-3D segmentation module raises GSEG F1 from 0.139 to 0.528 over its 2D counterpart. Code is available at https://github.com/Oncosoft-Research/RepConTUR.","authors":["Seunghwan Lee","Sangjoon Park","Jongmin Lee","Yoon Jo Kim","Wonyoung Cho","Jin Sung Kim","Han Joo Chae"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:59:44Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:59:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40094v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40094v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40094v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","instance","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40085v1","title":"Morphology-Aware Channel-Decoupled Mamba Network for Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Accurate multi-organ segmentation in computed tomography (CT) is essential for computer-aided diagnosis, treatment planning, and disease monitoring. However, substantial variations in organ shape and size, together with low tissue contrast and ambiguous boundaries, complicate the modeling of heterogeneous anatomical structures. To address these challenges, we propose a Morphology-Aware Channel-Decoupled Mamba Network (MCDNet), which integrates adaptive local structural enhancement with channel-decoupled long-range modeling. Specifically, we introduce a Morphology-Aware Channel-Decoupled State-Space (MCSS) block that divides feature channels into separate processing groups, allowing differentiated structural representations to be learned within an efficient state-space framework. Within each group, a Morphology-Aware Enhancement Module (MAEM) combines deformable convolutions with channel attention to adapt local feature extraction to anatomical shape variations. Group-wise state-space modeling then captures long-range spatial dependencies, and channel gating adaptively integrates the resulting features. Experiments on the Synapse abdominal CT dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of MCDNet, achieving an average Dice Similarity Coefficient of 81.61% for multi-organ segmentation.","authors":["Chenchen Wang","You Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:55:02Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:55:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40085v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40085v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40085v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40073v1","title":"Uncertainty-aware Coarse-to-Fine Main Pancreatic Duct Segmentation for Width Measurement in 3D MRCP","abstract":"Assessment of the main pancreatic duct (MPD) from volumetric magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) scans is an essential task for ductal dilatation diagnosis and pancreatic cancer analysis. However, manual assessment is affected by reader variability in which automated analysis may assist. Nevertheless, fully automated and uncertainty-aware MPD analysis from volumetric MRCP remains underexplored area of research. In this study, we propose an automatic and uncertainty-aware deep learning-based pipeline for MPD segmentation and maximum-width analysis from MRCP scans. A coarse localisation model first identifies the ductal region-of-interest, followed by a fine model that segments the MPD within the region. The dataset consists of 256 MPD-segmented MRCP scans from 69 patients. A randomly selected holdout test subset of 14 patients with 55 MRCP volumes was used for evaluation. We employed a five-model cross-validation ensemble for improved performance and uncertainty estimation. The best MPD segmentation performance was 0.634 DSC, 0.744 precision, and 0.593 recall. The mean absolute error for maximum MPD width prediction was 0.355~mm and dilated MPD ($\\geq 5$~mm) was detected with 75.0\\% sensitivity and 74.4\\% specificity. These novel results suggest that automated MPD segmentation can be used to aid MRCP-based duct measurements.","authors":["Zipei Zhang","Jaakko Sahlsten","Mikko Saukkoriipi","Johanna Ronkainen","Mona Lehto","Akseli Aittoniemi","Irina Rinta-Kiikka","Kimmo Kaski"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:51:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:51:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40073v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40073v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40073v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40068v1","title":"BRACHIO: Broad Representations Across Centres for Head-and-neck Imaging Oncology (HECKTOR 2026)","abstract":"The HECKTOR 2026 challenge benchmarks machine learning for head and neck cancer, where teams delineate primary and nodal tumour volumes, perform TN staging, and predict recurrence-free survival (RFS). This report describes the submissions for the HECKTOR challenge of team nucli. As the three tasks span both dense and global information from multicentric PET/CT scans, we utilise self-supervised pre-trained encoders, training a 2D DINOv2 encoder (PET-DINOv2-MIPs) on HECKTOR 2026's PET scans. Our final test submission ensembles a DynUNet trained from scratch with a SwinUNETR whose Swin trunk was initialised from a SimMIM-pre-trained checkpoint for segmentation, reaching a mean Dice of 0.5409. For TN staging, GTVp/GTVn geometric features from the segmentation masks are fit to stage probabilities by logistic regression (LR) and fused with PET-DINOv2-MIPs deep features (PCA + LR) via a second-level LR, reaching balanced accuracies of 0.3962 (T-stage) and 0.6432 (N-stage). For the RFS prognosis task, we fine-tune the last two blocks of the PET-DINOv2-MIPs ViT-S encoder jointly with an MLP head under a CoxPH loss, concatenating clinical covariates with the imaging features, reaching a test Harrell's C-index of 0.6577 and a weighted challenge score of 0.5802. The prognosis result is comparable to the best entry of the previous edition and consistent with our internal cross-validation, while our segmentation result is well below it and limits the staging results that depend on it.","authors":["Roxanne De Maeyer","Ine Dirks","Yannick Brackenier","Simon DeKeyser","Jef Vandemeulebroucke","Vicky De Ridder"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:50:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:50:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40068v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40068v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40068v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40061v1","title":"Localizing Concepts in Visual Autoregressive Models","abstract":"Understanding the internal routing of visual concepts is crucial for the safe and controllable adaptation of generative models. While concept localization has been widely studied in diffusion models, the emerging paradigm of next-scale visual autoregressive models remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we introduce LoCo, the first model-agnostic framework to localize where and when specific concepts emerge within autoregressive models. Driven by the native coarse-to-fine nature of next-scale generation, our method precisely maps conceptual knowledge across three distinct dimensions: Layer, Scale, and Position. To systematically localize and evaluate concept routing without context bias, we propose LoCoBench, a comprehensive dataset spanning 10 diverse categories. Extensive probing on image and video autoregressive models like Infinity, HunyuanImage-3.0, and InfinityStar shows that the localized positions are both interpretable and causally related to concept emergence. Building on these insights, we apply our localization method to three key applications: concept erasure, model personalization, and adversarial concept injection. Experiments demonstrate that our targeted intervention achieves state-of-the-art performance, substantially reducing computational overhead while preserving benign utility. Overall, our findings offer insights into how conceptual knowledge is routed during autoregressive generation, introducing a practical pathway for more interpretable, efficient, and secure adaptation.","authors":["Nanxiang Jiang","Yang Liu","Yuanhao Wang","Min Xu"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:49:01Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:49:01Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40061v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40061v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40061v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40046v1","title":"EPC-RawDC: Equivariant Physics Constrained 3D Diffusion with Raw Data Consistency for CBCT to CT Image Synthesis","abstract":"Cone beam computed tomography (CBCT) is widely used for image guided and adaptive radiotherapy, but scatter, noise, reconstruction artifacts, and inaccurate Hounsfield units limit its quantitative use. Recent diffusion approaches for CBCT to CT synthesis remain largely image driven, with limited use of the underlying acquisition physics. We propose EPC-RawDC, a conditional 3D latent diffusion framework that combines volumetric generative modeling with two complementary constraints derived from the same scanner specific CBCT forward model. First, geometry equivariant projection constraint exploits the rotational equivariance of the CBCT forward operator by supervising the synthesized CT through correspondingly transformed reference projections. Second, raw data consistency uses noisy projection measurements of the individual acquisition as scan specific supervision during training. The framework was evaluated on two independent clinical cohorts with distinct scanner geometries and projection sources. For JUST, the physics constraints used measured clinical raw CBCT projections acquired with an Elekta system, whereas for SynthRAD2025 the projection data were simulated from the reference CT using a Varian geometry with Poisson photon statistics. EPC-RawDC achieved an SSIM of 0.97 in both cohorts and improved PSNR over DDPM by 2.66 dB on SynthRAD2025 and 2.10 dB on JUST, while reducing mean MAE by 20.3% and 19.0%, respectively. On SynthRAD2025, projection consistency and equivariant errors were reduced by 55.0% and 41.4% relative to DDPM. These results demonstrate that combining a volumetric diffusion prior with scanner geometry and scan specific projection measurements improves quantitative CBCT to CT synthesis across both simulated and measured projection domains. Code available after publication at https://github.com/ALZAHRAALTALIB/EPC-RawDC.","authors":["Alzahra Altalib","Chunhui Li","Alessandro Perelli"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:42:56Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:42:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40046v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40046v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40046v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40029v1","title":"Segment Anything Model Based Context Guided Mamba Unet for Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Medical image segmentation is crucial for clinical diagnosis and treatment planning, requiring both global contextual understanding and fine-grained structural precision. While the Segment Anything Model (SAM) demonstrates strong generalization capabilities, its direct application to medical images often lacks domain-specific adaptability. Meanwhile, State Space Models (SSMs)-based methods, such as Mamba, efficiently capture long-range dependencies but may struggle with preserving fine anatomical details. To address these challenges, we propose a segment anything model based context guided mamba unet, SAMBA-UNet, a novel framework that integrates SAM's context knowledge with Mamba's hierarchical feature learning to enhance both global representation and local detail refinement. Specifically, we introduce the Hierarchical Context Enhancement Module (HCEM), an attention-based fusion module that deeply incorporates the multi-level features of SAM and Mamba to improve detail perception and segmentation precision. Additionally, we employ LoRA and Adapter-based fine-tuning to adapt SAM to medical imaging tasks, further enhancing its domain-specific segmentation capability and robustness. We conducted a comprehensive study on several medical segmentation datasets, including Synapse, ISIC17, and ISIC18, evaluating our method across multiple metrics. Experimental results demonstrate that SAMBA-UNet achieves state-of-the-art performance, effectively balancing generalization, segmentation accuracy, and computational efficiency. Our code will be released upon acceptance.","authors":["Chenchen Wang","You Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:36:01Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:36:01Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40029v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40029v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40029v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40007v1","title":"Greenscapes: Optimized Vegetation Configurations in Cities","abstract":"Vegetation plays a central role in cities, serving functional roles such as balancing shade and sky exposure, shaping the microclimate, carbon sequestration, and providing acoustic screening. Furthermore, aesthetic choices in landscape styles (formal, modernist, picturesque) are important for citizens' well-being and should be considered in the design of urban landscapes. In this paper, we present a novel method for placing vegetation, ranging from shrubs to large trees, in 3D urban scenes. Our method uses individual lots as input and employs a conditioned Vision-Language Model (VLM) to generate a top-down rendering of vegetation, placing large trees, small trees, and shrubs on lawn areas. This rendering is then converted into a 3D scene by detecting individual plants and selecting appropriate 3D assets. To refine the proposed configurations, we define an optimization loop that keeps the VLM-generated positions fixed after a deterministic feasibility repair and uses CMA-ES to optimize per-instance scale transformations along with discrete vegetation asset substitutions. To define the objective function for the optimization, we evaluate the generated 3D scenes with efficient surrogate models of shade and path-based acoustic exposure. Furthermore, we render in-scene views at each optimization step to compute view-dependent perceptual descriptors, enabling the generation of both aesthetic and functional vegetation layouts for urban scenes.","authors":["Patryk Bartkowiak","Marcin Marlewski","Paweł Wójcik","Jae Joong Lee","Dominik L. Michels","Bedrich Benes","Sören Pirk","Wojciech Pałubicki"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:24:24Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:24:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40007v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40007v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40007v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39972v1","title":"Blind Video Denoising for Vocal Tract rtMRI using a Self-Supervised Ensemble","abstract":"Real-time magnetic resonance imaging of the vocal tract makes speech articulation directly observable, but the trade-off between temporal resolution and signal quality introduces substantial noise and obscures fine articulatory detail. Since clean references cannot be acquired for a moving vocal tract, supervised denoising approaches are not applicable in this setting. We propose a self-supervised denoising framework that combines spatiotemporal super-resolution learning with pseudo-supervision derived from a variance-stabilized non-local means filter. A dual-model fusion strategy balances noise suppression and preservation of high-frequency articulatory structure. Experiments on the USC-75 multi-speaker dataset under natural noise conditions show that the ensemble raises the signal-to-noise ratio by 11.9 dB on unseen subjects while attaining more high-frequency articulatory content in the frames. Among tuned implementations of anisotropic diffusion, block-matching filtering, non-local means and pre-smoothed non-local means, every configuration that achieves comparable noise suppression does so at the cost of high-frequency content or of structural fidelity. Under severe added Gaussian, Rayleigh and Rician noise, peak signal-to-noise ratio improves by 7.6 to 11.8 dB and structural similarity roughly triples. An ablation shows that the variance-stabilized pseudo-supervision is an essential element for denoising, and that the effect is attributable to the hard thresholding contained in the stabilization transform.","authors":["Julian Andres Hernandez-Potes","Tomás Arias-Vergara","Daiqi Liu","Andreas Maier","Paula Andrea Perez-Toro"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:04:02Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:04:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39972v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39972v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39972v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39955v1","title":"Mind The Gap (MTG-AD): Dataset and Fine-grained Evaluation for End-to-End Inline Audio Descriptions","abstract":"Audio descriptions (AD) are essential for making visual media accessible to blind and low-vision (BLV) users. While Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) offer a scalable solution for generating audio descriptions on-demand, their performance on ``inline'' audio descriptions -- which must fit within existing silences in a video -- remains under-explored, particularly for diverse user-generated content (UGC). We present a comprehensive investigation into MLLM-generated inline audio descriptions. First, we introduce MTG-AD, a professionally annotated dataset of 36 hours of videos, each averaging $\\sim$5 mins. and 8k+ descriptions in total, co-created by paired blind and sighted AD professionals to provide a high-quality benchmark for this task. Second, we propose MTG-AD-Eval, an evaluation framework based on audio description expert guidelines that provides fine-grained, reference-free actionable metrics for model improvement. Crucially, our framework evaluates the end-to-end task: it renders the generated scripts into audio tracks to assess their fit within the original video's natural silences, minimizing disruptive overlaps. Our analysis across frontier MLLMs reveals that while models accurately describe visual events and substantially reduce speech overlaps, gaps persist in narrative pacing and flow. Finally, we empirically validate that our automated and reference-free metrics correlate significantly with human quality ratings from sighted raters and 7 BLV participants, finding that while users narrowly prefer human-authored audio descriptions, they find MLLM-generated audio descriptions highly beneficial for comprehension. We provide actionable system design guidance and release MTG-AD at https://hf.co/datasets/google/mind-the-gap-inline-ad and MTG-AD-Eval at https://github.com/google/mtg-ad.","authors":["Subhashini Venugopalan","Jenny Tan","Taylor Roper","Jimmy Tobin","Anton Kast","Alicia Martin","Sam Sepah","Amy Pavel"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:51:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:51:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39955v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39955v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39955v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39948v1","title":"UniRA: Unified Representation Alignment and Auto-Encoding for Efficient Diffusion Generation","abstract":"Diffusion transformers (DiTs) have become the dominant architecture for high-fidelity image synthesis, yet they suffer from slow training convergence and high computational cost. Two recent lines of work address this from complementary angles: Representation Alignment (REPA) accelerates DiT training by aligning intermediate features with a pretrained external representation model (e.g., DINOv2), while Representation Auto-Encoders (RAE) replace the pixel-space VAE with a compact representation-space tokenizer learned from self-supervised features. However, these approaches are developed and applied in isolation: REPA still relies on a pixel-space VAE for latent diffusion, and RAE does not exploit the alignment signal that accelerates the diffusion backbone. We present \\textbf{UniRA}, a unified framework that couples representation alignment and representation auto-encoding into a single objective. UniRA learns a representation-space tokenizer whose output serves simultaneously as the diffusion target and the alignment anchor, so the tokenizer, the diffusion backbone, and the external representation model are co-optimized. We give a theoretical result showing that this joint optimization preserves the representation quality of the external model while accelerating diffusion convergence, and we derive a principled weighting that balances the reconstruction, alignment, and generation objectives. On ImageNet class-conditional generation at 256*256 and 512*512, UniRA matches or surpasses state-of-the-art DiTs (SD3, REPA, RAE) in FID while reducing training steps by up to 5*. We further show that the unified representation space supports improved zero-shot classification and linear probing, confirming that generation and understanding benefit from a shared, aligned latent space.","authors":["An Lanji","Dawei Liu","Jin Li","Haoran Xu","Mei Chen","Yu Tian"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:46:29Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:46:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39948v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39948v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39948v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39936v1","title":"MLLM-GS: Multimodal Large Language Model Prior-Guided Fast Novel View Synthesis in Extreme Environments","abstract":"Novel view synthesis (NVS) in degraded scenes aims to reconstruct 3D scenes from multi-view observations affected by various degradations, and to generate clear and photorealistic images at unseen viewpoints. Despite recent advances, existing methods often rely on degradation-specific models, limiting their generalization across diverse extreme environments. Generative models provide rich visual priors, but their outputs often suffer from pixel misalignment and color deviations, which can propagate into the reconstructed 3D scene and compromise reconstruction quality. Furthermore, the high computational and memory costs of 3D reconstruction hinder the practical application of NVS in extreme environments. To this end, we propose MLLM-GS, a multimodal large language model (MLLM) prior-guided framework for fast NVS in extreme environments. MLLM-GS consists of two branches: MLLM prior-guided view enhancement and fast 3D scene reconstruction. The former leverages the rich visual priors of a frozen MLLM to enhance views under different severe degradations and introduces a lightweight correction network to reduce pixel shifts and color distortions in MLLM outputs. With supervision from only a few paired clean views, this branch produces reliable enhanced views for all degraded observations, providing pseudo-GT views for subsequent 3D reconstruction. The latter improves 3DGS efficiency by optimizing rasterization, Gaussian memory layout, and parameter updates, reducing redundant computation, memory-access overhead, and optimization costs. Extensive experiments on multiple degraded scenes demonstrate that MLLM-GS effectively handles various severe degradations and achieves high-quality NVS with significantly lower reconstruction time and memory consumption. Project Page: https://wzlwlm13.github.io/MLLM-GS","authors":["Zhiliang Wu","Xinye Zheng","Kun Li","Fei Wang","Hao Guo","Yanyan Wei","Guanghui Yue","Beier Zhu","Wei Zhou","Xun Yang"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:36:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:36:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39936v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39936v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39936v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39924v1","title":"PhysAg3D: Bridging 3D Generative and Agentic Models for High-Fidelity and Physically-Interactive Assets","abstract":"Generating interactive 3D assets from a single image requires realistic appearance, fidelity to the depicted object, and plausible physical behavior. Yet detailed geometry alone does not determine how an object should move or deform. We introduce PhysAg3D, which couples 3D generative models with agentic reasoning and tool use. A vision-language agent first infers semantic parts, candidate interactions, and physical priors from the image; a 3D generator then produces a textured whole-object mesh from the same image. A hierarchical interaction representation links these revisable priors to mechanisms and the generated surfaces they control, organizing interaction construction and refinement. Guided by this representation and observations of the mesh, the agent uses geometric and physical tools to construct parts, joints, deformable regions, and surface bindings while retaining useful generated geometry and materials. Rendered interactions and execution measurements guide targeted revisions within a fixed budget. The framework accommodates both rigid articulation and compliant deformation. Quantitative and qualitative evaluations on real photographs and external benchmarks demonstrate detailed, image-aligned assets with plausible interactions. Ablations support bounded refinement and source-surface preservation. Project Page: supra-3d.github.io/PhysAg3D.","authors":["Bi'an Du","Fanpeng Meng","Jiemin Fang","Chen Yang","Pufan Li","Daizong Liu","Qi Tian","Wei Hu"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:29:27Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:29:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39924v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39924v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39924v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39913v1","title":"TokenRepel: Generating Diverse Image Sets Without Compromising Quality","abstract":"We present an inference-time approach that enhances the visual diversity of image sets generated by text-to-image models, without compromising the average image quality. Our method leverages the intrinsic geometry of hidden representations in pretrained models to diversify images generated from the same prompt. First, we introduce a token repulsion mechanism that increases the angle between feature tokens across samples. It takes a negative Riemannian gradient step on a von Mises-Fisher mixture distribution of tokens on the unit hypersphere. Second, to preserve the quality of each output image, we propose a token density preservation mechanism that projects the repulsion-induced prediction change onto a half-space aligned with the model's score direction. This encourages the updates to remain within the high-density regions learned by the generative model. We further extend this mechanism to the output of each intermediate layer, enabling finer-grained iterative guidance. We evaluate our method on three types of pretrained text-to-image models. Experiments show substantial gains in visual diversity under identical prompts, while maintaining a comparable level of image fidelity.","authors":["Qingtao Yu","Zhen Wang","Ning Lai","Boyang Hu","Hongdong Li","Dylan Campbell"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:20:24Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:20:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39913v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39913v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39913v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39889v1","title":"sDMD: Sinkhorn Distribution Matching Distillation","abstract":"Distribution Matching Distillation (DMD) distills multi-step diffusion models into few-step generators by minimizing reverse KL divergence. Despite its strong performance, DMD guides samples by local density alone, which introduces two limitations: teacher scores can be unreliable in regions of low teacher density, and the mode-seeking behavior of reverse KL can collapse the student onto a subset of the teacher modes. We first connect DMD to Wasserstein gradient flow, showing that its gradient follows from a fixed-point formulation with integral KL (IKL) as the energy functional. This perspective motivates augmenting IKL with an optimal transport term that addresses these limitations by guiding outlier samples toward the target and promoting mode coverage. We realize this term using Sinkhorn divergence estimated from finite batches. In practice, however, errors in the estimated Sinkhorn velocity can perturb in-domain samples for which IKL guidance is already accurate. To this end, we introduce sDMD, which adaptively activates the Sinkhorn correction based on discriminator outputs, focusing on outlier samples where IKL may be unreliable. With 2--4 sampling steps on FLUX.1-dev, Qwen-Image-20B, and Wan2.1-14B, sDMD outperforms distillation baselines on GenEval, DPG, WISE, and VBench while improving sample diversity over DMD2.","authors":["Jaihoon Kim","Lingxiao Li","Soshi Shimada","Roshni Cooper","Minhyuk Sung","Yiwei Zhao"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:04:23Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:04:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39889v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39889v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39889v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39880v1","title":"What Survives Label Downsampling? Exact Region Survival for Coarse Segmentation Targets","abstract":"Segmentation networks that predict at an output stride are trained against label maps downsampled by strided sampling, nearest-neighbor resampling or majority pooling. These methods label each coarse grid cell on its own, so a region that wins no cell is absent from the target and receives no positive label. We present MatchDown, which builds the coarse target from a minimum-cost matching between regions and the grid cells they occupy. It keeps as many regions as any one-region-per-cell target can, certifies how many losses are unavoidable, and with priority tiers (MatchDown-P) keeps every evaluated region (a region that a metric scores) of at least one cell's area. On COCO, Cityscapes and BBBC038 at factors 2 to 16, MatchDown-P loses exactly the minimum number of regions, whereas the downsampling used by StarDist, nnU-Net and connectomics pyramids loses four to five times as many on COCO at factor 8. Keeping a region in the target and reconstructing its shape are different problems: we show that no region lost by majority pooling can be reconstructed above IoU 0.5 by upsampling any coarse target, yet on BBBC038 a star-convex head trained on MatchDown targets segments 35% of the nuclei that majority pooling loses above IoU 0.5, against 15% when trained on majority targets and 24% on StarDist's strided target. On two nucleus datasets, with thresholds cross-validated for count error, this head has instance accuracy at IoU 0.5 (Acc50) 2.6 to 4.2 points above the strided target and at most 2.8 points below cell-center sampling, and a lower count error than the strided and cell-center targets on BBBC039. On BBBC039, an affinity head, which outputs one label per cell, lowers count error relative to majority targets by 9.3 nuclei per image at 7.2 points lower Acc50. MatchDown runs once, before training, in place of the label-downsampling step.","authors":["Zhiyuan Zhang","Yukun Ou","San Tek Choi"],"published":"2026-10-29T11:57:44Z","updated":"2026-10-29T11:57:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39880v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39880v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39880v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39848v1","title":"LiDAR-SHIELD: A Fully Dockerized Adversarial Robustness Assessment Framework for LiDAR-Based 3D Perception Models","abstract":"LiDAR-based 3D perception underpins autonomous driving and robotics, yet deploying these models safely requires stress-testing them against adversarial inputs before real-world use. Although state-of-the-art adversarial attacks exist, their implementations are often fragmented across individual repositories, each tied to a specific architecture or task and requiring manual environment reconstruction and model-specific integration code. We present LIDARSHIELD, a unified and fully Dockerized framework that reverses this workflow: rather than installing the target model inside the toolkit, LIDARSHIELD exposes any model through three lightweight HTTP endpoints, namely /info, /forward, and /gradient As a result, connecting a new model or dataset requires only a single loader function or adapter. The framework supports both 3D semantic segmentation and 3D object detection through the same engine-service protocol, integrating a task-aware suite of state-of-the-art attacks, including PGD and Square Attack for segmentation, and point perturbation, point removal, point injection, and LiDAttack for detection. This enables systematic exposure of corner cases and vulnerabilities across heterogeneous LiDAR backbones. The LiDAR-Shield codebase is publicly available at https://github.com/alexandrosgk/LiDAR-shield.","authors":["Alexandros Gkillas","Ioulia Kapsali","Christos Anagnostopoulos","Aris S. Lalos"],"published":"2026-10-29T11:27:53Z","updated":"2026-10-29T11:27:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39848v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39848v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39848v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39835v1","title":"Aligning Medical Vision Language Models at the Capability Boundary with Entropy Adaptive Rubric Grounding","abstract":"Medical diagnostic reasoning requires verifiable links between visual evidence and clinical knowledge, yet current medical vision language models are aligned with objectives that reward the wrong behavior. Uniform supervised fine tuning lets easy cases dominate updates while clinically critical boundary cases saturate prematurely. Sparse rewards based only on final-answer correctness cannot assess anatomical grounding or clinical logic, inviting reward hacking through answer shortcuts. We propose MedCBR, a two-stage alignment framework coupling boundary-aware preference optimization with rubric-grounded reinforcement learning. First, we construct counterfactual boundary preferences at the model's capability frontier using empirical pass rates and preference pairs that share reasoning prefixes but diverge at decisive diagnostic branches. Difficulty Weighted Boundary Preference Optimization (DBPO) concentrates learning on this calibrated boundary region. Second, we distill reusable clinical rubrics from boundary trajectories and activate them according to prediction entropy. Entropy Gated Grounded Policy Optimization (EGPO) rewards answer correctness, anatomical grounding, and reasoning fidelity while suppressing rubric noise on reliable samples. A reference policy anchored to the boundary-aligned model couples population-level boundary priors with instance-level uncertainty, making diagnostic reasoning more accurate and clinically grounded. Experiments on six medical benchmarks show consistent improvements over strong general, specialized, and reinforcement learning baselines, reaching 61.2% average accuracy with the largest gains on challenging differential diagnosis tasks. Ablations show super-additive gains: combining the two stages exceeds the sum of their individual improvements.","authors":["Hongyu Bai","Jiaqi Zeng","Chunzheng Zhu"],"published":"2026-10-29T11:15:12Z","updated":"2026-10-29T11:15:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39835v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39835v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39835v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39812v1","title":"GROME: GROunding Factorized MEmory into Video World Models","abstract":"Long-horizon consistency is essential for video world models, yet it becomes difficult to maintain once observed content falls outside the context window. Existing memory mechanisms either retrieve history as implicit context, which remains constrained by context capacity and entangles static and dynamic information or consolidate history into explicit global representations, which struggle to model evolving dynamics and accumulate geometric errors. To overcome these limitations, we introduce \\textbf{GROME}, a novel factorized memory framework that augments video world models for long-horizon coherence across both static scenes and dynamic entities. GROME operates via three synergistic components: 1)~\\emph{Static-Dynamic Factorization} decouples historical evidence into static and dynamic components, preserving static scene geometry and dynamic entity states separately; 2)~\\emph{Local Memory Retrieval} selects spatially aligned static history to eliminate geometric drift alongside the latest dynamic entity states to ensure temporal continuity; 3)~\\emph{Training-Free Memory Injection} seamlessly grounds the factorized memory into video world models via their native conditioning pathways. Experiments on long-horizon revisit scenarios demonstrate that GROME substantially enhances scene persistence and dynamic entity fidelity over existing methods, all while preserving the visual quality and controllability of the frozen backbone without additional training. Beyond self-rollouts, GROME readily accommodates external observations as plug-and-play memory, unlocking flexible and controllable video continuation and scene exploration.","authors":["Fang Yu","Xiaoxue Wu","Haiyu Zhang","Yangfu Li","Botong Zhao","Yu Qiao","Tianfan Xue","Yue Lu","Xinyuan Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T10:54:32Z","updated":"2026-10-29T10:54:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39812v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39812v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39812v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39773v1","title":"Quantum-Inspired Convolutional Networks for High-Resolution Pseudo-Label Generation from Multimodal Earth Observation Data","abstract":"Accurate land-cover mapping from multimodal Earth observation (EO) data remains a challenge in regions where high-resolution annotations are scarce or costly to obtain. This work introduces a quantum convolutional encoder-decoder network for pseudo-label generation using Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery, guided only by coarse MODIS-derived land-cover maps. The proposed framework uses quantum-inspired convolutional blocks to enhance feature extraction and cross-sensor fusion, enabling the model to infer a fine-scale semantic structure from heterogeneous inputs. Experiments were conducted on the DFC2020 dataset, which provides a quadruple of Sentinel-1 SAR, Sentinel-2 multispectral, low-resolution MODIS labels and high-resolution reference land-cover maps. The proposed training framework first learns a robust representation of multimodal inputs through the reconstruction branch before any supervision from low-resolution labels is applied. This self-supervised stage enables the model to capture a fine-scale spectral-spatial structure, which is then used to generate high-resolution pseudo-labels. These pseudo-labels are subsequently evaluated through classification metrics on the test data, demonstrating that the proposed approach produces more spatially coherent and class-consistent compared to classical baselines. The results highlight the potential of quantum-inspired deep learning to improve label generation in low-annotation remote sensing scenarios, offering a scalable path towards high-quality land-cover products for EO applications.","authors":["Abhishek Singh","Giovanni Cuozzo","Alexander Jacob","Claudia Notarnicola"],"published":"2026-10-29T10:23:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T10:23:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39773v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39773v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39773v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39727v1","title":"Cross-Encoder Anchor Refinement for Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection","abstract":"Zero-shot anomaly detection aims to transfer anomaly discrimination from auxiliary training domains to unseen categories while localizing subtle defects. A key challenge is to construct discriminative references that combine contextual representations with complementary local evidence. We present Cross-Encoder Anchor Refinement (CEAR), which connects two visual encoding paths for the same image at the anchor stage, before normal and anomalous prototypes are constructed. An independent convolutional encoder pretrained for industrial product recognition builds an image-specific memory shared across selected vision transformer (ViT) layers. Spatial anchors read from ViT patch features query this memory through cross-attention and undergo residual refinement. Image-conditioned normal and anomalous queries then gate anchor aggregation to construct prototypes for anomaly scoring. Across 13 industrial and medical datasets, CEAR achieves the best performance in 57 of 68 evaluations spanning image-level detection and pixel-level localization. Notably, CEAR surpasses all other state-of-the-art methods in 41 of 44 pixel-level comparisons. Across 11 localization datasets, mean gains in pixel-level maximum F1 score and average precision are 4.45 and 3.79 percentage points, respectively. This study shows how cross-encoder interaction at the anchor stage can integrate complementary visual representations for fine-grained anomaly localization across domains.","authors":["Kaisong Ji","Ziyang Xu","Chang Bian","Yu Zhou","Wenyu Liu","Xinggang Wang"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:56:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:56:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39727v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39727v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39727v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39721v1","title":"WorldProbe: When Video World Models Fall Apart under Long-Horizon Spatial Exploration","abstract":"Video world models generate future visual observations from camera or action inputs, with the goal of synthesizing persistent 3D worlds that can be explored across time and space. Existing benchmarks typically evaluate short interactions or rely on camera trajectories fixed before generation. The performance of video world models under long-horizon exploration with extensive camera motion remains under-explored. We introduce WorldProbe, a diagnostic benchmark that uses a goal-conditioned multimodal agent to adapt camera actions online during generation. WorldProbe comprises 164 test cases across four probing families. Across 10 state-of-the-art models, we generate more than 1,600 rollouts totaling approximately 800 minutes, with horizons up to 180 seconds. Our evaluation across four dimensions reveals both the degradation of local measures and the emergence of long-range inconsistencies. We find that recent models sustain visual and content quality for longer, while long-range memory and evolution remain weak. These results expose a gap between locally stable generation and persistent world consistency that is largely missed by existing benchmarks. Code and benchmark resources are available at https://github.com/Untitled-W/WorldProbe.","authors":["Minqi Wang","Jie Peng","Hejun Dong","Ruchang Yao","Puyi Wang","Siyuan Huang","Jihai Zhang","Yafu Li","Qi Dou","Xiaoye Qu","Yu Cheng"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:51:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:51:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39721v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39721v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39721v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39715v1","title":"NIF: Normal-Intersection Fields for Geometrically Consistent Oriented Box Refinement","abstract":"Accurate oriented detection requires a box to follow object boundaries, yet a standard regression head predicts its center, size, and angle from a pooled region representation. Small errors in these coupled parameters can displace long boundaries even when the object is correctly classified. We propose NIF, a refinement module that replaces an additional box regressor with normal-intersection fields. Given a coarse oriented box, the module observes local features at 20 boundary anchors and predicts signed distances along their outward normals. These observations admit an exact affine representation in five geometric variables. A differentiable, reliability-weighted least-squares fit projects the predicted field onto this representation and analytically recovers a refined rectangle. Tangential pooling preserves the normal position profile, while shared projection limits the added network to 0.132M parameters. On DOTA-v1.0, NIF reaches 78.00%, 79.11%, and 79.02% mAP with Oriented R-CNN, LSKNet-S, and Strip R-CNN, respectively, achieving the highest single-scale SOTA result in Table 1. It reaches 68.19% on DIOR-R, improves matched FAIR1M/DIOR-R baselines by 0.30/0.68%, and raises HRSC mean IoU from 0.867 to 0.888 while reducing field error by 24.5%. Across four different detector frameworks with the detectors frozen, relative AP75 improves by 1.57-4.18% and COCO-style mAP by 0.42-3.74%, without detector retraining. Code is available at https://github.com/zsk466991125-collab/Normal-Intersection-Fields.","authors":["Shaokai Zheng"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:47:19Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:47:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39715v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39715v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39715v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39704v1","title":"Confidence Filtering in Video-Based Gait Assessment: Measurement Error versus Selection Shift","abstract":"Video-based gait assessment often keeps only high-confidence pose observations, which can lower measurement error but change the movement assessed. We compared confidence selection with budget-matched random selection in a Parkinson-related cohort (n = 96) and an NF2-related replication cohort (n = 18). In the Parkinson-related cohort, confidence selection produced a small but consistent shift in the observed movement summary (+0.05 SD, 95% CI 0.03 to 0.07), while held-out Mini-BESTest error stayed flat (-0.04 points, -0.13 to 0.05); a policy preserving motion-group proportions reduced the shift, while NF2 showed no shift and its predictor did not beat a constant baseline. Because this shift mixes measurement error with selection shift, we decompose and simulate both on the cohort's window structure. Filtering lowers local error when error depends on confidence, but biases the endpoint once confidence tracks movement more than error. In the Parkinson-related data, confidence was only weakly tied to movement (rho = 0.14). There, filtering is harmless if well-tracked windows are unbiased. If instead systematic error only redistributes within participants, filtering can add up to 0.58 degrees. Unchanged clinical error from such small-cohort predictors therefore cannot show that quality filtering preserves the assessed movement.","authors":["David Regev","Eran Beeri Bamani"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:37:48Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:37:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39704v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39704v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39704v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39701v1","title":"DEQ-Stereo: Deep Euilibrium Video Stereo Matching via Adaptive Convergence","abstract":"Iterative refinement models have significantly improved stereo matching accuracy, but their rigid, fixed update schedules incur heavy computational costs and a strict trade-off between speed and accuracy. This challenge is magnified in video streams, where traditional online methods resort to reducing feature channels or relying on expensive temporal warping. To address this, we propose \\textbf{DEQ-Stereo}, a novel video stereo matching framework based on the Deep Equilibrium (DEQ) paradigm. Instead of unrolling a fixed number of iterations, DEQ-Stereo reformulates the refinement as an implicit fixed-point problem, adaptively terminating once convergence is met. To ensure fast and stable intra-frame convergence, we introduce the \\textbf{Convolutional Simplified Gated Unit (ConvSGU)}, which eliminates unnecessary non-linearities by modeling the latent state as a gated, element-wise sum of inputs. Furthermore, to exploit cross-frame temporal coherence, we propose \\textbf{Temporal State Transfer (TST)}, a warm-start strategy that propagates the converged hidden state to the next frame without requiring explicit pose or warping priors. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that DEQ-Stereo delivers state-of-the-art performance with a superior accuracy-to-latency trade-off.","authors":["Kiljoon Han","Kyumin Hwang","Wonheok Choi","Jeonghoon Kim","Sunghoom Im"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:36:49Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:36:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39701v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39701v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39701v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39676v1","title":"Event-SAMA: Language-Grounded Video Object Segmentation on Neuromorphic Event Streams","abstract":"Event cameras offer microsecond temporal resolution, high dynamic range and no motion blur, but referring video object segmentation (RVOS) lacks event-stream datasets and models. We introduce Event-SAMA-239K, a synthetic-event conversion of the SAMA-239K grounded video-chat corpus and all additional SAMA training data: 30,650 video clips and 392,162 images. Optical-flow, FILM or saccade upsampling followed by a linear event generator preserves all mask, referring-expression and dialogue annotations. We also introduce Event-SAMA, adapting SAMA-4B with a two-channel polarity patch embedding initialised from pretrained RGB weights and a zero-initialised event adapter. End-to-end training yields 24.37 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ on MeViS valid_u and 22.33 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ on the converted ReVOS valid subset, establishing the first event-native RVOS baseline (RGB SAMA-4B: 55.4 on MeViS). Frozen RGB SAMA-4B with pseudo-RGB renderings or E2VID reconstructions of the same events scores 13.23 and 15.94 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$. Without retraining, Event-SAMA works on real event-camera recordings when the accumulation window matches their event density. A seed-controlled ablation with a measured noise floor of 0.47 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ finds gains from diverse training sources over MeViS alone (+4.99), RGB-mean event-stem initialisation (+4.33), and a trainable SAM2 mask decoder (+2.76); the event adapter, partial ViT unfreezing and event polarity are within noise at this budget. An earlier, partially converted corpus snapshot trained with the identical recipe scores 1.38 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ higher on MeViS and 1.12 higher on ReVOS. Both are single runs that also differ in schedule length, so we draw no conclusion about corpus scale.","authors":["Sergei Fedorchenko","Max Zehnder","Nikola Zubić","Rong Zou","Davide Scaramuzza"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:22:49Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:22:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39676v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39676v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39676v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39664v1","title":"EditAgent: Rethinking Agentic Image Editing","abstract":"Agentic image editors decompose complex requests into steps, but when a step fails, they mostly retry with the same narrow set of tools and a judge that scores each output by eye directly. We argue that such agents are bounded by two spaces: the action space of what the agent can do, and the observation space of what its judge can see. On our expert-annotated benchmark, a general-purpose editor yields no usable result in over 40% of the cases, and no judge is reliable across edit types. We present EditAgent, which expands both spaces. It routes each step to general editors and specialized methods, and attaches to each candidate a verification record. With these records, off-the-shelf VLM judges outperform finetuned reward models. Candidates are generated in parallel and judged in one listwise call: experts prefer this to sequential refinement in 78.5% of decided comparisons, at no more than half the judge calls. EditAgent improves two open-weight editors on three benchmarks and serves as a data engine for training stronger editors.","authors":["Haiyang Xu","Tianyu Wang","Jialu Li","Jiteng Mu","Yuanjun Xiong","Jianming Zhang","Zhuowen Tu","Mingze Xu"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:15:52Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:15:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39664v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39664v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39664v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39663v1","title":"AB-Attention: Accurate Block Attention for Accelerating Video Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Video diffusion transformers generate high-quality videos, but the quadratic cost of 3D full attention dominates their inference. Dynamic block-sparse attention reduces this cost by computing exact attention only on key/value blocks selected from current activations. However, its fidelity degrades under aggressive sparsity: blocks are scored from coarse summaries or formed by costly global clustering, pruned blocks vanish from the softmax, and whole tiles are kept or dropped even when only a few of their keys matter. To address these limitations, we propose Accurate Block Attention (AB-Attention), a training-free method that accurately approximates dense attention at high sparsity. Its Intra-Block Clustering Selector (IBCS) estimates block importance faithfully yet cheaply by clustering queries only within each query block, without permuting tokens. Sampling with Inclusion-Probability Weighting (SIP) then samples blocks in proportion to these estimates and reweights them by inverse inclusion probability, so that one pass estimates dense attention rather than truncating it. Finally, its kernel gathers key/value blocks much smaller than a tile into full tensor-core tiles, refining selection at no extra tensor-core cost. On Wan2.1 and HunyuanVideo, AB-Attention skips 89--92\\% of attention, whereas the baselines compute 1.8--3.6$\\times$ as much, yet it still improves PSNR by up to 4.4~dB over the strongest baseline and achieves up to 2.23$\\times$ end-to-end speedup. Our code is available at https://github.com/InfRec-Cardinal/AB-Attention.","authors":["Bin Xu","Mo Zhang","Zhengfeiyang Zhang","Xinfeng Yang","Yixing Ma","Wei Wang","HeXin Wang","Pengfei Zhou","Wangbo Zhao"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:15:39Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:15:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39663v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39663v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39663v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39648v1","title":"Annotation-Free Representation Learning for Polyp Retrieval in Colonoscopy Videos","abstract":"Colonoscopy is inherently comparative: reliable visual assessment of polyps often depends on complementary views acquired under different viewpoints and imaging conditions. Retrieving such views requires polyp tracklet representations, which recent contrastive methods learn from temporal cues but still build from manually annotated bounding boxes. We propose an annotation-free framework that constructs tracklet views directly from raw colonoscopy videos, enabling temporal self-supervised learning on full-procedure videos, including frames without visible polyps. On REAL-Colon, our method achieves state-of-the-art results on tracklet retrieval and re-identification, outperforming both self-supervised and supervised baselines. We then introduce few-shot polyp retrieval, a task closer to clinical use, where a few observations of a target polyp are used to retrieve its remaining tracklets: with only two examples, our method surpasses most competing methods given ten. Finally, we show that a modest number of unlabeled procedures suffices for competitive performance, making on-site model development more practical. Code is available at https://github.com/lparolari/freerep.","authors":["Luca Parolari","Lamberto Ballan","Carlo Biffi","Loic Le Folgoc"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:05:22Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:05:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39648v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39648v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39648v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39646v1","title":"FlexTry-On: A Time-Efficient Framework for Multi-Scenario Virtual Try-on","abstract":"Virtual try-on has achieved remarkable progress in recent years, yet most existing methods are designed for a specific scenario, such as cloth-to-person or person-to-person try-on, and often require separate conditioning strategies and processing pipelines for different settings. We present FlexTry-On, a unified and time-efficient diffusion-based framework for diverse virtual try-on scenarios, including cloth-to-person, person-to-person, cross-domain, and in-the-wild settings. FlexTry-On adopts a common conditioning formulation, where a text-aware condition extraction strategy converts heterogeneous garment sources into a unified representation, and a dual-conditioning diffusion transformer jointly models garment appearance and person identity. Extensive experiments demonstrate competitive performance on both cloth-to-person and person-to-person benchmarks while maintaining fast inference. Moreover, the same framework can be directly applied to unseen cross-domain scenarios without requiring separate architectures or scenario-specific processing pipelines. These results highlight the potential of unified and efficient virtual try-on frameworks as a practical step toward more flexible and widely applicable virtual try-on systems.","authors":["Phuong Dam","Quyen Nguyen Duc","Son Hoang Minh","Daeyoung Kim"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:03:27Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:03:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39646v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39646v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39646v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39639v1","title":"Where Do Vision Language Models Fail at 3D Reasoning?","abstract":"Vision-language models can get individual judgments right while struggling to recover and consistently use the exact properties of a 3D object. To characterize this gap, we introduce BedBench, a controlled benchmark of thirteen procedurally generated tasks spanning individual judgments and composite answers. Across twelve open-weight and four frontier models, per-color counting exposes a pronounced weakness: humans achieve 93.3% accuracy, compared with 7.2% for the best model. Targeted fine-tuning raises one model's accuracy from 3.0% to 68.6%, but reveals a further difficulty: its answers about the most common colors remain inconsistent with its own per-color counts. Supplying those counts as text improves its judgments, showing that recovering a property does not mean using it consistently. Reasoning, scaling, and added visual cues each improve individual judgments, but none makes exact answers reliable. To locate the difficulty, we probe Qwen3.5-27B: exact per-color counts remain recoverable at the language model's input, yet the model's answers rarely recover them (65.0% for the probe vs. 3.0% for the model). Image-removal controls further show that some apparent successes require no visual evidence. These findings motivate evaluating 3D reasoning by exact correctness, consistent use, and image dependence, rather than accuracy alone. Code and dataset are publicly available: https://github.com/leduckhai/BedBench","authors":["Khai Le-Duc","Nguyen Kim Hai Bui","An Ngo","Nam D. Hoang","Yueqi Song","Jiarui Liu","Thai Quoc Hoang","Fang Wu","Chris Ngo","Terry Jingchen Zhang","Mona T. Diab","Ran Xu","Silvio Savarese","Xiao-Ming Wu"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:00:14Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:00:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39639v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39639v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39639v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39619v1","title":"FutureWorld: A Plug-and-Play Causal Next-State Predictor for Interactive Video World Exploration","abstract":"Interactive video world models aim to follow user actions while maintaining scene consistency over extended interactions. History-based methods reuse generated latents to preserve scene content, but three limitations of this reuse contribute to error recirculation. 1) The latent generated at each interaction is retained as history, potentially containing blurred details, geometric distortions, or semantic drift. 2) Although retrieved historical content matches the current scene, it can still contain errors that are carried into the next chunk. 3) New outputs then become history for later interactions, potentially reinforcing the same errors through repeated reuse. To limit this cycle, we aim to predict the future scene state from the current generated latent and the next action before generating the next interaction's video chunk. This future-state prior provides guidance for the next chunk, with the aim of reducing the errors carried forward from history. To this end, we propose a plug-and-play Causal Next-Chunk Predictor (C-NCP) with cross-model adaptability and prediction-guided modulation. We integrate C-NCP into four base generators and evaluate the augmented models across three benchmarks for interactive long-video generation and action-conditioned world modeling. Extensive experiments demonstrate reduced error accumulation and improved long-horizon consistency across diverse generation settings. The project page is at https://GuangYuanKK.github.io/futureworld.","authors":["Guangyuan Li","Kelu Yao","Shuolin Xu","Xinlei Yu","Wei Xing","Lei Zhao","Peng-Tao Jiang","Xiaogang Xu","Xiaobin Hu"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:47:55Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:47:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39619v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39619v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39619v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39609v1","title":"DiFuse: Fusing Native Diffusion Representations for Spatial Reasoning in Unified Multimodal Models","abstract":"Unified multimodal models (UMMs) jointly support visual understanding and image generation, yet information flow between these two capabilities remains largely asymmetric: understanding representations routinely condition generation, whereas generative representations are rarely reused for multimodal reasoning. In this work, we investigate whether intermediate representations from a UMM's native diffusion branch can enhance spatial reasoning without compromising general multimodal understanding. We propose DiFuse, which selectively incorporates intermediate diffusion representations into the understanding pathway of UMMs. Through systematic layer-wise analysis, we identify which diffusion representations are most informative and where they can be integrated with minimal interference to semantic processing. Guided by these findings, DiFuse spatially aligns diffusion features with visual tokens and injects them into selected multimodal decoder layers via residual fusion. Extensive experiments on UniWorld-V1 show that, DiFuse consistently improves spatial reasoning over a matched SFT baseline while maintaining broadly comparable performance on general multimodal benchmarks. Further analyses show that these gains depend critically on the choice of diffusion representations, injection positions, and the preservation of spatial correspondence between diffusion features and visual tokens. Results on OmniGen2 further demonstrate the transferability of DiFuse across unified multimodal architectures. Overall, our findings show that native diffusion representations can provide complementary spatial cues for multimodal reasoning when selectively integrated in a structure-preserving manner.","authors":["Ruixin Fan","Weining Wang","Jiahui Sun","Tailai Chen","Jing Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:43:08Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:43:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39609v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39609v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39609v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39602v1","title":"Evaluating Cultural Knowledge Editing in Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) frequently fail to recognize culture-specific concepts, especially from underrepresented regions. Effectively injecting this long-tail knowledge into existing models remains a challenge. In this work, we investigate knowledge editing as a targeted alternative by introducing CulturalBind, a benchmark comprising 3.2K images of 1K concepts from 6 domains, designed to evaluate culture-aware cross-modal knowledge as well as concept-level knowledge editing in VLMs. Pre-edit evaluation reveals that textual and visual knowledge is decoupled: models often demonstrate text-based knowledge of cultural concepts but fail to identify them from images. We then evaluate editing methods in single and sequential scenarios. Although current methods successfully correct individual visual recognition errors, they exhibit low cross-modal portability, failing to integrate edits into the model's broader parametric knowledge. Furthermore, performance degrades rapidly under sequential editing, highlighting the need for comprehensive adaptation approaches that better consolidate multimodal knowledge over time.","authors":["Malvina Nikandrou","Georgios Pantazopoulos","Ioannis Konstas","Alessandro Suglia"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:39:51Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:39:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39602v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39602v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39602v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39592v1","title":"Operator-Aware Posterior Sampling for Multivariate Weather Downscaling","abstract":"Statistical downscaling is the task of reconstructing high-resolution meteorological fields from coarse inputs. A principled approach formulates downscaling as a Bayesian inverse problem, using a diffusion prior over high-resolution fields and incorporating the coarse field only at inference through a likelihood. This decouples the prior from the coarse source, allowing one pretrained model to adapt to different sources. However, two challenges limit this flexibility: (1) when coarse and target fields come from different reanalysis systems, no fixed coarsening operator can capture their relationship; and (2) guidance gradients can differ by orders of magnitude across variables, making a single guidance strength ineffective. This paper addresses both challenges within the guidance loop, leaving the prior unchanged. We learn the forward operator from paired data and normalize the gradient per channel. We show that this normalization corresponds to steepest descent under a block-max norm, allowing a single guidance strength across variables. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/CarloSaccardi/operator-aware-weather-downscaling.","authors":["Carlo Saccardi","Rudolf Saathof","Geethu Joseph","Justin Dauwels"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:31:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:31:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39592v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39592v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39592v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39585v1","title":"TactoHand: Learning Geometry-Based Dense Hand-Surface Interaction Fields from Video","abstract":"Understanding how humans interact with the world requires a representation of physical interactions at the hand surface. Tactile sensing captures these interactions, but collecting tactile data at scale requires specialized hardware. We introduce TactoHand, a model that predicts three dense hand-surface interaction fields---contact, proximity, and 3D force---from monocular video. We derive geometry-based contact and proximity labels and motion-derived force labels from eligible training data within a curated collection spanning approximately 50 million source frames. TactoHand uses a temporal transformer to aggregate visual evidence across frames, while three learned queries decode the corresponding hand-surface fields. A differentiable physics layer further constrains force predictions using observed object motion. Across in-domain and out-of-domain benchmarks, TactoHand improves contact and proximity estimation over prior methods. In addition, physics supervision reduces position and rotation errors in motion rollouts, and the predicted contact and proximity fields improve downstream hand-object reconstruction. Together, these results show a scalable, low-cost path to learning hand-surface interaction fields without collecting tactile measurements. Code will be released at https://github.com/zhenqis123/Tactohand.","authors":["Ziheng Xi","Weibo Li","Jiayi Yu","Sipeng Zheng","Wanpeng Zhang","Haiweng Xu","Hao Luo","Chuan Mao","Zongqing Lu"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:25:15Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:25:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39585v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39585v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39585v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39553v1","title":"BEACON: Distilled Begin/End-Aware Structured Decoding for Continuous Sign Language Segmentation","abstract":"In segmentation-based continuous sign language recognition, an untrimmed video is first partitioned into lexical units; segmentation is therefore a means to an end---downstream recognition. However, segmenters are usually judged by local boundary detection rather than by the quality of the lexical segments and the recognition they enable. We present BEACON, a begin/end-aware segmenter that combines four-class Outside/Begin/Inside/End (BIOE) labeling, constrained Viterbi decoding over eight legal transitions, and boundary knowledge distilled from a binary teacher into a shared student. Under strict no-cut evaluation with validation-based model selection, BEACON improves segment-F1 by 14.58/14.59 points and boundary-F1 by 2.17/1.22 points, and reduces the downstream word error rate (WER) by 9.10/4.15 points relative to the state-of-the-art in the PHOENIX-14/BSL Corpus. To examine which segmentation metric better reflects downstream recognition, we evaluated five distinct architecture families. Segment-F1 aligns more consistently with lower WER than boundary-F1 in both corpora (Spearman $ρ=-1.0/-0.9$ versus $-0.9/-0.8$). These results support evaluating sign language segmentation in terms of segment quality and downstream recognition utility rather than local boundary accuracy alone.","authors":["Guohou Zhang","Kalin Stefanov"],"published":"2026-10-29T07:55:11Z","updated":"2026-10-29T07:55:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39553v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39553v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39553v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"}],"favorites":[],"latest":{"generated_at_utc":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","category":"cs.CV","keywords":["video","retrieval","3d","agent","representation","instance","multimodal","diffusion"],"fetch_window_hours":72,"daily_window_hours":30,"match_count":75,"new_or_recent_papers":[{"id":"2610.40653v1","title":"Micro-Edits: Computational Support for Fine-Scale Edits in Film","abstract":"In film editing, every frame matters. While macro, content-level edits shape the high-level narrative of a film, small local edits often control timing and direct visual attention. For example, editors can adjust the precise timing of clips to control the length of a meaningful silence, or cut to shots that highlight a specific character's reactions to important moments. These small, detailed edits are slow and tedious to make in standard editing tools because users have to map high-level narrative goals to low-level frame-by-frame adjustments. At the same time, these fine-scale adjustments, which we call micro-edits, can be nuanced and expressive, with different choices serving different creative goals. In this work, we start by conducting a formative study with professional editors to understand the structure of micro-edits and how they are used. We then derive a mathematical formalization for this structure to develop tools that leverage AI and optimization to make exploring micro-edits simpler and more efficient for users. Finally, we validate our tools in multiple user studies and a wide range of video examples.","authors":["Nhan Tran","Hijung Valentina Shin","Abe Davis","Mackenzie Leake"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:59:49Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:59:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40653v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40653v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40653v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":103501,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：In film editing, every frame matters. While macro, content-level edits shape the high-level narrative of a film, small local edits often control timing and direct visual attention. For example, editors can adjust the precise timing of clips to control the len...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:53+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40643v1","title":"Luggage re-identification: A study of strategies towards a real-world application","abstract":"Luggage re-identification (ReID) is a critical task in video surveillance for forensics, security or border management (suspicious or abandoned luggage recognition), as well as luggage management (lost luggage recovery). It enables the association of observations of the same luggage across disjoint camera views in complex environments such as airports, train stations and border crossing. While existing approaches primarily focus on appearance-based cropped image matching under relatively clean conditions, real-world scenarios are characterized by frequent occlusions, cluttered background and crowded scenes which significantly degrade performance. Yet, strategies leveraging the complementarity of multiple shots and views from different video streams is overlooked. In this paper, we study strategies for an end-to-end pipeline that operates from multiple video streams to detect and re-identify luggage. We evaluate different variants on a benchmark dataset acquired in real-world environments to address the questions of selecting and merging different views and shots of the same luggage as well as training on different environment datasets for an overall ReID performance improvement in practical applications.","authors":["Quentin Dhôte","Romaric Audigier"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:59Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40643v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40643v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40643v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":43141,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Luggage re-identification (ReID) is a critical task in video surveillance for forensics, security or border management (suspicious or abandoned luggage recognition), as well as luggage management (lost luggage recovery). It enables the association of observat...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:54+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40640v1","title":"SonoWorld4D: From One Video to a 4D Audio-Visual Scene via Diffusion Synchronization","abstract":"Recent progress in visual scene generation has extended beyond static 3D scenes toward the more challenging problem of generating dynamic 4D scenes from minimal input. However, sound, despite being intrinsically coupled with dynamic objects and events, remains largely unmodeled in prior work. We introduce Video-to-4DAVScene, a new task that turns a video into an explorable, time-varying 4D audio-visual scene, and present the SonoWorld4D framework for this task. Given a monocular video, our method generates a 360 static background, reconstructs explicit motion trajectories of dynamic, sound-emitting foreground objects, and synthesizes a physically-grounded sound field consistent across multiple sources through dual-space audio diffusion synchronization. The resulting 4D scene supports real-time novel-view visual and spatial audio rendering. Evaluation on real-world dynamic scenes and a user study demonstrates SonoWorld4D generates 4D audio-visual scenes with perceptually realistic, physically grounded spatial audio consistent with scene geometry and visual content.","authors":["Derong Jin","Xiyi Chen","Ming C. Lin","Ruohan Gao"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:45Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40640v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40640v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40640v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93729,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Recent progress in visual scene generation has extended beyond static 3D scenes toward the more challenging problem of generating dynamic 4D scenes from minimal input. However, sound, despite being intrinsically coupled with dynamic objects and events, remain...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:54+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40636v1","title":"Probing Spatial State Representations in Visual Foundation Models","abstract":"An observer sees only part of the surrounding world, yet must keep track of what lies beyond the current view. Spatial perception therefore involves more than recognizing individual images: it calls for a persistent spatial state that is updated under self-motion and used to guide action. Do visual foundation models provide representations that support such a state? We study this question through four levels of spatial capability---spatial structure, object persistence, predictive spatial update, and actionability---using frozen-feature probes on ten representations under streaming egocentric observations of static indoor environments. The resulting capability profiles vary across tasks, network layers, and prefix lengths: strong readout of the visible scene does not ensure accurate object persistence or future-feature retrieval. Matched controls separate readout accuracy from reliance on visual history and motion. Native future prediction retains recoverable spatial structure, but a spatial reader trained on real features does not transfer to predicted features unchanged. Finally, goal-dependent candidate selection does not establish reliable continuous action utility: predicted actions respond to goal changes, yet supplying the correct goal does not reliably improve progress. Across the four levels, object persistence and actionability build on camera-pose readout, whereas feature prediction builds on visible-scene readout. Together, these findings motivate evaluating spatial representations not only by what can be decoded from them, but by whether that information is maintained beyond observation, updated predictively, and used for goal-directed action.","authors":["Baiqiao Yin","Zhongang Cai","Ruisi Wang","Qiwei Liang","Lei Yang","Ziwei Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:16Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40636v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40636v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40636v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":97438,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：An observer sees only part of the surrounding world, yet must keep track of what lies beyond the current view. Spatial perception therefore involves more than recognizing individual images: it calls for a persistent spatial state that is updated under self-mo...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:54+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40632v1","title":"SymAnt: Grounding Long-Horizon Action Anticipation with Adaptable Symbolic Priors","abstract":"As robots and intelligent systems increasingly interact with humans, they must anticipate long sequences of future actions from only brief observations. While purely neural models typically require extensive observations to predict future actions and remain difficult to interpret and adjust, symbolic priors offer a structured alternative. To this end, we introduce SymAnt, a novel neuro-symbolic approach that unifies the expressiveness of neural networks with the structured reasoning of scene and knowledge graphs. Given a short video, SymAnt performs a joint-search over both graphs to construct a symbolic context, enabling unified reasoning over spatial relationships, object attributes, affordances, and associated actions. This symbolic context serves as a prior for an iterative refinement process that progressively generates and improves the sequence of future actions. By grounding predictions in symbolic context, SymAnt anticipates long-horizon actions from short observation windows in a parameter-efficient manner, while enabling users to directly edit the underlying knowledge to adjust predictions without further training. Through our experiments, we demonstrate the effectiveness of SymAnt, particularly when only short observation contexts are available, across five diverse datasets: Breakfast, 50 Salads, EPIC-KITCHENS, EGTEA Gaze+, and Assembly101.","authors":["Fnu Aryan","Yaqi Xie","Joseph Campbell","Woojun Kim","Kwonjoon Lee","Hossein Nourkhiz Mahjoub","Ehsan Moradi-Pari","Katia Sycara","Simon Stepputtis"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:58:00Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:58:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40632v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40632v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40632v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93062,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：As robots and intelligent systems increasingly interact with humans, they must anticipate long sequences of future actions from only brief observations. While purely neural models typically require extensive observations to predict future actions and remain d...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:55+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40616v1","title":"Difficulty Calibration Across Time for Membership Inference in Diffusion Models","abstract":"Modern diffusion models are trained on massive datasets that often include copyrighted or private images. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) address this by testing whether a given image was in a model's training set. The standard approach scores an image by its reconstruction loss and flags low-loss images as members. This often fails because some images are simply easier to reconstruct than others, so a simple unseen image can score a lower loss than a complex training image. Difficulty calibration separates memorization from how difficult an image is to begin with. We show how to calibrate along two axes of time: training time and diffusion time. First, across training time, we introduce Reference-Model Calibration (RMC), which compares a fine-tuned model's loss against an earlier checkpoint. This cleanly isolates newly memorized data and gives near-perfect auditing, even without text captions. Since earlier checkpoints are not always available, we next present Temporal Self-Calibration (TSC), which calibrates across diffusion time using only a single model. Our key observation is that an image's difficulty persists across noise levels, whereas memorization is localized in time. By comparing the loss at a target timestep against neighboring timesteps from the same model, TSC attenuates image difficulty using the model's own trajectory. Empirically, our methods beat the state of the art by a wide margin, and unlike most existing attacks, generalize across different model parameterizations such as $x$-, $v$-, and $ε$-prediction models. Code is available at https://github.com/MichaelJoseph777/TSC.","authors":["Michael Joseph","Yedid Hoshen"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:54:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:54:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40616v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40616v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40616v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":88164,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Modern diffusion models are trained on massive datasets that often include copyrighted or private images. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) address this by testing whether a given image was in a model's training set. The standard approach scores an image by...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40614v1","title":"L3G-World: Real-Time Infinite World Generation with Latent 3D Gaussian Memory","abstract":"Interactive world models generate visual content conditioned on user actions, yet maintaining spatial consistency over long-horizon exploration in real time remains challenging. Existing approaches rely on either temporal context or explicit 3D memory, both of which scale poorly because memory grows with the length of the generated stream. Moreover, explicit 3D memories condition latent video generators by re-encoding RGB renderings, which precludes real-time generation. In this paper, we introduce a Latent 3D Gaussian Memory (L3G) that combines the efficiency of feed-forward reconstruction with a compact, rapidly renderable scene representation. Building on this memory, we propose L3G-World, in which a memory updater and a video diffusion transformer (DiT) alternate autoregressively to generate spatially consistent worlds. The memory updater spawns Gaussians in newly observed regions and refines existing ones, so that the memory grows with scene extent rather than stream length. Each Gaussian further stores a VAE latent feature, allowing the memory to be rendered directly into the DiT's latent space without VAE re-encoding. Experiments show that L3G-World achieves state-of-the-art generation quality and long-horizon spatial consistency, with up to $38.6\\times$ fewer 3D primitives, $3.2\\times$ faster memory updates, and $359\\times$ faster memory rendering relative to the most efficient prior 3D-memory baseline for each metric, enabling real-time world generation at 9 FPS.","authors":["Jinhyeok Choi","JoungBin Lee","Jongmin Lee","Minkyeong Jeon","Jiyoung Kim","Kaeun Han","Yoonjin Yoon","Seungryong Kim"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:54:42Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:54:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40614v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40614v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40614v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":97455,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Interactive world models generate visual content conditioned on user actions, yet maintaining spatial consistency over long-horizon exploration in real time remains challenging. Existing approaches rely on either temporal context or explicit 3D memory, both o...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40607v1","title":"Fight World Model: Customizable Combat through Visual Interaction Proxies","abstract":"A playable, programmable game world must maintain persistent state, enforce executable rules, and support extensible gameplay mechanics. Existing video world models can generate plausible combat, yet struggle to keep hit reactions and health updates consistent with game rules. We introduce Fight World Model (FWM), which separates rule-governed combat execution from video rendering. An executable engine maintains state and resolves interactions. A deterministic compiler encodes these outcomes and attempted actions in a spatially and temporally aligned interaction proxy. The proxy aligns action and skill windows, brief hit reactions, and persistent health by fighter identity and timing. These cues can be controlled separately to render interactions under modified rules. Identity-bound references preserve character appearance across side switches. We train FWM on FightWorld Corpus, spanning 52 skill effects, 187 character-appearance variants, and 15 arenas. Without retraining the renderer, FWM supports damage scaling, limited-hit immunity, lifesteal, and multi-stage skill mechanisms beyond the native game. It also allows character and arena customization. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of FWM, which outperforms existing game world models in action fidelity, hit reactions, health-state consistency, and character appearance preservation. Our project is available at https://fight-world-model.github.io.","authors":["Jiahua Li","Zixu John","Tom Zhong","Fuping Wu","Tianhao Xu","Ruicheng Zhang","Jun Zhou","Zunnan Xu"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:53:02Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:53:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40607v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40607v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40607v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":60417,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：A playable, programmable game world must maintain persistent state, enforce executable rules, and support extensible gameplay mechanics. Existing video world models can generate plausible combat, yet struggle to keep hit reactions and health updates consisten...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40599v1","title":"HairDeck: Animation-Ready Stylized Hair Cards from a Single Image","abstract":"The hair of stylized characters in games and avatars is built from hair cards: artist-placed ribbons whose geometry is the visible hair, animated by spring bones. Existing image-based methods produce realistic hair or a single fused mesh, while CHARM, the only generator of stylized hair cards, decodes a hairstyle autoregressively as a long sequence of control points. Observing that a card hairstyle is an unordered set of artist-defined primitives, we propose HairDeck, which generates it as one: each card is a single token, and a set diffusion transformer denoises all cards together. From a single image, HairDeck generates the cards and the spring-bone chains that drive them, yielding an animatable asset that exports to ready-to-use downstream formats. On CHARM's own test set, it lowers Chamfer distance by 44% and card-orientation error by more than half while running 33 times faster, and from single images it outperforms CHARM, image-to-3D pipelines and strand-based methods. Project page: https://hyzcluster.github.io/HairDeck","authors":["Yuze He","Yixiang Wei","Wang Zhao","Chuhao Chen","Yanning Zhou","Yong-Jin Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:50:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:50:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40599v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40599v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40599v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":77653,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：The hair of stylized characters in games and avatars is built from hair cards: artist-placed ribbons whose geometry is the visible hair, animated by spring bones. Existing image-based methods produce realistic hair or a single fused mesh, while CHARM, the onl...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40578v1","title":"VeriCUA: Verification as Agentic Exploration for Computer-Using Agents","abstract":"Building and scaling computer-using agents (CUAs) across desktop, mobile, and web environments requires reliable task-completion verification for data curation, evaluation, and reinforcement learning. Typical automated approaches prompt vision-language models (VLMs) to judge trajectories or generate programs to inspect application state. However, both rely on partial views of complex digital environments. VLM judges are restricted to supplied observations, while generated programs depend on accessible state. These views can omit decisive evidence or leave completion judgments unsupported. Tailoring separate verifiers to different platforms and access settings adds further engineering cost. We present VeriCUA, a platform-agnostic framework for verification through agentic exploration. It treats verification as dynamic evidence gathering and assessment within a shared procedure for online and offline settings across platforms and observation modalities. After inspecting the available context to construct a task rubric, the verifier selects GUI or code checks for unresolved requirements and combines their findings under explicit evidence rules. Experiments show improved agreement with reference labels over the compared baselines across these settings, with the largest offline gains on hard cases and stronger rejection of false completion claims online. Further analyses connect these gains to complementary evidence and test-time exploration.","authors":["Qiushi Sun","Da Yu","Alexander Knop","Amer Sinha","Lingpeng Kong","Ben Kao","Ravi Kumar","Pritish Kamath"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:45:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:45:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40578v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40578v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40578v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":70955,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Building and scaling computer-using agents (CUAs) across desktop, mobile, and web environments requires reliable task-completion verification for data curation, evaluation, and reinforcement learning. Typical automated approaches prompt vision-language models...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40530v1","title":"PhysCount: Do MLLMs Count Visual Appearances or Physical Entities?","abstract":"Images reveal appearances, but counting often asks about physical objects. A single entity may be visible directly, in several reflections, or through glass, so detecting every visible occurrence does not determine how many entities are present. Existing visual counting benchmarks rarely separate these two operations. We introduce PhysCount, a benchmark for evaluating whether multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can distinguish what appears in an image from what physically exists in the scene. PhysCount contains 546 real-world images and 4,595 human-written questions organized into 651 target-specific groups. For each group, annotators locate the relevant appearances, label how each is observed, and identify appearances of the same entity. Four complementary question families test the enumeration of appearances, the enumeration of physical entities, image-formation-specific counting, and appearance--entity correspondence. We measure both accuracy on each family and the proportion of groups answered entirely correctly. Across 40 MLLMs, counting visible appearances is substantially easier than recovering the identities that connect them. Even the strongest model answers every question correctly in only 13.36\\% of groups. Performance declines when one entity produces many appearances or when its appearances cross observation modes. Higher image resolution improves access to visual evidence but brings limited gains in complete-group accuracy, whereas answering related questions jointly produces a marked improvement. These results expose a gap between recognizing what is visible and correctly recovering the physical entities behind those observations.","authors":["Lidong Lu","Jiaxin Lv","Liangrui Dong","Xinyi Mao","Lidong Zou","Zhenquan Li","Tong Lu","Guo Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:33:34Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:33:34Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40530v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40530v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40530v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":128776,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Images reveal appearances, but counting often asks about physical objects. A single entity may be visible directly, in several reflections, or through glass, so detecting every visible occurrence does not determine how many entities are present. Existing visu...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40525v1","title":"CrossWeave: Cross-Modal Scene and Instance Retrieval from Sparse 2D-3D Alignment","abstract":"Cross-modal scene and instance retrieval aims to retrieve a corresponding 3D environment or object given query 2D images, serving as a foundational mechanism for spatial reasoning in robotics and AR/VR. Existing methods achieve this by learning alignment only at the level of whole scenes or objects, which provides no supervision for the fine-grained local structure within them. In this work, we show that supervision on a set of sparse 2D-3D correspondences, without scene- or instance-level contrastive objectives, is sufficient to train a single model for both scene- and instance-level retrieval. Specifically, we propose $\\textbf{CrossWeave}$, a novel framework for cross-modal retrieval that learns sparse patch-point alignment, which alone outperforms existing methods. An optimal transport-based aggregator over the aligned features gives further gains by clustering structurally redundant regions, freeing descriptor capacity for semantically distinct local features. CrossWeave achieves state-of-the-art performance on both scene- and instance-level retrieval on ScanNet and 3RScan with a $\\textit{single model}$, whereas existing methods require separate models for each task. Our results suggest that whole-scene encoding is unnecessary for cross-modal retrieval and that sparse local correspondence supervision is a more effective and flexible alternative.","authors":["Aadith Warrier","Gnana Prakash Punnavajhala","Siddharth Tourani","Muhammad Haris Khan","Avinash Sharma","Madhava Krishna"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:32:46Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:32:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40525v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40525v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40525v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","3d","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":64753,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, 3d, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Cross-modal scene and instance retrieval aims to retrieve a corresponding 3D environment or object given query 2D images, serving as a foundational mechanism for spatial reasoning in robotics and AR/VR. Existing methods achieve this by learning alignment only...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:56+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40495v1","title":"LucidLyra: Feed-Forward Generative 3D Scene Reconstruction and Joint Understanding via Multi-Task Distillation","abstract":"We present LucidLyra, a framework that makes generatively reconstructed 3D scenes directly queryable for semantics and instances in a feed-forward manner from video latents. While generative 3D reconstruction can synthesize virtual environments from a single image, downstream applications in robotics and physical AI require not only visual realism but also access to scene semantics and object identities. Existing 3D understanding approaches, however, require captured multi-view scenes, have limited generalization capabilities, or rely on expensive per-scene processing and optimization. To bridge this gap, our method distills complementary knowledge from a camera-controlled video diffusion model and frozen 2D vision foundation models into a unified model to jointly infer 3D Gaussians encoding geometry, appearance, open-vocabulary semantics, and class-agnostic instance cues. The resulting scenes support novel-view rendering, open-vocabulary segmentation, and object grouping without per-scene optimization, while training requires neither manual 3D annotations nor captured multi-view data. Our method outperforms baselines combining state-of-the-art 3DGS segmentation approaches with generative 3D scene reconstruction on curated synthetic and real-image-conditioned benchmarks, while preserving reconstruction quality. Furthermore, our latent formulation naturally extends to monocular video, reconstructing structured dynamic 3D scenes across time. By turning renderable but unstructured outputs into queryable 3D worlds, LucidLyra enables downstream applications including 3D scene editing, 3D asset mining, and robotic simulation.","authors":["Oliver Hahn","Rodrigo Marcuzzi","Sangeetha Grama Srinivasan","Alessandro Burzio","Sherwin Bahmani","Jiahui Huang","Stefan Roth","Shengyu Huang","Qunjie Zhou","Laura Leal-Taixé"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:18:51Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:18:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40495v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40495v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40495v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","instance","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":113478,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, instance, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present LucidLyra, a framework that makes generatively reconstructed 3D scenes directly queryable for semantics and instances in a feed-forward manner from video latents. While generative 3D reconstruction can synthesize virtual environments from a single...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40480v1","title":"AVPS-360: A Spatial Audio-Visual Benchmark for Instance Segmentation and Tracking of Sound-Producing Persons","abstract":"Audio-visual instance segmentation (AVIS) segments and tracks individual sound-producing objects in videos. Existing benchmarks predominantly use monaural audio, which lacks inter-channel cues for spatially distinguishing concurrent sound sources. We introduce AVPS-360 (Audio-Visual Person Segmentation 360), a spatial audio-visual benchmark bridging AVIS and sound event localisation and detection (SELD). Built upon STARSS23, it pairs first-order Ambisonics audio with synchronised 360° video across 230 recordings totalling 10.5 hours. Recordings average 164.0 seconds. The dataset captures intermittent sound production in real-world interactions dominated by speech, alongside laughter, clapping, and footsteps. It provides 31,678 manually reviewed instance masks linked to source identities and sound-event labels. We also present a baseline that integrates spatial audio with visual features for frame-level segmentation and identity association across frames. Experiments on AVPS-360 show that existing AVIS methods struggle with detection and identity association in long recordings with intermittent sound production, while our baseline substantially improves performance in both aspects.","authors":["Yifu Sun","Parthasaarathy Sudarsanam","Tuomas Virtanen","Archontis Politis"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:12:04Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:12:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40480v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40480v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40480v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":28953,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Audio-visual instance segmentation (AVIS) segments and tracks individual sound-producing objects in videos. Existing benchmarks predominantly use monaural audio, which lacks inter-channel cues for spatially distinguishing concurrent sound sources. We introduc...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40478v1","title":"Accurate MRI-free PET Quantification and Reliability Assessment via CT-to-MRI Synthesis and Conformal Prediction","abstract":"Standardized uptake value ratio (SUVR) quantification of amyloid PET commonly relies on a subject-specific T1-weighted MRI, yet a usable MRI is not always available. We therefore combined CT-to-MRI synthesis with conformal prediction for MRI-free PET quantification with reliability assessment. A slice-consistent Brownian Bridge Diffusion Model was strengthened with multi-dataset training and data-source conditioning to enrich the anatomical prior learned from MRI, and with expanded multi-slice CT context to exploit the through-plane continuity of subject-specific anatomy in CT. Synthetic MRI was used for FreeSurfer-based anatomical parcellation and PET quantification, while deep-ensemble uncertainty was calibrated with conformal prediction to estimate a plausible range for the corresponding real MRI-based SUVR. On $^{11}$C-PiB PET from OASIS-3, the proposed method achieved the highest synthesis and parcellation fidelity among the compared methods. Mean cortical SUVR from the proposed synthetic MRI agreed closely with the real MRI-based reference (MAE $0.016 \\pm 0.015$, Pearson $r = 0.9987$) and yielded the same amyloid-positivity classification as the real MRI in all 87 test subjects at the prespecified cut-off. Conformal prediction achieved empirical coverage close to the prespecified levels, and at 90% target coverage, 85 of 87 subjects had prediction intervals that supported an unambiguous amyloid-positivity decision. Zero-shot evaluation on an independent external cohort showed that the proposed CT-to-MRI model could support PET quantification without retraining, although conformal calibration required target-cohort recalibration to recover the intended coverage. These findings support CT-to-MRI-based MRI-free PET quantification as a promising approach for providing accurate SUVR measurements and an explicit assessment of their reliability.","authors":["Jungwoo Seo","Seung Yun Choi","Kun Ho Lee","Seun Jeon","Byoung Seok Ye","Jiook Cha"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:11:19Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:11:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40478v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40478v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40478v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":142992,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Standardized uptake value ratio (SUVR) quantification of amyloid PET commonly relies on a subject-specific T1-weighted MRI, yet a usable MRI is not always available. We therefore combined CT-to-MRI synthesis with conformal prediction for MRI-free PET quantifi...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40467v1","title":"InfantNet: A Large Scale Dataset for Infant Body Pose and Shape Estimation","abstract":"Infant pose and shape estimation is essential for applications in childcare, developmental monitoring, and medical diagnosis. However, existing methods and datasets are largely designed for adults, and direct transfer to infants fails due to substantial differences in body proportions, articulation limits, and frequent self-occlusion. To address this gap, we introduce InfantNet, the largest real-image infant dataset to date, comprising 108,902 RGB images of infants aged 6-18 months. Each image is annotated with 2D keypoints, and a curated subset of 11,642 images additionally includes 3D pose and shape annotations with full SMIL parameters, verified through multi-view rendering and reprojection-error filtering. We use an iterative annotation pipeline to ensure high fidelity across both 2D and 3D labels. InfantNet establishes a large-scale, comprehensive benchmark for infant 2D keypoint detection and 3D pose-and-shape recovery. Baseline experiments demonstrate that state-of-the-art adult pose estimators do not generalize well to infants, whereas fine-tuning on InfantNet yields a consistent improvement. The gains are even more pronounced for 3D pose and shape estimation, where SMIL-based estimation demonstrates robust performance with accuracy comparable to results reported for SMPL-based models. By releasing the InfantNet dataset and benchmark, we provide a vital resource for advancing infant pose analysis and related healthcare applications.","authors":["Zhipeng Hui","Jiahao Wang","Xun Dong","Guofeng Zhang","Xingrui Wang","Jiawei Peng","Qihao Liu","Xiaoding Yuan","Yi Zhang","Junjie Oscar Yin","Wufei Ma","Qinfan Lyu","Sara N. Lupolt","Keeve Edward Nachman","Adam Kortylewski","Alan Yuille"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:06:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:06:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40467v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40467v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40467v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":73536,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Infant pose and shape estimation is essential for applications in childcare, developmental monitoring, and medical diagnosis. However, existing methods and datasets are largely designed for adults, and direct transfer to infants fails due to substantial diffe...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40455v1","title":"A Unified Benchmark for Aerial-Enhanced Autonomous Driving: The KITScenes Aerial Dataset","abstract":"Aerial imagery provides complementary spatial context for autonomous driving by exposing road structure beyond the immediate perception range of onboard sensors. Yet map construction from aerial imagery, aerial-assisted online HD mapping, and cross-view localization are typically studied on separate data and benchmarks. We introduce KITScenes Aerial, an extension of the KITScenes Multimodal dataset that augments synchronized onboard sensor data, vehicle poses, and rich Lanelet2 map annotations with spatially associated aerial imagery and higher-resolution neural representations, providing a common basis for a variety of research tasks. Our mapping evaluation focuses on all 53 KITScenes map classes across different model designs, spatial ranges, and aerial resolutions, while retaining compatibility with the widely used three-class setting. Representative baselines show that aerial context is particularly beneficial for fine-grained and extended-range mapping, more than doubling 53-class mAP at the larger mapping extent. Higher-resolution aerial imagery yields a further 4.3 mAP (+24 %) improvement, with gains extending to challenging map elements such as traffic lights. We additionally evaluate cross-view localization under geographically disjoint conditions, where an adapted fine-grained feature-matching baseline achieves a mean translation error of 5.13 m. KITScenes Aerial thus enables unified study of aerial and ground-level information across complementary mapping and localization tasks. Project page: https://kitscenes.com/aerial","authors":["Daniel Lengerer","Richard Schwarzkopf","Jonas Merkert","Fabian Immel","Rupert Polley","Frank Bieder","Jan-Hendrik Pauls","J. Marius Zöllner","Klaus Bogenberger","Christoph Stiller","Carsten Markgraf"],"published":"2026-10-29T17:01:30Z","updated":"2026-10-29T17:01:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40455v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40455v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40455v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":56309,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Aerial imagery provides complementary spatial context for autonomous driving by exposing road structure beyond the immediate perception range of onboard sensors. Yet map construction from aerial imagery, aerial-assisted online HD mapping, and cross-view local...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40432v1","title":"Soil Moisture Estimation in Precision Agriculture: A Knowledge Distillation Approach","abstract":"Accurate soil moisture estimation is critical for precision agriculture, influencing irrigation, fertilization, and harvesting strategies. Traditional methods primarily rely on meteorological and soil property data, while recent approaches incorporate visual modalities such as aerial and geospatial imagery. However, these high-resolution images are expensive and computationally demanding to analyze. In this work, we propose Soil Moisture Distillation (SMD), a Knowledge Distillation (KD)-based multimodal learning framework that leverages unimodal teachers to enhance multimodal soil moisture estimation. SMD surpasses not only traditional multimodal fusion techniques, including MIS-ME, but also outperforms unimodal models, demonstrating the benefits of guided unimodal knowledge transfer. Additionally, we conduct a comparative analysis of soil patches and crop images, showing that crop images provide superior visual cues, leading to improved prediction accuracy. SMD achieves a 2% improvement in MAPE for soil patches and 1.2% for crops over state-of-the-art baselines, highlighting the effectiveness of leveraging both meteorological and visual data through knowledge distillation for enhanced soil moisture forecasting.","authors":["Mohammed Rakib","Vrinda Nagula","Arunkumar Bagavathi"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:53:56Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:53:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40432v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40432v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40432v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":1317,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Accurate soil moisture estimation is critical for precision agriculture, influencing irrigation, fertilization, and harvesting strategies. Traditional methods primarily rely on meteorological and soil property data, while recent approaches incorporate visual...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"abstract","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:57+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40401v1","title":"Continuous-Action World Models: Learning Cross-Embodiment Spatiotemporal Dynamics Priors","abstract":"We introduce Continuous-Action World Models (CoAWM), a framework for learning cross-embodiment spatiotemporal dynamics priors that support robot action generation across embodiments and execution frequencies. At its core is a shared latent function space that captures the spatial and temporal structure of robot actions. We learn this space through two stages. First, function pretraining brings trajectories from different embodiments and sampling frequencies into a common representation, learning shared dynamics priors through action reconstruction and consistency across temporal sampling views. By accommodating heterogeneous robot trajectories, this stage broadens the range of data sources available for pretraining and facilitates scaling to larger and more diverse datasets. Second, we connect the pretrained function space to a world-model backbone, enabling observations, language instructions, and the current robot state to condition the prediction of a latent action function. A single decoder takes any query time and returns the corresponding end-effector action. By separating shared dynamics priors from task-conditioned action selection and temporal sampling, our approach provides a unified foundation for transferring robot experience across embodiments and control frequencies.","authors":["Liangyu Li","Qingwen Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:34:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:34:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40401v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40401v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40401v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":48924,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We introduce Continuous-Action World Models (CoAWM), a framework for learning cross-embodiment spatiotemporal dynamics priors that support robot action generation across embodiments and execution frequencies. At its core is a shared latent function space that...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:58+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40373v1","title":"HiFOR: Hierarchical Foresight with Action-Guided World Grounding for Vision-Language-Action Model","abstract":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have demonstrated promising capabilities in robotic manipulation by integrating visual perception, language understanding, and action generation. Recent advances have further incorporated future prediction to enhance the understanding of environmental dynamics and task progression. However, existing methods often model short-term dynamics and long-term task objectives separately, while directly incorporating predicted futures into action policies without sufficiently considering their relevance to current action requirements. To address these limitations, we propose HiFOR, a hierarchical foresight framework with action-guided world retrieval. Specifically, Hierarchical Foresight Prediction jointly predicts short-term state evolution, semantic milestones, and final goal states through a Hierarchical Dependency Mask (HDM), establishing dependencies across temporal horizons. Building upon these predictions, Structure-Aware Latent World Modeling organizes future latent representations along the horizon, view, and spatial dimensions to facilitate fine-grained retrieval. Furthermore, Action-Guided Factorized World Retrieval adaptively retrieves relevant future information from the structured latent world based on evolving action representations during denoising, providing targeted guidance for action generation. Extensive experiments on two simulation benchmarks and six real-world robotic manipulation tasks demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach.","authors":["Rongxing Ding","Hongyu Qu","Kehong Wu","Xudong Wang","Fan Zhang","Xinguang Xiang","Xiangbo Shu"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:21:06Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:21:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40373v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40373v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40373v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":55936,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have demonstrated promising capabilities in robotic manipulation by integrating visual perception, language understanding, and action generation. Recent advances have further incorporated future prediction to enhance the un...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:58+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40339v1","title":"Wizard: Manipulate Any World in Real Time via Agentic Control","abstract":"Recent advances in video world models have enabled the generation of increasingly realistic and interactive virtual environments, opening new possibilities for player-customizable gameplay. Agentic frameworks further advance this direction by decoupling explicit game logic and controllable world representations from visual generation. However, existing approaches support only simple game settings and predefined motion patterns, generalize poorly to unseen environments and characters, and offer limited real-time interactivity. In this paper, we introduce Wizard, an agentic game world model that enables real-time, precise control over character motions while generalizing zero-shot across novel scenes, characters, and actions. To achieve this, we construct a diverse dataset spanning 750 scenes and 178 characters, incorporating skeleton and geometry information as structured representations. We leverage these representations as intermediate proxies to condition a video generation model, together with an adaptive masking mechanism that improves alignment between the conditioning signals and generated video rollouts. Furthermore, we introduce an agentic framework that explicitly maintains and updates game states, allowing players to freely configure their virtual worlds and exercise fine-grained control over character motions while preserving visual quality and gameplay consistency. By combining agentic control with structured proxy-based generation, our framework unlocks new possibilities for player-driven game creation with real-time, generalizable, and mechanically consistent world simulation.","authors":["Yuzhe Wu","Zijun Lin","Yeying Jin"],"published":"2026-10-29T16:03:19Z","updated":"2026-10-29T16:03:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40339v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40339v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40339v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40333v1","title":"RFW-A: Agentic Procedural Generation of 400K Articulated Assets over 500 Categories","abstract":"High-quality articulated assets are essential for embodied perception, simulation, and manipulation, yet constructing them at scale remains difficult. Manual annotation and procedural modeling require substantial human effort. Recent agentic systems automate asset construction through program synthesis, but repeat costly reasoning and refinement for every new asset. Our key observation is that assets within a category can share construction logic despite variations in structure and appearance. Agentic synthesis and procedural generation are therefore complementary: agents can construct reusable programs, while procedural execution reuses the resulting logic across many samples. We therefore present RFW-A, which combines the two in a new articulated-asset generation paradigm. Starting from existing articulated programs, RFW-A organizes reusable components and their dependencies into category-level generators. Agents test and refine these generators across sampled configurations and motion states. Once constructed and validated, the generators produce diverse assets through procedural execution with no further model calls or token charges. The estimated API cost of generator authoring averages $10.62, amortizing to $0.05 per asset over 200 assets, versus $1.24 for the sampled per-asset agentic synthesis baseline. Using RFW-A, we built RFW-A-400K, containing over 400K sim-ready articulated assets across more than 500 categories. Experiments show high structural and kinematic validity and stable execution across physics simulators. Beyond asset validity, training with RFW-A-400K improves part-level articulation estimation on seen and unseen categories, while policies trained in simulation with retrieved and program-edited assets transfer zero-shot to real articulated objects.","authors":["Yuanbo Lyu","Changjian Pan","Saining Zhang","Yi Yang","Peishuo Li","Zhiheng Li","Jialu Liu","Junhao Chen","Jiawei Weng","Yupeng Gao","Weiqing Xiao","Wenyi Li","Jiarui Guo","Rui Zhang","Jingrun Du","Weiyi Lin","Zenan Meng","Hu Wei","Lin Qu","Zongzheng Zhang","Hao Zhao","Chaojian Li"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:58:15Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:58:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40333v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40333v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40333v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40327v1","title":"Radiology Guideline-derived Decision Graph for Efficient Clinical Decision Support","abstract":"Radiological risk stratification and management planning are essential downstream tasks in medical imaging. Automating these tasks is challenging because clinical guideline criteria are distributed across prose, tables, and cross-referenced definitions. We present Guideline-derived Decision Graph (GDGraph), a framework that decouples guideline compilation from case evaluation. During compilation, a large language model partitions guidelines into section-based chunks, extracts local decision subgraphs with linked definitions, and aggregates related branches into an executable decision graph. During inference, a fast System One model answers localized clinical questions while deterministic traversal resolves Boolean logic, turning opaque single-pass predictions into a traceable decision path without task-specific training. Across 347 cases spanning renal cyst, adnexal mass, and thyroid nodule guidelines, GDGraph improves System One accuracy from 57.3% to 90.8%, outperforming retrieval-augmented GPT-4o (80.1%) and graph-guided GPT-4o with chain-of-thought (82.7%) at substantially lower latency and cost. The source code will be available.","authors":["Xuewen Zhou","Zhen Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:56:27Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:56:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40327v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40327v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40327v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40309v1","title":"Textual Response-Aware Guidance for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) exhibit strong zero-shot generalization but remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks. Existing adversarial fine-tuning methods often use pre-trained model guidance to preserve image--text alignment while improving robustness. Our analysis of visual representation drift reveals complementary limitations of two representative forms of guidance. Matching visual features provides broad coverage by constraining all directions equally, but lacks semantic emphasis. Matching prediction distributions prioritizes semantically relevant directions, but remains limited by the candidate texts and prediction probabilities. These limitations can hinder preservation of pre-trained image--text relationships and degrade zero-shot performance. To address these issues, we propose Textual Response-Aware Guidance (TRAG), which constrains visual representation drift using the pre-trained text encoder's responses to local variations in input embeddings. Specifically, TRAG constructs anchor-level constraints from the response directions and magnitudes around semantic anchors, then aggregates them using pre-trained image--anchor relevance, yielding an image-adaptive constraint. Extensive experiments across architectures and tasks demonstrate state-of-the-art clean and robust performance. Further analysis shows that TRAG helps preserve pre-trained image--text relationships.","authors":["Yubo Cui","Xianchao Guan","Zheng Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:48:43Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:48:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40309v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40309v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40309v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40284v1","title":"LoRe-MIM: Lost-or-Replaced Masked Image Modeling for Self-Supervised Representation Learning in Whole-Body PET/CT","abstract":"Deep learning models for whole-body positron emission tomography/computed tomography (PET/CT) hold significant clinical potential for disease detection, treatment planning, and response assessment. However, the time and cost associated with expert annotations remain major bottlenecks in developing robust and generalizable models. Although self-supervised learning (SSL) has emerged as a promising approach to mitigating annotation scarcity, its application to whole-body PET/CT remains relatively underexplored due to the limited availability of large-scale datasets and the challenges associated with learning complementary cross-modal representations. To address these limitations, we curate a heterogeneous, multi-tracer, multi-institutional cohort comprising 6,531 whole-body PET/CT scans and propose LoRe-MIM (Lost-Or-Replaced Masked Image Modelling), a novel self-supervised framework designed to learn multimodal representations through masked image reconstruction. Specifically, LoRe-MIM introduces a masking strategy in which image regions are either replaced with zeros (Lost) or substituted with corresponding complementary imaging modality (Replaced), encouraging the model to capture both modality-specific characteristics and cross-modal relationships. We evaluate the learned representations across multiple downstream tasks, including multimodal PET/CT image reconstruction, patch-based classification into cancer vs. non-cancer, whole-body lesion segmentation, label-efficient training, and robustness assessment. Experimental results demonstrate that LoRe-MIM consistently outperforms the corresponding state-of-the-art baselines across these tasks, highlighting its potential as a scalable and generalizable self-supervised learning framework for whole-body PET/CT analysis.","authors":["Amartya Bhattacharya","Bashirul Azam Biswas","Biratal Raj Wagle","Michael S. Leapman","Matthew E. Maeder","James B. Yu","Indrani Bhattacharya"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:36:15Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:36:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40284v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40284v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40284v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40276v1","title":"TaskFrame: Connecting Task-Level Planning with Frame-Level Control for Game Agents","abstract":"Building general game agents requires both high-level task reasoning and low-latency action control in rapidly changing visual environments. These capabilities favor models with substantially different computational profiles, making them difficult to realize within a single model. In this work, we present TaskFrame, a hierarchical planner-controller framework that connects task-level planning with frame-level keyboard-and-mouse control. A vision-language task planner decomposes a high-level goal into an ordered sequence of atomic instructions and is optimized through supervised learning and dependency-aware GRPO. The hidden states of special goal tokens that terminate each atomic instruction provide continuous goal embeddings that are cached and reused across control steps. A causal Transformer controller combines the embedding of the active goal with recent observations to predict discrete keyboard and mouse-button actions and continuous camera motion at every game step. We train the system in three stages: planner training, controller pretraining, and joint alignment. Experiments in Minecraft show that TaskFrame is competitive with general-purpose vision-language models on instruction decomposition, and that in closed-loop evaluation the agent achieves a higher combat success rate than the evaluated baselines.","authors":["Guangzhi Wang","Junfu Pu","Yiran Wang","Xingyilang Yin","Honghao Chen","Cheng Cheng","Lingen Li","Yinan Zhou","Jiarui Wu","Yinuo Jiang","Hongbo Jin","Xiaodong Cun","Ying Shan"],"published":"2026-10-29T15:31:37Z","updated":"2026-10-29T15:31:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40276v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40276v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40276v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40212v1","title":"Benchmarking color judgment in multimodal LLMs with CIE-computable ground truth","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are now used as zero-shot judges of image quality, but their color judgments are rarely tested with colorimetric ground truth. We present a five-task benchmark based on the CIE colorimetric framework: AWB pair judgment, skin-tone bias direction, CCT estimation, CIE Delta E 2000 pair ordering, and color naming under changing illuminants. We test eight MLLM endpoints, including four Qwen3-VL variants, GLM-4.5V, GPT-5.4, Claude-Opus-4.7, and Gemini-3.1-Pro. The models often solve coarse binary AWB judgments, but they remain weak on numeric CCT estimation and on some fine color-difference judgments. On ColorChecker-24 stimuli, the three closed-weight frontier models reach CCT MAE of 715-1056 K, compared with 1358-2133 K for the five open-weight models. A non-ColorChecker control shows that this advantage is partly template-dependent. The results suggest that current MLLMs have useful color words and coarse visual priors, but their color judgments are not reliably calibrated to CIE-computable color differences.","authors":["Shuwei Yue","Kaida Xiao","Zhihua Wang","Qiao Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:58:08Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:58:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40212v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40212v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40212v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40199v1","title":"Decision-Level Error Attribution and Depth-Aware Safety Evaluation of Monocular RGB Human Tracking for Autonomous Robot Navigation","abstract":"Monocular RGB tracking is useful for robots operating near people, but perception errors affect binary stop/proceed decisions in ways that geometric accuracy metrics do not capture. We examine how much decision error arises from the safety rule versus perception. We prove that lateral-only distance is a lower bound on planar distance: with accurate positions, it cannot miss dangers identified by the planar rule, but it produces unnecessary stops. We introduce a two-factor Shapley attribution separating rule and perception contributions, and further distinguish missed detections from localization errors. Experiments use ETH and UCY pedestrian videos with simulated stationary robots, disjoint calibration and evaluation pedestrians, and time-block bootstrap intervals. Across 898 pedestrians in three scenes, the lateral rule missed no dangers on annotated positions but achieved only 5.6-31.9% stop precision. In two scenes with RGB perception, calibrated ground-plane localization achieved 0.59 m and 0.51 m planar RMSE, compared with 1.72 m and 1.39 m for a geometric baseline and 0.81 m and 0.68 m for calibrated Depth Anything V2. Detection recall of 0.43 and 0.26 made missed detections the leading source of missed danger. A two-standard-deviation safety margin with ground-plane localization matched or improved on the lateral rule's missed-danger rate in all eight scene-threshold settings and reduced unnecessary stops in seven. All robot interactions are simulated, and outcomes are offline decision classifications, not demonstrations of collision avoidance.","authors":["Abdulahi Taiwo","Chijioke Cyriacus Ekechi"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:52:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:52:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40199v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40199v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40199v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40191v1","title":"Video Generation Meets Agent Harness: Agentic Video Generation, Agentic World Modeling and Recursive Self-Improvement","abstract":"Video generation is shifting from end-to-end synthesis toward agentic generation, where planning, tool use, and feedback enable coherent, controllable multi-shot videos. In parallel, video world models provide action-conditioned observations and predicted futures, serving as interactive environments for agents. We study their intersection through the agent harness: an executable runtime that constructs context, exposes tools, maintains state, routes feedback, and manages budgets around video models. This report examines three directions. First, agentic video generation, where harnesses coordinate scripting, shot planning, asset management, production, and editing, alongside the evaluation challenges of these systems. Second, agentic world modeling, where harnesses mediate observation, control, execution, and state. We discuss hybrid designs in which game engines or agent-written programs maintain world state while video models render observations, and how agents and world models can mutually evaluate and improve one another. Third, recursive self-improvement (RSI) in video model development, distinguishing output revisions, updates to harness state, generator parameters, and evaluators, and changes to the improvement procedure itself. We identify how these improvements feed back into subsequent development. Across these directions, we analyze essential agent capabilities, including audiovisual perception, long-horizon planning, memory, code-based interaction, feedback-guided control, skill reuse, and multi-agent coordination. We conclude with challenges in autonomous video production, lifelong memory, programmable hybrid control, autonomous model development, provenance, cost accounting, and evaluation linking video quality and task performance to sustained improvements in models and harnesses.","authors":["Liaoyuan Fan","Yuhao Zheng","Dongxing Mao","Hangyu Ran","Siyuan Hu","Weijia Mao","Rui Zhao","Weijia Wu","Guian Fang","Yanzhe Chen","Zeyu Zhu","Yuxin Jiang","Yiren Song","Yuchao Gu","Junchao Yi","Qi Hu","Dan Kong","Alex Jinpeng Wang","Kevin Qinghong Lin","Mike Zheng Shou","Wei Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:47:39Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:47:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40191v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40191v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40191v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40190v1","title":"AdMuse: Skill-Guided Creative Advertising Image Generation","abstract":"An outstanding creative advertising image should both attract attention and communicate a product's positioning. How to choose a persuasive conceptual bridge before rendering begins is challenging for advertising generation. We present AdMuse, a skill-guided multi-agent framework that distills high-quality advertisements into reusable creative skills for cross-domain advertising strategy. Rather than retrieving isolated exemplars, AdMuse abstracts recurring bridge patterns into a skill library that can guide new product briefs. At generation time, AdMuse coordinates skill routing, multi-candidate ideation and selection, and VLM-guided visual refinement: diverse skills propose alternative concepts, relative judgment selects the most promising direction, and vision-language feedback helps realize the selected intent in the final image. This design treats advertising creativity as a structured decision process rather than a single prompt-writing step. Experiments across diverse product and brand positionings show that AdMuse produces visually compelling advertisements that faithfully communicate product positioning.","authors":["Minyan Luo","Yuxin Zhang","Meng Zhao","Xincan Wang","Fuzhang Wu","Tong-Yee Lee","Oliver Deussen","Fan Tang","Weiming Dong"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:47:22Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:47:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40190v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40190v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40190v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40166v1","title":"EventSeeColor: Learning to Reconstruct Color from Monochrome Events","abstract":"Event cameras capture visual brightness changes under challenging lighting conditions. However, traditional event-based image reconstruction targets grayscale intensity and produces images without scene color. Color distinguishes surfaces with similar intensity. Reconstructing color from events can reveal appearance differences that reconstructed grayscale images cannot express. Although monochrome event cameras do not directly measure color, their event-triggering patterns depend on surface reflectance, illumination, and the sensor's spectral response. These patterns may contain cues that support color reconstruction. In this paper, we present EventSeeColor, which uses event-reconstructed grayscale and aligned events to jointly guide a diffusion colorizer. Grayscale-derived semantic context supports color inference, while region-balanced chroma supervision and local RGB refinement improve color distribution, brightness, and texture. In the combined evaluation on two real event datasets, EventSeeColor achieves the best structural similarity, learned perceptual scores, and chromatic-region color difference among the external methods.","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Langyi Chen","Haoxian Zhou","Zelin Zhang","Haodong Chen","Qiang Qu","Xiaoming Chen","Weidong Cai"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:38:48Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:38:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40166v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40166v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40166v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40153v1","title":"Hierarchical Step Decomposition for Training-free Online Video Step Grounding","abstract":"Given a set of procedural steps, Video Step Grounding (VSG) aims to identify which steps occur in a video and localize when they are performed. Existing methods are either offline, requiring access to the full video before making predictions, or online, but relying on fixed-length video segments producing noisy, over-segmented outputs. In this work, we propose Hierarchical Step Decomposition (HSD), a training-free framework for online VSG that leverages the semantic understanding capabilities of Large Multimodal Models. HSD addresses two central challenges in online grounding: constructing semantically coherent temporal segments from a streaming video, and bridging the semantic gap between coarse procedural step descriptions and visual observations. To construct elementary temporal units, HSD incrementally groups incoming segments into semantically consistent events, using an anchor caption that is continuously validated against the visual stream. Based on these events, we perform zero-shot procedural step classification and selectively refine uncertain predictions by decomposing candidate steps into finegrained substeps that capture more nuanced visual evidence. By operating on semantically coherent events rather than every fixed-length segment, HSD reduces redundant computation and produces more stable predictions. Experiments on HT-Step, Ego4D Goal-Step, and CrossTask show that HSD substantially outperforms state-of-the-art methods, overall achieving better step recognition, more accurate temporal localization, and faster inference.","authors":["Long Zhou","Luca Zanella","Paolo Rota","Massimiliano Mancini","Elisa Ricci"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:35:33Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:35:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40153v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40153v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40153v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40137v1","title":"NeurEO: dissecting Earth observation embeddings with computational neuroscience","abstract":"Earth embeddings are compressed representations of rich Earth observation data that enable downstream models to tackle a wide range of complex geospatial tasks. Yet, this increase in performance comes at the cost of decreased interpretability, as the semantic meaning of Earth embeddings is unknown. To address this challenge, we borrow methods from the field of computational neuroscience, which aim to interpret activity in (biological) neurons. Here, we adapt and apply these methods to assign meaning to AlphaEarth embeddings. We analyse embedding features both as stand-alone data, equivalent to spontaneous neural activity, and in relation to land cover and elevation correlates, equivalent to stimulus-response neural activity. We demonstrate correlational and multi-scale structure in the embedding features. Embedding features can display highly specific and directional tuning to land cover and elevation, such as a `North vs South woodland edge' response. When we remove the main trends of combined land cover responses (like water versus land tuning), we discover fine-grained representational details with increased effective dimensionality. Together, our analyses start to unravel what these Earth embeddings represent, which is critical for developing trustworthy downstream applications.","authors":["Thijs L van der Plas","Jacob JW Bakermans","Ioannis N Athanasiadis"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:28:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:28:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40137v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40137v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40137v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40131v1","title":"VideoMemBench: How Well Do MLLMs Remember in Video Conversations?","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can now process hour-long videos, making extended multi-turn dialogue over a single video a natural mode of use. Sustaining such a dialogue requires more than answering each question in isolation: the model must retrieve and reuse information established earlier in the conversation, both what it has seen and what it has said, an ability we term in-context video memory. Existing video QA benchmarks leave this ability unmeasured: their questions are mutually independent, never reference one another, and each is answerable from the video alone, so nothing ever requires carrying information across turns. We introduce VideoMemBench, in which each sample pairs a single video with a long multi-turn dialogue, whose turns are connected by typed cross-turn dependencies: an Episodic Dependency refers back to a visual episode described in an earlier turn, probing memory of what the model has seen, while an Answer Dependency builds on the model's own earlier answer, probing memory of what the model has said. Every dependency is verified considering two properties: 1) necessity, meaning the question cannot be answered without its earlier turns, and 2) non-leakage, meaning the question does not restate the information from its earlier turns. To enforce these properties, we design a construction pipeline with three LLM roles: a planner, a generator, and a judger, with each dialogue reviewed by human experts. VideoMemBench comprises 326 videos with 2,476 turns, of which 1,387 depend on earlier turns, including multi-parent dependencies and long-range call-back. Among over 20 evaluated open- and closed-source MLLMs, Claude Opus 5 performs the best with an overall score of only 45.7\\%, highlighting the challenge of in-context video memory. Our dataset and evaluation are released.","authors":["Zhengdong Hu","Chao Wang","Kaituo Feng","Huanjin Yao","Mingfei Lu","Hehe Fan","Yi Yang"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:22:51Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:22:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40131v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40131v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40131v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40124v1","title":"It Takes Two to Probe, Thousands to Retrieve: Putting Vision--Language Probes to the Retrieval Test","abstract":"Vision--language models (VLMs) are tested for fine-grained understanding with image--text matching probes, a model must prefer the correct image or caption over handcrafted negative. In retrieval, the correct item must beat thousands of alternatives. We measure how these two evaluation methods relate by using two probing benchmarks, \\svo{} and SugarCrepe, for retrieval tasks. For 100 image and caption queries we judge the top-10 retrievals from full dataset of six VLMs, with human annotators and an LLM assessor. We than compare how well models rank pool of items on the same queries, while changing only where the negatives comes from: the benchmark, a random draw, or the judged-incorrect retrievals of other models. On \\svo{} the accuracy of retrieving a positive over handcrafted negatives is significantly higher than for retrieved ones (the correct item is preferred in 85\\% against 77\\% of the time). Using pool of ten retrieved negatives leads to same accuracy as when one uses pool of 200 to 430 random negatives. On SugarCrepe the order is reversed: its handcrafted negatives are harder than the retrieved ones. Incomplete relevance labels explain most of the low retrieval scores on \\svo{}, where the top-ranked item is correct for 54--90\\% of the queries under human labels against 6--21\\% under the benchmark's labels. However, we detect no association between passing the probe for a query and retrieving a correct item first. The LLM assessor o3 agrees with individual annotators ($κ$ 0.57--0.61) slightly less than they agree with each other (0.64--0.68).","authors":["Ivana Beňová","Michal Gregor","Marián Šimko"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:20:31Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:20:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40124v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40124v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40124v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40121v1","title":"Learning a Motion Manifold to Constrain Ambiguity in Unsupervised 4D Medical Image Interpolation","abstract":"Unsupervised 4D medical image interpolation reconstructs intermediate anatomical states from temporally sparse observations without intermediate supervision. Distinct motion fields may attain comparable endpoint agreement while producing different intermediate anatomy, giving rise to motion ambiguity. This ambiguity is difficult to constrain when many independently adjustable motion variables must be estimated from the observed endpoints. We develop a framework that learns a motion manifold to restrict candidate motion fields while retaining sample-specific variation. Cross-Sample Spectral Basis Learning extracts recurring motion structure from endpoint-derived dense estimates and encodes it in a shared spectral basis. Sample-Conditioned Cayley Basis Reorientation uses predicted motion coordinates to rotate the effective motion directions for each endpoint pair without increasing the number of independent coordinates. Their composition defines a nonlinear family of complete velocity fields across endpoint pairs, accommodating anatomical and motion-state differences within a restricted representation. Integrating each predicted field supports bidirectional reconstruction at arbitrary intermediate times. Theoretical analysis establishes sufficient conditions under which endpoint observations distinguish nearby motions within the learned family. Experiments on ACDC and 4D-Lung demonstrate state-of-the-art performance among the evaluated methods trained without intermediate volumes and competitive results against methods using intermediate supervision.","authors":["Haojin Li","Huazhu Fu","Heng Li","Jiang Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:17:16Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:17:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40121v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40121v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40121v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40119v1","title":"SynthFaces: Balanced Large Scale Human Dataset","abstract":"The pursuit of fair and equitable AI in human-centric tasks is critically hindered by the inherent biases and privacy risks of real-world datasets, which offer imbalanced representations of identity and often contain sensitive personal information. Consequently, state-of-the-art generative models trained on this skewed data exhibit unfair performance, particularly in identity-preserving generation. In this work we propose a pipeline for controllable generation of dataset with diverse faces for generative models training and finetuning. We achieve this by expanding foundational Arc2Face diffusion model with a flow matching generative model in the manifold of identity embeddings. We demonstrate that such approach achieves both goals of generative fidelity and label diversity not present in real human-centric datasets. As a result we collect and further contextualize a highly balanced identity-centric dataset of humans named SynthFaces. By design, our released images and identities are synthetic, reducing direct reliance on real personal images; we empirically check identity separation with a nearest-neighbor non-leakage metric. We purpose it for training and tuning of fair models for various downstream tasks, and showcase its usability in finetuning an existing adapter-based method for personalized generation.","authors":["Sergey Karpukhin","Denis Bobkov","Kirill Morozov","Aibek Alanov"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:17:06Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:17:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40119v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40119v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40119v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40115v1","title":"SelfThinker: Self-Evolving Reasoning via Reliability-Verified Intrinsic Visual Thoughts","abstract":"Unified multimodal models can both generate and understand images within a single set of weights, offering an opportunity to use image synthesis as training supervision for visual comprehension. However, existing visual chain-of-thought methods rely on human-annotated helper images during training and require slow image generation at test time. We present SelfThinker, the first self-evolving framework that turns a unified model's self-generated visual thoughts into stronger understanding without human image annotations. Given a visual problem, SelfThinker samples candidate helper images from its generation pathway and uses its frozen understanding pathway to score each candidate via conditional pointwise mutual information. High-utility visual thoughts then refine the generator via flow matching and serve as privileged teacher context for on-policy self-distillation. At inference, SelfThinker reasons directly from the original image in text without generating intermediate images. Although trained only on small synthetic visual-search and navigation datasets, SelfThinker transfers broadly to natural-image and spatial benchmarks, raising the seven-benchmark average of ThinkMorph-7B from 52.7 to 64.0 (+11.3 points). Controlled image-perturbation probes further show that SelfThinker surpasses ThinkMorph-7B by up to 40.4 points and its pretrained BAGEL-7B backbone by up to 23.2 points across fine-grained and spatial perception tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/zhangquanchen/SelfThinker.","authors":["Zhangquan Chen","Ruqi Huang"],"published":"2026-10-29T14:14:16Z","updated":"2026-10-29T14:14:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40115v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40115v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40115v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40094v1","title":"RepConTUR: A Report-Conditioned Volumetric Multimodal Model for Abdominal Tumor Segmentation","abstract":"Tumor delineation is required for radiotherapy planning, yet automatic segmentation is limited by errors, predominantly false positives, that distort dose and increase editing time. The radiology report accompanies the scan and localizes disease at the suborgan level, but existing text-promptable models condition on short referring phrases, and most operate in 2D. We present RepConTUR, a single native-3D early-fusion multimodal model that conditions volumetric abdominal tumor segmentation on the narrative report. We extend AbdomenAtlas 3.0 to a suborgan-level promptable segmentation dataset and generate report-based VQA pairs, yielding four report-derived tasks on the same annotations: referring segmentation (RES), report-grounded segmentation (GSEG), report generation (RRG), and visual question answering (VQA). We train RepConTUR, comprising a 3D CT encoder, an early-fusion language model, and a native-3D segmentation module, jointly on the four tasks. Across liver, kidney and pancreas, RepConTUR attains the highest Dice similarity coefficient (DSC), instance-wise precision, and detection F1 among the text-promptable baselines, and adding the report raises mean precision while leaving recall comparable. Against two vision-only specialists, nnU-Net and the report-supervised R-Super, RepConTUR attains the highest value on every in-domain metric for liver and pancreas and the highest mean DSC, recall, precision, and detection F1 over the four out-of-distribution public cohorts. Ablations indicate that conditioning on the narrative report raises detection F1 from 0.499 to 0.528 within the same model, that joint GSEG training raises RES F1 from 0.450 to 0.482, and that the native-3D segmentation module raises GSEG F1 from 0.139 to 0.528 over its 2D counterpart. Code is available at https://github.com/Oncosoft-Research/RepConTUR.","authors":["Seunghwan Lee","Sangjoon Park","Jongmin Lee","Yoon Jo Kim","Wonyoung Cho","Jin Sung Kim","Han Joo Chae"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:59:44Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:59:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40094v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40094v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40094v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","instance","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40085v1","title":"Morphology-Aware Channel-Decoupled Mamba Network for Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Accurate multi-organ segmentation in computed tomography (CT) is essential for computer-aided diagnosis, treatment planning, and disease monitoring. However, substantial variations in organ shape and size, together with low tissue contrast and ambiguous boundaries, complicate the modeling of heterogeneous anatomical structures. To address these challenges, we propose a Morphology-Aware Channel-Decoupled Mamba Network (MCDNet), which integrates adaptive local structural enhancement with channel-decoupled long-range modeling. Specifically, we introduce a Morphology-Aware Channel-Decoupled State-Space (MCSS) block that divides feature channels into separate processing groups, allowing differentiated structural representations to be learned within an efficient state-space framework. Within each group, a Morphology-Aware Enhancement Module (MAEM) combines deformable convolutions with channel attention to adapt local feature extraction to anatomical shape variations. Group-wise state-space modeling then captures long-range spatial dependencies, and channel gating adaptively integrates the resulting features. Experiments on the Synapse abdominal CT dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of MCDNet, achieving an average Dice Similarity Coefficient of 81.61% for multi-organ segmentation.","authors":["Chenchen Wang","You Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:55:02Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:55:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40085v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40085v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40085v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40073v1","title":"Uncertainty-aware Coarse-to-Fine Main Pancreatic Duct Segmentation for Width Measurement in 3D MRCP","abstract":"Assessment of the main pancreatic duct (MPD) from volumetric magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) scans is an essential task for ductal dilatation diagnosis and pancreatic cancer analysis. However, manual assessment is affected by reader variability in which automated analysis may assist. Nevertheless, fully automated and uncertainty-aware MPD analysis from volumetric MRCP remains underexplored area of research. In this study, we propose an automatic and uncertainty-aware deep learning-based pipeline for MPD segmentation and maximum-width analysis from MRCP scans. A coarse localisation model first identifies the ductal region-of-interest, followed by a fine model that segments the MPD within the region. The dataset consists of 256 MPD-segmented MRCP scans from 69 patients. A randomly selected holdout test subset of 14 patients with 55 MRCP volumes was used for evaluation. We employed a five-model cross-validation ensemble for improved performance and uncertainty estimation. The best MPD segmentation performance was 0.634 DSC, 0.744 precision, and 0.593 recall. The mean absolute error for maximum MPD width prediction was 0.355~mm and dilated MPD ($\\geq 5$~mm) was detected with 75.0\\% sensitivity and 74.4\\% specificity. These novel results suggest that automated MPD segmentation can be used to aid MRCP-based duct measurements.","authors":["Zipei Zhang","Jaakko Sahlsten","Mikko Saukkoriipi","Johanna Ronkainen","Mona Lehto","Akseli Aittoniemi","Irina Rinta-Kiikka","Kimmo Kaski"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:51:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:51:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40073v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40073v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40073v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40068v1","title":"BRACHIO: Broad Representations Across Centres for Head-and-neck Imaging Oncology (HECKTOR 2026)","abstract":"The HECKTOR 2026 challenge benchmarks machine learning for head and neck cancer, where teams delineate primary and nodal tumour volumes, perform TN staging, and predict recurrence-free survival (RFS). This report describes the submissions for the HECKTOR challenge of team nucli. As the three tasks span both dense and global information from multicentric PET/CT scans, we utilise self-supervised pre-trained encoders, training a 2D DINOv2 encoder (PET-DINOv2-MIPs) on HECKTOR 2026's PET scans. Our final test submission ensembles a DynUNet trained from scratch with a SwinUNETR whose Swin trunk was initialised from a SimMIM-pre-trained checkpoint for segmentation, reaching a mean Dice of 0.5409. For TN staging, GTVp/GTVn geometric features from the segmentation masks are fit to stage probabilities by logistic regression (LR) and fused with PET-DINOv2-MIPs deep features (PCA + LR) via a second-level LR, reaching balanced accuracies of 0.3962 (T-stage) and 0.6432 (N-stage). For the RFS prognosis task, we fine-tune the last two blocks of the PET-DINOv2-MIPs ViT-S encoder jointly with an MLP head under a CoxPH loss, concatenating clinical covariates with the imaging features, reaching a test Harrell's C-index of 0.6577 and a weighted challenge score of 0.5802. The prognosis result is comparable to the best entry of the previous edition and consistent with our internal cross-validation, while our segmentation result is well below it and limits the staging results that depend on it.","authors":["Roxanne De Maeyer","Ine Dirks","Yannick Brackenier","Simon DeKeyser","Jef Vandemeulebroucke","Vicky De Ridder"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:50:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:50:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40068v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40068v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40068v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40061v1","title":"Localizing Concepts in Visual Autoregressive Models","abstract":"Understanding the internal routing of visual concepts is crucial for the safe and controllable adaptation of generative models. While concept localization has been widely studied in diffusion models, the emerging paradigm of next-scale visual autoregressive models remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we introduce LoCo, the first model-agnostic framework to localize where and when specific concepts emerge within autoregressive models. Driven by the native coarse-to-fine nature of next-scale generation, our method precisely maps conceptual knowledge across three distinct dimensions: Layer, Scale, and Position. To systematically localize and evaluate concept routing without context bias, we propose LoCoBench, a comprehensive dataset spanning 10 diverse categories. Extensive probing on image and video autoregressive models like Infinity, HunyuanImage-3.0, and InfinityStar shows that the localized positions are both interpretable and causally related to concept emergence. Building on these insights, we apply our localization method to three key applications: concept erasure, model personalization, and adversarial concept injection. Experiments demonstrate that our targeted intervention achieves state-of-the-art performance, substantially reducing computational overhead while preserving benign utility. Overall, our findings offer insights into how conceptual knowledge is routed during autoregressive generation, introducing a practical pathway for more interpretable, efficient, and secure adaptation.","authors":["Nanxiang Jiang","Yang Liu","Yuanhao Wang","Min Xu"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:49:01Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:49:01Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40061v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40061v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40061v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40046v1","title":"EPC-RawDC: Equivariant Physics Constrained 3D Diffusion with Raw Data Consistency for CBCT to CT Image Synthesis","abstract":"Cone beam computed tomography (CBCT) is widely used for image guided and adaptive radiotherapy, but scatter, noise, reconstruction artifacts, and inaccurate Hounsfield units limit its quantitative use. Recent diffusion approaches for CBCT to CT synthesis remain largely image driven, with limited use of the underlying acquisition physics. We propose EPC-RawDC, a conditional 3D latent diffusion framework that combines volumetric generative modeling with two complementary constraints derived from the same scanner specific CBCT forward model. First, geometry equivariant projection constraint exploits the rotational equivariance of the CBCT forward operator by supervising the synthesized CT through correspondingly transformed reference projections. Second, raw data consistency uses noisy projection measurements of the individual acquisition as scan specific supervision during training. The framework was evaluated on two independent clinical cohorts with distinct scanner geometries and projection sources. For JUST, the physics constraints used measured clinical raw CBCT projections acquired with an Elekta system, whereas for SynthRAD2025 the projection data were simulated from the reference CT using a Varian geometry with Poisson photon statistics. EPC-RawDC achieved an SSIM of 0.97 in both cohorts and improved PSNR over DDPM by 2.66 dB on SynthRAD2025 and 2.10 dB on JUST, while reducing mean MAE by 20.3% and 19.0%, respectively. On SynthRAD2025, projection consistency and equivariant errors were reduced by 55.0% and 41.4% relative to DDPM. These results demonstrate that combining a volumetric diffusion prior with scanner geometry and scan specific projection measurements improves quantitative CBCT to CT synthesis across both simulated and measured projection domains. Code available after publication at https://github.com/ALZAHRAALTALIB/EPC-RawDC.","authors":["Alzahra Altalib","Chunhui Li","Alessandro Perelli"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:42:56Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:42:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40046v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40046v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40046v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40029v1","title":"Segment Anything Model Based Context Guided Mamba Unet for Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Medical image segmentation is crucial for clinical diagnosis and treatment planning, requiring both global contextual understanding and fine-grained structural precision. While the Segment Anything Model (SAM) demonstrates strong generalization capabilities, its direct application to medical images often lacks domain-specific adaptability. Meanwhile, State Space Models (SSMs)-based methods, such as Mamba, efficiently capture long-range dependencies but may struggle with preserving fine anatomical details. To address these challenges, we propose a segment anything model based context guided mamba unet, SAMBA-UNet, a novel framework that integrates SAM's context knowledge with Mamba's hierarchical feature learning to enhance both global representation and local detail refinement. Specifically, we introduce the Hierarchical Context Enhancement Module (HCEM), an attention-based fusion module that deeply incorporates the multi-level features of SAM and Mamba to improve detail perception and segmentation precision. Additionally, we employ LoRA and Adapter-based fine-tuning to adapt SAM to medical imaging tasks, further enhancing its domain-specific segmentation capability and robustness. We conducted a comprehensive study on several medical segmentation datasets, including Synapse, ISIC17, and ISIC18, evaluating our method across multiple metrics. Experimental results demonstrate that SAMBA-UNet achieves state-of-the-art performance, effectively balancing generalization, segmentation accuracy, and computational efficiency. Our code will be released upon acceptance.","authors":["Chenchen Wang","You Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:36:01Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:36:01Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40029v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40029v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40029v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.40007v1","title":"Greenscapes: Optimized Vegetation Configurations in Cities","abstract":"Vegetation plays a central role in cities, serving functional roles such as balancing shade and sky exposure, shaping the microclimate, carbon sequestration, and providing acoustic screening. Furthermore, aesthetic choices in landscape styles (formal, modernist, picturesque) are important for citizens' well-being and should be considered in the design of urban landscapes. In this paper, we present a novel method for placing vegetation, ranging from shrubs to large trees, in 3D urban scenes. Our method uses individual lots as input and employs a conditioned Vision-Language Model (VLM) to generate a top-down rendering of vegetation, placing large trees, small trees, and shrubs on lawn areas. This rendering is then converted into a 3D scene by detecting individual plants and selecting appropriate 3D assets. To refine the proposed configurations, we define an optimization loop that keeps the VLM-generated positions fixed after a deterministic feasibility repair and uses CMA-ES to optimize per-instance scale transformations along with discrete vegetation asset substitutions. To define the objective function for the optimization, we evaluate the generated 3D scenes with efficient surrogate models of shade and path-based acoustic exposure. Furthermore, we render in-scene views at each optimization step to compute view-dependent perceptual descriptors, enabling the generation of both aesthetic and functional vegetation layouts for urban scenes.","authors":["Patryk Bartkowiak","Marcin Marlewski","Paweł Wójcik","Jae Joong Lee","Dominik L. Michels","Bedrich Benes","Sören Pirk","Wojciech Pałubicki"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:24:24Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:24:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40007v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40007v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40007v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39972v1","title":"Blind Video Denoising for Vocal Tract rtMRI using a Self-Supervised Ensemble","abstract":"Real-time magnetic resonance imaging of the vocal tract makes speech articulation directly observable, but the trade-off between temporal resolution and signal quality introduces substantial noise and obscures fine articulatory detail. Since clean references cannot be acquired for a moving vocal tract, supervised denoising approaches are not applicable in this setting. We propose a self-supervised denoising framework that combines spatiotemporal super-resolution learning with pseudo-supervision derived from a variance-stabilized non-local means filter. A dual-model fusion strategy balances noise suppression and preservation of high-frequency articulatory structure. Experiments on the USC-75 multi-speaker dataset under natural noise conditions show that the ensemble raises the signal-to-noise ratio by 11.9 dB on unseen subjects while attaining more high-frequency articulatory content in the frames. Among tuned implementations of anisotropic diffusion, block-matching filtering, non-local means and pre-smoothed non-local means, every configuration that achieves comparable noise suppression does so at the cost of high-frequency content or of structural fidelity. Under severe added Gaussian, Rayleigh and Rician noise, peak signal-to-noise ratio improves by 7.6 to 11.8 dB and structural similarity roughly triples. An ablation shows that the variance-stabilized pseudo-supervision is an essential element for denoising, and that the effect is attributable to the hard thresholding contained in the stabilization transform.","authors":["Julian Andres Hernandez-Potes","Tomás Arias-Vergara","Daiqi Liu","Andreas Maier","Paula Andrea Perez-Toro"],"published":"2026-10-29T13:04:02Z","updated":"2026-10-29T13:04:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39972v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39972v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39972v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39955v1","title":"Mind The Gap (MTG-AD): Dataset and Fine-grained Evaluation for End-to-End Inline Audio Descriptions","abstract":"Audio descriptions (AD) are essential for making visual media accessible to blind and low-vision (BLV) users. While Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) offer a scalable solution for generating audio descriptions on-demand, their performance on ``inline'' audio descriptions -- which must fit within existing silences in a video -- remains under-explored, particularly for diverse user-generated content (UGC). We present a comprehensive investigation into MLLM-generated inline audio descriptions. First, we introduce MTG-AD, a professionally annotated dataset of 36 hours of videos, each averaging $\\sim$5 mins. and 8k+ descriptions in total, co-created by paired blind and sighted AD professionals to provide a high-quality benchmark for this task. Second, we propose MTG-AD-Eval, an evaluation framework based on audio description expert guidelines that provides fine-grained, reference-free actionable metrics for model improvement. Crucially, our framework evaluates the end-to-end task: it renders the generated scripts into audio tracks to assess their fit within the original video's natural silences, minimizing disruptive overlaps. Our analysis across frontier MLLMs reveals that while models accurately describe visual events and substantially reduce speech overlaps, gaps persist in narrative pacing and flow. Finally, we empirically validate that our automated and reference-free metrics correlate significantly with human quality ratings from sighted raters and 7 BLV participants, finding that while users narrowly prefer human-authored audio descriptions, they find MLLM-generated audio descriptions highly beneficial for comprehension. We provide actionable system design guidance and release MTG-AD at https://hf.co/datasets/google/mind-the-gap-inline-ad and MTG-AD-Eval at https://github.com/google/mtg-ad.","authors":["Subhashini Venugopalan","Jenny Tan","Taylor Roper","Jimmy Tobin","Anton Kast","Alicia Martin","Sam Sepah","Amy Pavel"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:51:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:51:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39955v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39955v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39955v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39948v1","title":"UniRA: Unified Representation Alignment and Auto-Encoding for Efficient Diffusion Generation","abstract":"Diffusion transformers (DiTs) have become the dominant architecture for high-fidelity image synthesis, yet they suffer from slow training convergence and high computational cost. Two recent lines of work address this from complementary angles: Representation Alignment (REPA) accelerates DiT training by aligning intermediate features with a pretrained external representation model (e.g., DINOv2), while Representation Auto-Encoders (RAE) replace the pixel-space VAE with a compact representation-space tokenizer learned from self-supervised features. However, these approaches are developed and applied in isolation: REPA still relies on a pixel-space VAE for latent diffusion, and RAE does not exploit the alignment signal that accelerates the diffusion backbone. We present \\textbf{UniRA}, a unified framework that couples representation alignment and representation auto-encoding into a single objective. UniRA learns a representation-space tokenizer whose output serves simultaneously as the diffusion target and the alignment anchor, so the tokenizer, the diffusion backbone, and the external representation model are co-optimized. We give a theoretical result showing that this joint optimization preserves the representation quality of the external model while accelerating diffusion convergence, and we derive a principled weighting that balances the reconstruction, alignment, and generation objectives. On ImageNet class-conditional generation at 256*256 and 512*512, UniRA matches or surpasses state-of-the-art DiTs (SD3, REPA, RAE) in FID while reducing training steps by up to 5*. We further show that the unified representation space supports improved zero-shot classification and linear probing, confirming that generation and understanding benefit from a shared, aligned latent space.","authors":["An Lanji","Dawei Liu","Jin Li","Haoran Xu","Mei Chen","Yu Tian"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:46:29Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:46:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39948v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39948v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39948v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39936v1","title":"MLLM-GS: Multimodal Large Language Model Prior-Guided Fast Novel View Synthesis in Extreme Environments","abstract":"Novel view synthesis (NVS) in degraded scenes aims to reconstruct 3D scenes from multi-view observations affected by various degradations, and to generate clear and photorealistic images at unseen viewpoints. Despite recent advances, existing methods often rely on degradation-specific models, limiting their generalization across diverse extreme environments. Generative models provide rich visual priors, but their outputs often suffer from pixel misalignment and color deviations, which can propagate into the reconstructed 3D scene and compromise reconstruction quality. Furthermore, the high computational and memory costs of 3D reconstruction hinder the practical application of NVS in extreme environments. To this end, we propose MLLM-GS, a multimodal large language model (MLLM) prior-guided framework for fast NVS in extreme environments. MLLM-GS consists of two branches: MLLM prior-guided view enhancement and fast 3D scene reconstruction. The former leverages the rich visual priors of a frozen MLLM to enhance views under different severe degradations and introduces a lightweight correction network to reduce pixel shifts and color distortions in MLLM outputs. With supervision from only a few paired clean views, this branch produces reliable enhanced views for all degraded observations, providing pseudo-GT views for subsequent 3D reconstruction. The latter improves 3DGS efficiency by optimizing rasterization, Gaussian memory layout, and parameter updates, reducing redundant computation, memory-access overhead, and optimization costs. Extensive experiments on multiple degraded scenes demonstrate that MLLM-GS effectively handles various severe degradations and achieves high-quality NVS with significantly lower reconstruction time and memory consumption. Project Page: https://wzlwlm13.github.io/MLLM-GS","authors":["Zhiliang Wu","Xinye Zheng","Kun Li","Fei Wang","Hao Guo","Yanyan Wei","Guanghui Yue","Beier Zhu","Wei Zhou","Xun Yang"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:36:54Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:36:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39936v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39936v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39936v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39924v1","title":"PhysAg3D: Bridging 3D Generative and Agentic Models for High-Fidelity and Physically-Interactive Assets","abstract":"Generating interactive 3D assets from a single image requires realistic appearance, fidelity to the depicted object, and plausible physical behavior. Yet detailed geometry alone does not determine how an object should move or deform. We introduce PhysAg3D, which couples 3D generative models with agentic reasoning and tool use. A vision-language agent first infers semantic parts, candidate interactions, and physical priors from the image; a 3D generator then produces a textured whole-object mesh from the same image. A hierarchical interaction representation links these revisable priors to mechanisms and the generated surfaces they control, organizing interaction construction and refinement. Guided by this representation and observations of the mesh, the agent uses geometric and physical tools to construct parts, joints, deformable regions, and surface bindings while retaining useful generated geometry and materials. Rendered interactions and execution measurements guide targeted revisions within a fixed budget. The framework accommodates both rigid articulation and compliant deformation. Quantitative and qualitative evaluations on real photographs and external benchmarks demonstrate detailed, image-aligned assets with plausible interactions. Ablations support bounded refinement and source-surface preservation. Project Page: supra-3d.github.io/PhysAg3D.","authors":["Bi'an Du","Fanpeng Meng","Jiemin Fang","Chen Yang","Pufan Li","Daizong Liu","Qi Tian","Wei Hu"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:29:27Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:29:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39924v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39924v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39924v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39913v1","title":"TokenRepel: Generating Diverse Image Sets Without Compromising Quality","abstract":"We present an inference-time approach that enhances the visual diversity of image sets generated by text-to-image models, without compromising the average image quality. Our method leverages the intrinsic geometry of hidden representations in pretrained models to diversify images generated from the same prompt. First, we introduce a token repulsion mechanism that increases the angle between feature tokens across samples. It takes a negative Riemannian gradient step on a von Mises-Fisher mixture distribution of tokens on the unit hypersphere. Second, to preserve the quality of each output image, we propose a token density preservation mechanism that projects the repulsion-induced prediction change onto a half-space aligned with the model's score direction. This encourages the updates to remain within the high-density regions learned by the generative model. We further extend this mechanism to the output of each intermediate layer, enabling finer-grained iterative guidance. We evaluate our method on three types of pretrained text-to-image models. Experiments show substantial gains in visual diversity under identical prompts, while maintaining a comparable level of image fidelity.","authors":["Qingtao Yu","Zhen Wang","Ning Lai","Boyang Hu","Hongdong Li","Dylan Campbell"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:20:24Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:20:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39913v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39913v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39913v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39889v1","title":"sDMD: Sinkhorn Distribution Matching Distillation","abstract":"Distribution Matching Distillation (DMD) distills multi-step diffusion models into few-step generators by minimizing reverse KL divergence. Despite its strong performance, DMD guides samples by local density alone, which introduces two limitations: teacher scores can be unreliable in regions of low teacher density, and the mode-seeking behavior of reverse KL can collapse the student onto a subset of the teacher modes. We first connect DMD to Wasserstein gradient flow, showing that its gradient follows from a fixed-point formulation with integral KL (IKL) as the energy functional. This perspective motivates augmenting IKL with an optimal transport term that addresses these limitations by guiding outlier samples toward the target and promoting mode coverage. We realize this term using Sinkhorn divergence estimated from finite batches. In practice, however, errors in the estimated Sinkhorn velocity can perturb in-domain samples for which IKL guidance is already accurate. To this end, we introduce sDMD, which adaptively activates the Sinkhorn correction based on discriminator outputs, focusing on outlier samples where IKL may be unreliable. With 2--4 sampling steps on FLUX.1-dev, Qwen-Image-20B, and Wan2.1-14B, sDMD outperforms distillation baselines on GenEval, DPG, WISE, and VBench while improving sample diversity over DMD2.","authors":["Jaihoon Kim","Lingxiao Li","Soshi Shimada","Roshni Cooper","Minhyuk Sung","Yiwei Zhao"],"published":"2026-10-29T12:04:23Z","updated":"2026-10-29T12:04:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39889v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39889v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39889v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39880v1","title":"What Survives Label Downsampling? Exact Region Survival for Coarse Segmentation Targets","abstract":"Segmentation networks that predict at an output stride are trained against label maps downsampled by strided sampling, nearest-neighbor resampling or majority pooling. These methods label each coarse grid cell on its own, so a region that wins no cell is absent from the target and receives no positive label. We present MatchDown, which builds the coarse target from a minimum-cost matching between regions and the grid cells they occupy. It keeps as many regions as any one-region-per-cell target can, certifies how many losses are unavoidable, and with priority tiers (MatchDown-P) keeps every evaluated region (a region that a metric scores) of at least one cell's area. On COCO, Cityscapes and BBBC038 at factors 2 to 16, MatchDown-P loses exactly the minimum number of regions, whereas the downsampling used by StarDist, nnU-Net and connectomics pyramids loses four to five times as many on COCO at factor 8. Keeping a region in the target and reconstructing its shape are different problems: we show that no region lost by majority pooling can be reconstructed above IoU 0.5 by upsampling any coarse target, yet on BBBC038 a star-convex head trained on MatchDown targets segments 35% of the nuclei that majority pooling loses above IoU 0.5, against 15% when trained on majority targets and 24% on StarDist's strided target. On two nucleus datasets, with thresholds cross-validated for count error, this head has instance accuracy at IoU 0.5 (Acc50) 2.6 to 4.2 points above the strided target and at most 2.8 points below cell-center sampling, and a lower count error than the strided and cell-center targets on BBBC039. On BBBC039, an affinity head, which outputs one label per cell, lowers count error relative to majority targets by 9.3 nuclei per image at 7.2 points lower Acc50. MatchDown runs once, before training, in place of the label-downsampling step.","authors":["Zhiyuan Zhang","Yukun Ou","San Tek Choi"],"published":"2026-10-29T11:57:44Z","updated":"2026-10-29T11:57:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39880v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39880v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39880v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39848v1","title":"LiDAR-SHIELD: A Fully Dockerized Adversarial Robustness Assessment Framework for LiDAR-Based 3D Perception Models","abstract":"LiDAR-based 3D perception underpins autonomous driving and robotics, yet deploying these models safely requires stress-testing them against adversarial inputs before real-world use. Although state-of-the-art adversarial attacks exist, their implementations are often fragmented across individual repositories, each tied to a specific architecture or task and requiring manual environment reconstruction and model-specific integration code. We present LIDARSHIELD, a unified and fully Dockerized framework that reverses this workflow: rather than installing the target model inside the toolkit, LIDARSHIELD exposes any model through three lightweight HTTP endpoints, namely /info, /forward, and /gradient As a result, connecting a new model or dataset requires only a single loader function or adapter. The framework supports both 3D semantic segmentation and 3D object detection through the same engine-service protocol, integrating a task-aware suite of state-of-the-art attacks, including PGD and Square Attack for segmentation, and point perturbation, point removal, point injection, and LiDAttack for detection. This enables systematic exposure of corner cases and vulnerabilities across heterogeneous LiDAR backbones. The LiDAR-Shield codebase is publicly available at https://github.com/alexandrosgk/LiDAR-shield.","authors":["Alexandros Gkillas","Ioulia Kapsali","Christos Anagnostopoulos","Aris S. Lalos"],"published":"2026-10-29T11:27:53Z","updated":"2026-10-29T11:27:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39848v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39848v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39848v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39835v1","title":"Aligning Medical Vision Language Models at the Capability Boundary with Entropy Adaptive Rubric Grounding","abstract":"Medical diagnostic reasoning requires verifiable links between visual evidence and clinical knowledge, yet current medical vision language models are aligned with objectives that reward the wrong behavior. Uniform supervised fine tuning lets easy cases dominate updates while clinically critical boundary cases saturate prematurely. Sparse rewards based only on final-answer correctness cannot assess anatomical grounding or clinical logic, inviting reward hacking through answer shortcuts. We propose MedCBR, a two-stage alignment framework coupling boundary-aware preference optimization with rubric-grounded reinforcement learning. First, we construct counterfactual boundary preferences at the model's capability frontier using empirical pass rates and preference pairs that share reasoning prefixes but diverge at decisive diagnostic branches. Difficulty Weighted Boundary Preference Optimization (DBPO) concentrates learning on this calibrated boundary region. Second, we distill reusable clinical rubrics from boundary trajectories and activate them according to prediction entropy. Entropy Gated Grounded Policy Optimization (EGPO) rewards answer correctness, anatomical grounding, and reasoning fidelity while suppressing rubric noise on reliable samples. A reference policy anchored to the boundary-aligned model couples population-level boundary priors with instance-level uncertainty, making diagnostic reasoning more accurate and clinically grounded. Experiments on six medical benchmarks show consistent improvements over strong general, specialized, and reinforcement learning baselines, reaching 61.2% average accuracy with the largest gains on challenging differential diagnosis tasks. Ablations show super-additive gains: combining the two stages exceeds the sum of their individual improvements.","authors":["Hongyu Bai","Jiaqi Zeng","Chunzheng Zhu"],"published":"2026-10-29T11:15:12Z","updated":"2026-10-29T11:15:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39835v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39835v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39835v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39812v1","title":"GROME: GROunding Factorized MEmory into Video World Models","abstract":"Long-horizon consistency is essential for video world models, yet it becomes difficult to maintain once observed content falls outside the context window. Existing memory mechanisms either retrieve history as implicit context, which remains constrained by context capacity and entangles static and dynamic information or consolidate history into explicit global representations, which struggle to model evolving dynamics and accumulate geometric errors. To overcome these limitations, we introduce \\textbf{GROME}, a novel factorized memory framework that augments video world models for long-horizon coherence across both static scenes and dynamic entities. GROME operates via three synergistic components: 1)~\\emph{Static-Dynamic Factorization} decouples historical evidence into static and dynamic components, preserving static scene geometry and dynamic entity states separately; 2)~\\emph{Local Memory Retrieval} selects spatially aligned static history to eliminate geometric drift alongside the latest dynamic entity states to ensure temporal continuity; 3)~\\emph{Training-Free Memory Injection} seamlessly grounds the factorized memory into video world models via their native conditioning pathways. Experiments on long-horizon revisit scenarios demonstrate that GROME substantially enhances scene persistence and dynamic entity fidelity over existing methods, all while preserving the visual quality and controllability of the frozen backbone without additional training. Beyond self-rollouts, GROME readily accommodates external observations as plug-and-play memory, unlocking flexible and controllable video continuation and scene exploration.","authors":["Fang Yu","Xiaoxue Wu","Haiyu Zhang","Yangfu Li","Botong Zhao","Yu Qiao","Tianfan Xue","Yue Lu","Xinyuan Chen"],"published":"2026-10-29T10:54:32Z","updated":"2026-10-29T10:54:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39812v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39812v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39812v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39773v1","title":"Quantum-Inspired Convolutional Networks for High-Resolution Pseudo-Label Generation from Multimodal Earth Observation Data","abstract":"Accurate land-cover mapping from multimodal Earth observation (EO) data remains a challenge in regions where high-resolution annotations are scarce or costly to obtain. This work introduces a quantum convolutional encoder-decoder network for pseudo-label generation using Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery, guided only by coarse MODIS-derived land-cover maps. The proposed framework uses quantum-inspired convolutional blocks to enhance feature extraction and cross-sensor fusion, enabling the model to infer a fine-scale semantic structure from heterogeneous inputs. Experiments were conducted on the DFC2020 dataset, which provides a quadruple of Sentinel-1 SAR, Sentinel-2 multispectral, low-resolution MODIS labels and high-resolution reference land-cover maps. The proposed training framework first learns a robust representation of multimodal inputs through the reconstruction branch before any supervision from low-resolution labels is applied. This self-supervised stage enables the model to capture a fine-scale spectral-spatial structure, which is then used to generate high-resolution pseudo-labels. These pseudo-labels are subsequently evaluated through classification metrics on the test data, demonstrating that the proposed approach produces more spatially coherent and class-consistent compared to classical baselines. The results highlight the potential of quantum-inspired deep learning to improve label generation in low-annotation remote sensing scenarios, offering a scalable path towards high-quality land-cover products for EO applications.","authors":["Abhishek Singh","Giovanni Cuozzo","Alexander Jacob","Claudia Notarnicola"],"published":"2026-10-29T10:23:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T10:23:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39773v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39773v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39773v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39727v1","title":"Cross-Encoder Anchor Refinement for Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection","abstract":"Zero-shot anomaly detection aims to transfer anomaly discrimination from auxiliary training domains to unseen categories while localizing subtle defects. A key challenge is to construct discriminative references that combine contextual representations with complementary local evidence. We present Cross-Encoder Anchor Refinement (CEAR), which connects two visual encoding paths for the same image at the anchor stage, before normal and anomalous prototypes are constructed. An independent convolutional encoder pretrained for industrial product recognition builds an image-specific memory shared across selected vision transformer (ViT) layers. Spatial anchors read from ViT patch features query this memory through cross-attention and undergo residual refinement. Image-conditioned normal and anomalous queries then gate anchor aggregation to construct prototypes for anomaly scoring. Across 13 industrial and medical datasets, CEAR achieves the best performance in 57 of 68 evaluations spanning image-level detection and pixel-level localization. Notably, CEAR surpasses all other state-of-the-art methods in 41 of 44 pixel-level comparisons. Across 11 localization datasets, mean gains in pixel-level maximum F1 score and average precision are 4.45 and 3.79 percentage points, respectively. This study shows how cross-encoder interaction at the anchor stage can integrate complementary visual representations for fine-grained anomaly localization across domains.","authors":["Kaisong Ji","Ziyang Xu","Chang Bian","Yu Zhou","Wenyu Liu","Xinggang Wang"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:56:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:56:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39727v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39727v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39727v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39721v1","title":"WorldProbe: When Video World Models Fall Apart under Long-Horizon Spatial Exploration","abstract":"Video world models generate future visual observations from camera or action inputs, with the goal of synthesizing persistent 3D worlds that can be explored across time and space. Existing benchmarks typically evaluate short interactions or rely on camera trajectories fixed before generation. The performance of video world models under long-horizon exploration with extensive camera motion remains under-explored. We introduce WorldProbe, a diagnostic benchmark that uses a goal-conditioned multimodal agent to adapt camera actions online during generation. WorldProbe comprises 164 test cases across four probing families. Across 10 state-of-the-art models, we generate more than 1,600 rollouts totaling approximately 800 minutes, with horizons up to 180 seconds. Our evaluation across four dimensions reveals both the degradation of local measures and the emergence of long-range inconsistencies. We find that recent models sustain visual and content quality for longer, while long-range memory and evolution remain weak. These results expose a gap between locally stable generation and persistent world consistency that is largely missed by existing benchmarks. Code and benchmark resources are available at https://github.com/Untitled-W/WorldProbe.","authors":["Minqi Wang","Jie Peng","Hejun Dong","Ruchang Yao","Puyi Wang","Siyuan Huang","Jihai Zhang","Yafu Li","Qi Dou","Xiaoye Qu","Yu Cheng"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:51:26Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:51:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39721v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39721v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39721v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39715v1","title":"NIF: Normal-Intersection Fields for Geometrically Consistent Oriented Box Refinement","abstract":"Accurate oriented detection requires a box to follow object boundaries, yet a standard regression head predicts its center, size, and angle from a pooled region representation. Small errors in these coupled parameters can displace long boundaries even when the object is correctly classified. We propose NIF, a refinement module that replaces an additional box regressor with normal-intersection fields. Given a coarse oriented box, the module observes local features at 20 boundary anchors and predicts signed distances along their outward normals. These observations admit an exact affine representation in five geometric variables. A differentiable, reliability-weighted least-squares fit projects the predicted field onto this representation and analytically recovers a refined rectangle. Tangential pooling preserves the normal position profile, while shared projection limits the added network to 0.132M parameters. On DOTA-v1.0, NIF reaches 78.00%, 79.11%, and 79.02% mAP with Oriented R-CNN, LSKNet-S, and Strip R-CNN, respectively, achieving the highest single-scale SOTA result in Table 1. It reaches 68.19% on DIOR-R, improves matched FAIR1M/DIOR-R baselines by 0.30/0.68%, and raises HRSC mean IoU from 0.867 to 0.888 while reducing field error by 24.5%. Across four different detector frameworks with the detectors frozen, relative AP75 improves by 1.57-4.18% and COCO-style mAP by 0.42-3.74%, without detector retraining. Code is available at https://github.com/zsk466991125-collab/Normal-Intersection-Fields.","authors":["Shaokai Zheng"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:47:19Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:47:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39715v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39715v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39715v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39704v1","title":"Confidence Filtering in Video-Based Gait Assessment: Measurement Error versus Selection Shift","abstract":"Video-based gait assessment often keeps only high-confidence pose observations, which can lower measurement error but change the movement assessed. We compared confidence selection with budget-matched random selection in a Parkinson-related cohort (n = 96) and an NF2-related replication cohort (n = 18). In the Parkinson-related cohort, confidence selection produced a small but consistent shift in the observed movement summary (+0.05 SD, 95% CI 0.03 to 0.07), while held-out Mini-BESTest error stayed flat (-0.04 points, -0.13 to 0.05); a policy preserving motion-group proportions reduced the shift, while NF2 showed no shift and its predictor did not beat a constant baseline. Because this shift mixes measurement error with selection shift, we decompose and simulate both on the cohort's window structure. Filtering lowers local error when error depends on confidence, but biases the endpoint once confidence tracks movement more than error. In the Parkinson-related data, confidence was only weakly tied to movement (rho = 0.14). There, filtering is harmless if well-tracked windows are unbiased. If instead systematic error only redistributes within participants, filtering can add up to 0.58 degrees. Unchanged clinical error from such small-cohort predictors therefore cannot show that quality filtering preserves the assessed movement.","authors":["David Regev","Eran Beeri Bamani"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:37:48Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:37:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39704v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39704v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39704v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39701v1","title":"DEQ-Stereo: Deep Euilibrium Video Stereo Matching via Adaptive Convergence","abstract":"Iterative refinement models have significantly improved stereo matching accuracy, but their rigid, fixed update schedules incur heavy computational costs and a strict trade-off between speed and accuracy. This challenge is magnified in video streams, where traditional online methods resort to reducing feature channels or relying on expensive temporal warping. To address this, we propose \\textbf{DEQ-Stereo}, a novel video stereo matching framework based on the Deep Equilibrium (DEQ) paradigm. Instead of unrolling a fixed number of iterations, DEQ-Stereo reformulates the refinement as an implicit fixed-point problem, adaptively terminating once convergence is met. To ensure fast and stable intra-frame convergence, we introduce the \\textbf{Convolutional Simplified Gated Unit (ConvSGU)}, which eliminates unnecessary non-linearities by modeling the latent state as a gated, element-wise sum of inputs. Furthermore, to exploit cross-frame temporal coherence, we propose \\textbf{Temporal State Transfer (TST)}, a warm-start strategy that propagates the converged hidden state to the next frame without requiring explicit pose or warping priors. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that DEQ-Stereo delivers state-of-the-art performance with a superior accuracy-to-latency trade-off.","authors":["Kiljoon Han","Kyumin Hwang","Wonheok Choi","Jeonghoon Kim","Sunghoom Im"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:36:49Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:36:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39701v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39701v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39701v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39676v1","title":"Event-SAMA: Language-Grounded Video Object Segmentation on Neuromorphic Event Streams","abstract":"Event cameras offer microsecond temporal resolution, high dynamic range and no motion blur, but referring video object segmentation (RVOS) lacks event-stream datasets and models. We introduce Event-SAMA-239K, a synthetic-event conversion of the SAMA-239K grounded video-chat corpus and all additional SAMA training data: 30,650 video clips and 392,162 images. Optical-flow, FILM or saccade upsampling followed by a linear event generator preserves all mask, referring-expression and dialogue annotations. We also introduce Event-SAMA, adapting SAMA-4B with a two-channel polarity patch embedding initialised from pretrained RGB weights and a zero-initialised event adapter. End-to-end training yields 24.37 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ on MeViS valid_u and 22.33 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ on the converted ReVOS valid subset, establishing the first event-native RVOS baseline (RGB SAMA-4B: 55.4 on MeViS). Frozen RGB SAMA-4B with pseudo-RGB renderings or E2VID reconstructions of the same events scores 13.23 and 15.94 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$. Without retraining, Event-SAMA works on real event-camera recordings when the accumulation window matches their event density. A seed-controlled ablation with a measured noise floor of 0.47 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ finds gains from diverse training sources over MeViS alone (+4.99), RGB-mean event-stem initialisation (+4.33), and a trainable SAM2 mask decoder (+2.76); the event adapter, partial ViT unfreezing and event polarity are within noise at this budget. An earlier, partially converted corpus snapshot trained with the identical recipe scores 1.38 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ higher on MeViS and 1.12 higher on ReVOS. Both are single runs that also differ in schedule length, so we draw no conclusion about corpus scale.","authors":["Sergei Fedorchenko","Max Zehnder","Nikola Zubić","Rong Zou","Davide Scaramuzza"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:22:49Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:22:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39676v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39676v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39676v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39664v1","title":"EditAgent: Rethinking Agentic Image Editing","abstract":"Agentic image editors decompose complex requests into steps, but when a step fails, they mostly retry with the same narrow set of tools and a judge that scores each output by eye directly. We argue that such agents are bounded by two spaces: the action space of what the agent can do, and the observation space of what its judge can see. On our expert-annotated benchmark, a general-purpose editor yields no usable result in over 40% of the cases, and no judge is reliable across edit types. We present EditAgent, which expands both spaces. It routes each step to general editors and specialized methods, and attaches to each candidate a verification record. With these records, off-the-shelf VLM judges outperform finetuned reward models. Candidates are generated in parallel and judged in one listwise call: experts prefer this to sequential refinement in 78.5% of decided comparisons, at no more than half the judge calls. EditAgent improves two open-weight editors on three benchmarks and serves as a data engine for training stronger editors.","authors":["Haiyang Xu","Tianyu Wang","Jialu Li","Jiteng Mu","Yuanjun Xiong","Jianming Zhang","Zhuowen Tu","Mingze Xu"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:15:52Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:15:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39664v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39664v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39664v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39663v1","title":"AB-Attention: Accurate Block Attention for Accelerating Video Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Video diffusion transformers generate high-quality videos, but the quadratic cost of 3D full attention dominates their inference. Dynamic block-sparse attention reduces this cost by computing exact attention only on key/value blocks selected from current activations. However, its fidelity degrades under aggressive sparsity: blocks are scored from coarse summaries or formed by costly global clustering, pruned blocks vanish from the softmax, and whole tiles are kept or dropped even when only a few of their keys matter. To address these limitations, we propose Accurate Block Attention (AB-Attention), a training-free method that accurately approximates dense attention at high sparsity. Its Intra-Block Clustering Selector (IBCS) estimates block importance faithfully yet cheaply by clustering queries only within each query block, without permuting tokens. Sampling with Inclusion-Probability Weighting (SIP) then samples blocks in proportion to these estimates and reweights them by inverse inclusion probability, so that one pass estimates dense attention rather than truncating it. Finally, its kernel gathers key/value blocks much smaller than a tile into full tensor-core tiles, refining selection at no extra tensor-core cost. On Wan2.1 and HunyuanVideo, AB-Attention skips 89--92\\% of attention, whereas the baselines compute 1.8--3.6$\\times$ as much, yet it still improves PSNR by up to 4.4~dB over the strongest baseline and achieves up to 2.23$\\times$ end-to-end speedup. Our code is available at https://github.com/InfRec-Cardinal/AB-Attention.","authors":["Bin Xu","Mo Zhang","Zhengfeiyang Zhang","Xinfeng Yang","Yixing Ma","Wei Wang","HeXin Wang","Pengfei Zhou","Wangbo Zhao"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:15:39Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:15:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39663v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39663v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39663v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39648v1","title":"Annotation-Free Representation Learning for Polyp Retrieval in Colonoscopy Videos","abstract":"Colonoscopy is inherently comparative: reliable visual assessment of polyps often depends on complementary views acquired under different viewpoints and imaging conditions. Retrieving such views requires polyp tracklet representations, which recent contrastive methods learn from temporal cues but still build from manually annotated bounding boxes. We propose an annotation-free framework that constructs tracklet views directly from raw colonoscopy videos, enabling temporal self-supervised learning on full-procedure videos, including frames without visible polyps. On REAL-Colon, our method achieves state-of-the-art results on tracklet retrieval and re-identification, outperforming both self-supervised and supervised baselines. We then introduce few-shot polyp retrieval, a task closer to clinical use, where a few observations of a target polyp are used to retrieve its remaining tracklets: with only two examples, our method surpasses most competing methods given ten. Finally, we show that a modest number of unlabeled procedures suffices for competitive performance, making on-site model development more practical. Code is available at https://github.com/lparolari/freerep.","authors":["Luca Parolari","Lamberto Ballan","Carlo Biffi","Loic Le Folgoc"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:05:22Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:05:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39648v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39648v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39648v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39646v1","title":"FlexTry-On: A Time-Efficient Framework for Multi-Scenario Virtual Try-on","abstract":"Virtual try-on has achieved remarkable progress in recent years, yet most existing methods are designed for a specific scenario, such as cloth-to-person or person-to-person try-on, and often require separate conditioning strategies and processing pipelines for different settings. We present FlexTry-On, a unified and time-efficient diffusion-based framework for diverse virtual try-on scenarios, including cloth-to-person, person-to-person, cross-domain, and in-the-wild settings. FlexTry-On adopts a common conditioning formulation, where a text-aware condition extraction strategy converts heterogeneous garment sources into a unified representation, and a dual-conditioning diffusion transformer jointly models garment appearance and person identity. Extensive experiments demonstrate competitive performance on both cloth-to-person and person-to-person benchmarks while maintaining fast inference. Moreover, the same framework can be directly applied to unseen cross-domain scenarios without requiring separate architectures or scenario-specific processing pipelines. These results highlight the potential of unified and efficient virtual try-on frameworks as a practical step toward more flexible and widely applicable virtual try-on systems.","authors":["Phuong Dam","Quyen Nguyen Duc","Son Hoang Minh","Daeyoung Kim"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:03:27Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:03:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39646v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39646v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39646v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39639v1","title":"Where Do Vision Language Models Fail at 3D Reasoning?","abstract":"Vision-language models can get individual judgments right while struggling to recover and consistently use the exact properties of a 3D object. To characterize this gap, we introduce BedBench, a controlled benchmark of thirteen procedurally generated tasks spanning individual judgments and composite answers. Across twelve open-weight and four frontier models, per-color counting exposes a pronounced weakness: humans achieve 93.3% accuracy, compared with 7.2% for the best model. Targeted fine-tuning raises one model's accuracy from 3.0% to 68.6%, but reveals a further difficulty: its answers about the most common colors remain inconsistent with its own per-color counts. Supplying those counts as text improves its judgments, showing that recovering a property does not mean using it consistently. Reasoning, scaling, and added visual cues each improve individual judgments, but none makes exact answers reliable. To locate the difficulty, we probe Qwen3.5-27B: exact per-color counts remain recoverable at the language model's input, yet the model's answers rarely recover them (65.0% for the probe vs. 3.0% for the model). Image-removal controls further show that some apparent successes require no visual evidence. These findings motivate evaluating 3D reasoning by exact correctness, consistent use, and image dependence, rather than accuracy alone. Code and dataset are publicly available: https://github.com/leduckhai/BedBench","authors":["Khai Le-Duc","Nguyen Kim Hai Bui","An Ngo","Nam D. Hoang","Yueqi Song","Jiarui Liu","Thai Quoc Hoang","Fang Wu","Chris Ngo","Terry Jingchen Zhang","Mona T. Diab","Ran Xu","Silvio Savarese","Xiao-Ming Wu"],"published":"2026-10-29T09:00:14Z","updated":"2026-10-29T09:00:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39639v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39639v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39639v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39619v1","title":"FutureWorld: A Plug-and-Play Causal Next-State Predictor for Interactive Video World Exploration","abstract":"Interactive video world models aim to follow user actions while maintaining scene consistency over extended interactions. History-based methods reuse generated latents to preserve scene content, but three limitations of this reuse contribute to error recirculation. 1) The latent generated at each interaction is retained as history, potentially containing blurred details, geometric distortions, or semantic drift. 2) Although retrieved historical content matches the current scene, it can still contain errors that are carried into the next chunk. 3) New outputs then become history for later interactions, potentially reinforcing the same errors through repeated reuse. To limit this cycle, we aim to predict the future scene state from the current generated latent and the next action before generating the next interaction's video chunk. This future-state prior provides guidance for the next chunk, with the aim of reducing the errors carried forward from history. To this end, we propose a plug-and-play Causal Next-Chunk Predictor (C-NCP) with cross-model adaptability and prediction-guided modulation. We integrate C-NCP into four base generators and evaluate the augmented models across three benchmarks for interactive long-video generation and action-conditioned world modeling. Extensive experiments demonstrate reduced error accumulation and improved long-horizon consistency across diverse generation settings. The project page is at https://GuangYuanKK.github.io/futureworld.","authors":["Guangyuan Li","Kelu Yao","Shuolin Xu","Xinlei Yu","Wei Xing","Lei Zhao","Peng-Tao Jiang","Xiaogang Xu","Xiaobin Hu"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:47:55Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:47:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39619v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39619v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39619v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39609v1","title":"DiFuse: Fusing Native Diffusion Representations for Spatial Reasoning in Unified Multimodal Models","abstract":"Unified multimodal models (UMMs) jointly support visual understanding and image generation, yet information flow between these two capabilities remains largely asymmetric: understanding representations routinely condition generation, whereas generative representations are rarely reused for multimodal reasoning. In this work, we investigate whether intermediate representations from a UMM's native diffusion branch can enhance spatial reasoning without compromising general multimodal understanding. We propose DiFuse, which selectively incorporates intermediate diffusion representations into the understanding pathway of UMMs. Through systematic layer-wise analysis, we identify which diffusion representations are most informative and where they can be integrated with minimal interference to semantic processing. Guided by these findings, DiFuse spatially aligns diffusion features with visual tokens and injects them into selected multimodal decoder layers via residual fusion. Extensive experiments on UniWorld-V1 show that, DiFuse consistently improves spatial reasoning over a matched SFT baseline while maintaining broadly comparable performance on general multimodal benchmarks. Further analyses show that these gains depend critically on the choice of diffusion representations, injection positions, and the preservation of spatial correspondence between diffusion features and visual tokens. Results on OmniGen2 further demonstrate the transferability of DiFuse across unified multimodal architectures. Overall, our findings show that native diffusion representations can provide complementary spatial cues for multimodal reasoning when selectively integrated in a structure-preserving manner.","authors":["Ruixin Fan","Weining Wang","Jiahui Sun","Tailai Chen","Jing Liu"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:43:08Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:43:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39609v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39609v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39609v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39602v1","title":"Evaluating Cultural Knowledge Editing in Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) frequently fail to recognize culture-specific concepts, especially from underrepresented regions. Effectively injecting this long-tail knowledge into existing models remains a challenge. In this work, we investigate knowledge editing as a targeted alternative by introducing CulturalBind, a benchmark comprising 3.2K images of 1K concepts from 6 domains, designed to evaluate culture-aware cross-modal knowledge as well as concept-level knowledge editing in VLMs. Pre-edit evaluation reveals that textual and visual knowledge is decoupled: models often demonstrate text-based knowledge of cultural concepts but fail to identify them from images. We then evaluate editing methods in single and sequential scenarios. Although current methods successfully correct individual visual recognition errors, they exhibit low cross-modal portability, failing to integrate edits into the model's broader parametric knowledge. Furthermore, performance degrades rapidly under sequential editing, highlighting the need for comprehensive adaptation approaches that better consolidate multimodal knowledge over time.","authors":["Malvina Nikandrou","Georgios Pantazopoulos","Ioannis Konstas","Alessandro Suglia"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:39:51Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:39:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39602v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39602v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39602v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39592v1","title":"Operator-Aware Posterior Sampling for Multivariate Weather Downscaling","abstract":"Statistical downscaling is the task of reconstructing high-resolution meteorological fields from coarse inputs. A principled approach formulates downscaling as a Bayesian inverse problem, using a diffusion prior over high-resolution fields and incorporating the coarse field only at inference through a likelihood. This decouples the prior from the coarse source, allowing one pretrained model to adapt to different sources. However, two challenges limit this flexibility: (1) when coarse and target fields come from different reanalysis systems, no fixed coarsening operator can capture their relationship; and (2) guidance gradients can differ by orders of magnitude across variables, making a single guidance strength ineffective. This paper addresses both challenges within the guidance loop, leaving the prior unchanged. We learn the forward operator from paired data and normalize the gradient per channel. We show that this normalization corresponds to steepest descent under a block-max norm, allowing a single guidance strength across variables. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/CarloSaccardi/operator-aware-weather-downscaling.","authors":["Carlo Saccardi","Rudolf Saathof","Geethu Joseph","Justin Dauwels"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:31:38Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:31:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39592v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39592v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39592v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39585v1","title":"TactoHand: Learning Geometry-Based Dense Hand-Surface Interaction Fields from Video","abstract":"Understanding how humans interact with the world requires a representation of physical interactions at the hand surface. Tactile sensing captures these interactions, but collecting tactile data at scale requires specialized hardware. We introduce TactoHand, a model that predicts three dense hand-surface interaction fields---contact, proximity, and 3D force---from monocular video. We derive geometry-based contact and proximity labels and motion-derived force labels from eligible training data within a curated collection spanning approximately 50 million source frames. TactoHand uses a temporal transformer to aggregate visual evidence across frames, while three learned queries decode the corresponding hand-surface fields. A differentiable physics layer further constrains force predictions using observed object motion. Across in-domain and out-of-domain benchmarks, TactoHand improves contact and proximity estimation over prior methods. In addition, physics supervision reduces position and rotation errors in motion rollouts, and the predicted contact and proximity fields improve downstream hand-object reconstruction. Together, these results show a scalable, low-cost path to learning hand-surface interaction fields without collecting tactile measurements. Code will be released at https://github.com/zhenqis123/Tactohand.","authors":["Ziheng Xi","Weibo Li","Jiayi Yu","Sipeng Zheng","Wanpeng Zhang","Haiweng Xu","Hao Luo","Chuan Mao","Zongqing Lu"],"published":"2026-10-29T08:25:15Z","updated":"2026-10-29T08:25:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39585v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39585v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39585v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"},{"id":"2610.39553v1","title":"BEACON: Distilled Begin/End-Aware Structured Decoding for Continuous Sign Language Segmentation","abstract":"In segmentation-based continuous sign language recognition, an untrimmed video is first partitioned into lexical units; segmentation is therefore a means to an end---downstream recognition. However, segmenters are usually judged by local boundary detection rather than by the quality of the lexical segments and the recognition they enable. We present BEACON, a begin/end-aware segmenter that combines four-class Outside/Begin/Inside/End (BIOE) labeling, constrained Viterbi decoding over eight legal transitions, and boundary knowledge distilled from a binary teacher into a shared student. Under strict no-cut evaluation with validation-based model selection, BEACON improves segment-F1 by 14.58/14.59 points and boundary-F1 by 2.17/1.22 points, and reduces the downstream word error rate (WER) by 9.10/4.15 points relative to the state-of-the-art in the PHOENIX-14/BSL Corpus. To examine which segmentation metric better reflects downstream recognition, we evaluated five distinct architecture families. Segment-F1 aligns more consistently with lower WER than boundary-F1 in both corpora (Spearman $ρ=-1.0/-0.9$ versus $-0.9/-0.8$). These results support evaluating sign language segmentation in terms of segment quality and downstream recognition utility rather than local boundary accuracy alone.","authors":["Guohou Zhang","Kalin Stefanov"],"published":"2026-10-29T07:55:11Z","updated":"2026-10-29T07:55:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39553v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.39553v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39553v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-30T06:42:52+00:00"}],"tracked_paper_count":75,"storage_mode":"latest_overwrite"}};
